\subsubsection{Stable periodic orbits: high $\elastohydronum$} \label{sec:pinwheeling}

The above-described out-of-plane helical trajectory undergoes a critical transition to a trapped periodic state as $\elastohydronum$ increases further. For example, after an initial transient, the filament follows a tight helical path for $\elastohydronum = 16,000$, but beyond $\elastohydronum=17,000$, the early-time planar translation deviates quickly to a tight, circular path that does not translate. An example of this is shown in \Cref{fig: pinwheeling} for $\elastohydronum=23,000$. We refer to this state as `pinwheeling'.

This pinwheeling behaviour, once exhibited, seems to be a stable attractor. As $\elastohydronum$ increases, the early-time dynamics prior to pinwheeling goes through a range of ballistic trajectories, including planar translation, flapping back and forth and more chaotic-like motion, but these
trajectories soon transition to the stable orbiting motion. These orbits appear to exist up to $\elastohydronum \approx 33,000$. 

During pinwheeling, the shape of the filament appears to be fixed, as shown in \Cref{fig: pinwheeling}c. It is symmetric about the midpoint, and takes the form of a U-shape that is bent out-of-plane near the tips. This bend becomes more pronounced as $\elastohydronum$ increases, resulting in a tighter pinwheeling trajectory, i.e.~motion on a small circular attractor. 


