\subsubsection{Diffusive-like motion: very high $\elastohydronum$} 

The pinwheeling trajectory, however, cannot become tighter indefinitely. Once $\elastohydronum \approx 34,000$, the pinwheeling regime gives way to `flap and tumble' dynamics, as shown in \Cref{fig: high EH dynamics}a. This regime is characterised by periods of coherent, periodic, antisymmetric flapping motion --- where the filament makes reasonable forwards progress --- punctuated by intervals of chaotic tumbling where the swimmer reorients. Viewed over longer timescales, this behaviour appears similar to that of diffusing particles.

For very high values of the elastohydrodynamic number, $\elastohydronum\gtrsim50,000$, simulations show that, after some early-time transient planar motion, the chemically-active filament soon transitions to fully three-dimensional motion. The active forces are sufficiently strong compared to the elastic forces that the filament deforms significantly, resulting in sharp changes in direction and speed. This behaviour looks qualitatively like a random walk, and perhaps even chaotic. An example trajectory is shown in \Cref{fig: high EH dynamics}b.

The progression in behaviour of the chemoelastic filaments can be clearly characterised by considering the midpoint displacement from its initial position over time, as shown in \Cref{fig: high EH dynamics}c. As $\elastohydronum$ increases, the periodic, oscillatory motion at $\elastohydronum=30,000$ --- corresponding to the stable periodic orbiting of \Cref{sec:pinwheeling} --- is lost, with an initially more persistent motion at $\elastohydronum=40,000$ that stalls after some time, beyond which we observe displacement plots more characteristic of diffusion or random walks.


