\subsubsection{Persistent planar states} 

Looking at the early-time evolution from our planar S-shape initial condition, we see that near-planar trajectories that propel in a consistent direction with flapping back-and-forth can be observed prior to the above-mentioned later-time dynamics from around $\elastohydronum\approx30,000$. For values beyond $\elastohydronum\approx40,000$, these initial meandering trajectories persist for a noticeably longer time period. An example trajectory is shown in \Cref{fig: stable flappers}a for $\elastohydronum=47,000$. 

The progressive flapping motion comprises a travelling wave of curvature, that initiates in the middle of the filament, and alternates its direction of propagation towards either end of the filament, as seen in \Cref{fig: stable flappers}b. As such, progress is a rocking back and forth motion along a straight trajectory. 

Looking at the displacement over time for parameters $40,000<\elastohydronum<50,000$, as shown in \Cref{fig: stable flappers}c, we see that there is an intermediate band of elastohydrodynamic numbers in which this near-planar motion continues for long times. For values $42,000\leq\elastohydronum\leq47,000$, this motion persists for at least $t=100$. Additional simulations from a helical perturbation suggest that this near-planar dynamics can be reached from non-planar states, and so may be a stable attractor for some set of parameter values.

Outside of this band, the filament eventually deviates from these trajectories, showing that there are parameter values for which these dynamics are transient states, after which they follow the more diffusive-like motion observed for values of $\elastohydronum$ larger and smaller than this. For $\elastohydronum\gtrsim50,000$, the filaments revert to diffusive behaviour and this persistent planar motion is lost. 

