    \label{fig: trans to helical swimmers}
\end{figure}


Similarly to the symmetric activity profiles, we observe a transition from planar motion to full three-dimensional dynamics at sufficiently high $\elastohydronum$. When this occurs, the planar circling behaviour appears to be metastable, as our initial planar perturbation settles into a fixed hook-shape circling behaviour on relatively short timescales, but a localised growth in torsion leads to out-of-plane motion. An example of the growth in torsion is shown in \Cref{fig:circling}c for $\elastohydronum=12,000$, demonstrating the exponential local growth of torsion. This localised torsion, typically observed between the midpoint of the filament and the most-curved region of the hook (positions highlighted in \Cref{fig:circling}b) for values of $\elastohydronum$ as low as 6000, destabilises the planar motion of the filament, twisting the filament out-of-plane into fully three-dimensional trajectories. However, for many of our simulations, we do not observe this transition because, although the torsion grows exponentially, it can be sufficiently slow that the torsion magnitude is not significant within the timeframes that we explore.


To fully understand which values of the elastohydrodynamic number permit helical trajectories, we simulate the long-time dynamics of our chemoelastic filament when starting from an initial three-dimensional helical perturbation, and fit a helix to the resulting midpoint trajectory.
In contrast to the sudden transition at a critical $\elastohydronum$ for the symmetrically-patterned filaments previously studied, this out-of-plane transition appears gradually as $\elastohydronum$ increases. The resulting pitch of this helix is plotted as a function of $\elastohydronum$ in \Cref{fig: trans to helical swimmers}a, showing that, at lower $\elastohydronum$, there is a negligible pitch to the helix that may be difficult to observe except on extremely long timescales. As $\elastohydronum$ increases, the helix becomes more pronounced, with a larger pitch, allowing clear three-dimensional motion to be observed, with an example shown in \Cref{fig: trans to helical swimmers}b. 

Conversely, from the initial planar S-shape, the resulting helical trajectory only becomes apparent within a dimensionless time $t=100$ once the elastohydrodynamic number surpasses $\elastohydronum\approx20,000$, although, as can be seen from \Cref{fig: trans to helical swimmers}a, low-pitch helical paths exist for $\elastohydronum$ much lower than this.
A typical example of this helical motion from the planar initial configuration is shown in \Cref{fig:helical_spinning}.
The motion initially remains planar for some time before transitioning to a helical path. At larger $\elastohydronum$, the out-of-plane motion occurs sooner and the resulting helix pitch increases. During the helical motion, the base state of the filament is a hook shape with a localised twist out-of-plane. Around this, there is evidence of a travelling wave from the centre towards the tip from the heatmaps of curvature and torsion (\Cref{fig:helical_spinning}).



