\subsubsection{Run-and-tumble and re-straightening: high $\elastohydronum$} 


When $\elastohydronum$ moves beyond approximately $40,000$, the helical dynamics are lost; the filament becomes more flexible leading to more chaotic-looking paths. Examples of these trajectories are given in \Cref{fig:beyond_helix}a,b. The resulting trajectories are reminiscent of a persistent random walk, or perhaps the run-and-tumble dynamics of bacteria, with intervals of relatively persistent trajectories with some lateral oscillations due to filament flapping, connected by sharp turns. 


