\subsection{Generalities}

Here, we investigate the map 
\begin{equation}
F_c \colon \Sp_c(\Cc) \to \Sp_c(\Dc)
\end{equation}
that is induced by a symmetric monoidal left adjoint $F \colon \Cc \to \Dc$, where $\Cc$ and $\Dc$ and presentably symmetric monoidal $\infty$-categories. We are particularly interested in characterizing when $F_c(E)$ admits a particularly simple description in the sense that the objects of $\Dc$ in the representation of $F_c(E)$ as a symmetric spectrum (see e.g. \cite[\S 1]{annala-iwasa:MotSp}) are obtained from those of $E$ by applying the functor $F$. 

The reason for our interest is that many cohomology theories are the result of applying such an $F$, for example a sheafified left Kan extension, to an already existing cohomology theory. Our results promote such statements into statements about spectra, which is useful for extending cohomology operations among other things.

In our investigations of spectra, we will mostly use the notation of \cite[\S 1]{annala-iwasa:MotSp}, except that we will denote the $\infty$-category of \emph{symmetric sequences} by 
\begin{equation}
\SSeq(\Cc) :=  \Fun(\Fin^\simeq, \Cc)
\end{equation}
instead of $\Cc^\Sigma$. Recall that $\SSeq(\Cc)$ has a symmetric monoidal structure given by the \emph{Day convolution}, where the groupoid of finite sets $\Fin^\simeq$ is equipped with its cocartesian symmetric monoidal structure.

\begin{lem}
Let $F \colon \Cc \to \Dc$ be a symmetric monoidal map in $\Pr^L$. Then, the composition with $F$ induces a symmetric monoidal map $F_* \colon \SSeq(\Cc) \to \SSeq(\Dc)$ in $\Pr^L$. 
\end{lem}
\begin{proof}
\textcolor{red}{Indeed, $\SSeq(\Cc) \simeq \SSeq(\Ani) \otimes \Cc$ in $\CAlg(\Pr^L)$ from which the claim follows immediately.}
\end{proof}

Recall that if $c \in \Cc$ then the category of \emph{lax $c$-spectra in $\Cc$}, $\Sp_c^\lax(\Cc)$, is by definition the category $\Mod_{S_c}(\SSeq (\Cc))$ of modules of the free commutative algebra $S_c$ that is generated by the symmetric sequence obtained by placing $c$ in degree 1 and the initial object in the other degrees. Because $F_*$ is symmetric monoidal and preserves initial objects, it canonically enhances into a functor between module categories
\begin{equation}
F_c^\lax \colon \Sp_c^\lax(\Cc) \to \Sp_c^\lax(\Dc),
\end{equation}
where we have denoted by $c \in \Dc$ the image of $c$ under $F$. 

Unfortunately, $F_c^\lax$ might not preserve the full subcategory of $c$-spectra, as the condition of being a $c$-spectrum involves exponential objects and right adjoints \cite[Definition~1.3.8]{annala-iwasa:MotSp}. Thus, in general, the functor $F_c$ is the composition
\[
F_c = \Sp_c(\Cc) \xto{F_c^\lax} \Sp_c^\lax(\Dc) \xto{L_\Sp} \Sp_c(\Dc),
\]
where $L_\Sp$ is the \emph{spectrification} functor. Nonetheless, there are interesting cases where $F^\lax_c(E)$ is a $c$-spectrum. For this reason, we record the following tautological observation. 

\begin{obs}\label{obs:triv}
Suppose that $E \in \Sp_c(\Cc)$ is such that $F_c^\lax (E) \in \Sp^\lax_c(\Dc)$ is contained in the full subcategory of $c$-spectra. Then 
\[
F_c(E) = F^\lax_c(E),
\] 
i.e., the underlying symmetric sequence of $F_c(E)$ is obtained by applying $F$ objectwise.
\end{obs}


\subsection{Applications to variants of motivic spectra}

Here, we apply Observation~\ref{obs:triv} to variants of motivic spectra. Recall that if $\Cc \to \Dc$ is a map of symmetric monoidal $\infty$-categories, then it induces a map
\[
\Pc(\Cc) \to \Pc(\Dc)
\]
in $\CAlg(\Pr^L)$ \cite[Proposition~4.8.1.10]{HA}. If we combine this with stabilization and symmetric monoidal localizations (see e.g. \cite[Appendix~A]{nikolaus-scholze}), we obtain numerous useful maps in $\CAlg(\Pr^L_\st)$.

\begin{ex}\label{ex:LocalLKE}
Two specific examples are relevant for the main text.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Let $k$ be a field, and consider the inclusion $\Sm_k \to \Sch^{\fp}_k$ of smooth $k$-schemes to finitely presented ones. Then we have a symmetric monoidal left adjoint
\begin{equation*}
F_{\procdh} \colon \Pc_{\Nis, \sbe}(\Sm_k; \Sp) \to \Pc_{\procdh, \sbe}(\Sch^\fp_k; \Sp)
\end{equation*}
which is obtained by procdh locally left Kan extending a presheaf and enforcing smooth blowup excision.  Motivic cohomology of equicharacteristic schemes is procdh-locally left Kan extended from smooth varieties \cite[Corollary~1.11]{kelly-saito}, and satisfies smooth blowup excision \cite[Theorem~5.24(3)]{elmanto-morrow}, we see that
\[
F_{\procdh}(H\Zb^{\Ab^1}(n)) = H\Zb(n)
\]
of sbe-local procdh sheaves of spectra on $\Sch^\fp_k$.

\item Let $k$ be a field, and consider the inclusion $\Sm_k \to \dSch^{\afp}_k$ of smooth $k$-schemes into almost finitely presented derived $k$-schemes. Then we have a symmetric monoidal left adjoint
\[
F_? \colon \Pc_{\Nis, \sbe}(\Sm_k; \Sp) \to \Pc_{??}(\dSch^\afp_k; \Sp)
\]
and $F_?(H_\syn) = H_\syn$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{ex}










Let us recall how to construct a motivic spectrum that represents a cohomology theory \cite[Construction~6.5]{AHI:atiyah}. Observation: a commutative algebra $E$ in $\Sp_c^\lax(\Cc)$ is equivalent to an commutative algebra in $\SSeq(\Cc)$ together with a map $\sigma \colon c \to E(\{1\})$. In particular, a commutative algebra object in $E_\bullet \in \Gr_\Nb (\Cc) := \Fun(\Nb^\simeq, \Cc)$ together with a map $\sigma \colon c \to E_1$ can be enhanced into a lax $c$-spectrum. 


To restrict to the algebro-geometric setting, suppose that $\Cc$ is a presentably symmetric monoidal algebra over the category $\Pc := \Pc_{\Zar, \ebe}(\Sm_\Zb; \Sp)$ of Zariski sheaves of spectra on $\Sm_\Zb$ that satisfy elementary blowup excision~\cite[\S 2]{annala-iwasa:MotSp}. Morally, you should think that $\Cc$ is a category of sheaves of spectra on schemes over a basis. For a Zariski sheaf $\Xfr \in \Pc_{\Zar}(\Sm_\Zb)$, we denote the associated object in $\Pc$, and also $\Cc$, by $\Xfr$. Note that this convention differs slightly from that used in earlier work, where a subscript $+$ is added. We denote by $\Tb \in \Pc$ the object associated to the pointed projective line $(\Pb^1, \infty)$. 

Recall the following notions from \cite[Appendix~C]{annala-shin}.
\begin{enumerate}
\item A \emph{pre-orientation} of a commutative algebra $E´ \in \Gr_\Nb(\Cc)$ is a map $c_1 \colon \rm{B} \Gb_m \to E_1$ together with a trivialization of its composition with the quotient map $\pt \to \rm{B}\Gb_m$. In other words, it is a point $c_1 \in E_1(\rm{B}\Gb_m, \pt)$ in the relative cohomology spectrum.

\item A pre-orientation $c_1$ is an \emph{orientation} if $E_\bullet$ satisfies the graded projective bundle formula with respect to $c_1$, i.e., if for every $\Xfr \in \Cc$, and for every $n,m \geq 0$, the map
\[
c_1(\Oc(1))^i \colon \bigoplus_{i = 0}^n E_{m-i}(\Xfr) \to E_m(\Pb^n \otimes \Xfr)
\]
is an equivalence of spectra, where $c_1(\Oc(1)) \in E(\Pb^n)$ is the pullback of $c_1$ along the map $\Oc(1) \colon \Pb^n \to \rm{B} \Gb_m$. 
\end{enumerate}

The point of these conventions is that the symmetric sequence associated to a commutative algebra $E_\bullet \in \Gr_\Nb(\Cc)$ together with an orientation lifts to a $\Tb$-spectrum \cite[Construction~6.5]{AHI:atiyah}.

\begin{prop}\label{prop:SpectrumFromOriented}
Let $E_\bullet \in \Gr_\Nb$ be an oriented commutative algebra object. Then, the lax $\Tb$-spectrum associated to the symmetric sequence $E$ associated to $E_\bullet$ together with $\sigma = c_1(\Oc(1)) \in E_1(\Tb)$ is a $\Tb$-spectrum.
\end{prop}

In particular, in situations like that in Example~\ref{ex:LocalLKE}, the equality of sheaves can be promoted to an equivalence of motivic spectra.

\begin{thm}
Let $F \colon \Cc \to \Dc$ be a map in $\CAlg_\Pc(\Pr^L)$, and let $E_\bullet \in \CAlg(\Gr_\Nb(\Cc))$ be oriented. Suppose that the pre-oriented object $FE_\bullet \in \CAlg_\Pc(\Pr^L)$ is oriented. Then 
\[
F_\Tb(E) = FE, 
\]
where $E \in \Sp_\Tb(\Dc)$ and $FE \in \Sp_\Tb(\Dc)$ are the spectra associated to oriented commutative algebras in Proposition~\ref{prop:SpectrumFromOriented} 
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}
Indeed, by Observation~\ref{obs:triv} and Proposition~\ref{prop:SpectrumFromOriented}, the underlying symmetric sequence of $F_\Tb(E)$  is obtained by applying $F$ objectwise to the underlying symmetric sequence of $E$, which proves the claim.
\end{proof}



