The open-loop response of the model ($u_k=0$) would present the worst-case scenario. In Figure~\ref{fig:comparison_u0_uhalf}, this scenario is compared to the case where partial isolation was in place ($u_k=0.5$). Without any isolation measures, the number of infected individuals would shoot up to nearly 70,000 cases, requiring facilities to accommodate a peak of 1500 hospitalisations, and the resulting number of deaths settling to 350 at the end of the wave. It should also be noted that the peak values of infection/hospitalisation occurs quite swiftly (within 30 days for $u_k=0$), implying a large burden on health centres. 

