\section{Conclusion and Future Work}
\label{sec:Conclusion}
A data-driven MPC scheme has been proposed to better mitigate the impact of a future pandemic, with a case study built using statistical data gathered during the most prominent COVID-19 wave in Mauritius. A nonlinear dynamical system representing the propagation of the virus as well as the population response was parameterised using genetic algorithms. Our approach shows that the hospital occupancy level remained below the threshold level of 200 by adopting isolation levels specified by the MPC, rather than a fixed isolation level with the partial lockdown adopted by the authorities. In turn, the number of casualties is reduced from 240 to 140. Furthermore, we investigated the effect of introducing additional containment levels and show that there is room for the epidemic to be better managed even further albeit with additional administrative burden on the authorities. One avenue for future work will involve addressing the limitation of having a grey-box model at the onset with a Gaussian process model that is updated as new data is gathered.

