\section{Main result summary}\label{sec:summary}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
The main result of this article is summarized as follows: we obtain relations between ionic densities, voltage, and an influx current composed of single cation ($z_1 > 0$). The bounded domain $\Omega$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:fig0}) is of characteristic length-scale
\beq
R = \frac{1}{2}\max\left.\left\{\|\x - \y\| \right| \x,\,\y \in \p\Omega \right\}\,,
\eeq
with an isoperimetric ratio satisfying $|\p\Omega|/|\Omega|^{2/3} \sim O(1)$. The boundary $\p\Omega$ contains two narrow disk channels $\p\Omega_{A_1}$, $\p\Omega_{A_2}$ of radii $A_1,\,A_2 \ll R$, centered in $\x_1,\,\x_2$, which receive and emit ionic fluxes. The mean membrane curvature in $\x \in \p\Omega$ is given by $H(\x)$ and $\alpha$ is the ratio
\beq
\alpha=\frac{R}{\lambda_D},
\eeq
between R and the Debye length $\lambda_D$. With the above domain characteristics, we use the electro-diffusion model to obtain the following results:
