\subsection{Temporal Point Processes} 
\glspl{tpp} provide a principled statistical framework for modeling the generative process of events over continuous time. In this study, we leverage \glspl{tpp} to model the timing of aggressive behavior onsets in autistic youth. A \gls{tpp} is a stochastic process that represents the occurrence of discrete events (in our case, aggressive behavior onsets) over a fixed time window $[0, T]$ \cite{bae2023meta}. A session, or realization, of a \gls{tpp}, is an ordered sequence of aggressive behavior onset times, denoted as $S_i = \{ t_1^{(i)}, t_2^{(i)}, \dots, t_{J_i}^{(i)} \}$, where $J_i$ is the number of onsets in session $i$, and $t_j^{(i)}$ represents the time elapsed since the session's start when the $j$-th aggressive behavior onset occurred. Equivalently, a \gls{tpp} can be formulated as a counting process $N(t)$, representing the cumulative number of aggressive behavior onsets up to time $t$ in a given observation session. Formally, we define this as:
\begin{equation}
    N(t) = \max \{ j : t_j \leq t , t_j \in S \},
\end{equation}
where $N(t)$ tracks the number of onsets within $[0, t]$. A function that  characterizes a \gls{tpp} is the conditional intensity function $\lambda(t | \h_{t})$, which represents the instantaneous rate of aggressive behavior onsets given the history of past onsets up to time $t$ in an observation session $S$: $\h_{t} = \{t_j \in S | t_j < t\}$. We use the notation \( t^- \) to indicate that \( t \) is included in the history \( \h \). For example, \( \h_{t_j^-} = \{t_1, t_2, \ldots, t_j\} \).
We define the conditional intensity function as:
\begin{align}
    \lambda(t | \h_{t_j^-}) &= \lim_{\Delta t \to 0} \frac{\mathbb{E}[N(t + \Delta t) - N(t) | \h_{t_j^-}]}{\Delta t} = \frac{f(t | \h_{t_j^-})}{1-F(t|\h_{t_j^-})},
\end{align}
where $F(t|\h_{t_j^-})$ denotes the conditional \gls{cdf} corresponding to the conditional \gls{pdf} $f(t|\h_{t_j^-})$. Informally, $\lambda^*(t | \h_t) \times \Delta t$ is the average number of aggressive behavior onsets within the time interval $[t,t+\Delta t]$ for an infinitesimally small $\Delta t$ \cite{rasmussen2018lecture}. The conditional cumulative intensity function (\cref{eqn:condcumint}) is the integral of the conditional intensity function and represents the expected number of aggressive behavior onsets within the time window \([t_j, t]\), conditioned on the history of onsets up to \(t_j\), where the last onset occurred at \(t_j\). The cumulative intensity function (\cref{eqn:cum}) is the average number of aggressive behavior onsets that occur within the observation session $[0,t]$.
\noindent\begin{minipage}{.5\linewidth}
\begin{equation}
    \Lambda(t | \h_{t_j^-}) = \int_{t_j^-}^{t} \lambda(s| \h_s) ds \label{eqn:condcumint}
\end{equation}
\end{minipage}%
\begin{minipage}{.5\linewidth}
\begin{equation}
    \Lambda(t) = \int_{0}^{t} \lambda(s| \h_s) ds \label{eqn:cum}
\end{equation}
\end{minipage}
\newline \newline

The conditional \gls{pdf} and conditional \gls{cdf} of an aggressive behavior onset occurring at and before time $t$, respectively, given the past history of aggressive behavior onsets within an observation session, is described by the conditional intensity function:

\noindent\begin{minipage}{.5\linewidth}
\begin{equation}
    f(t|\h_{t_j^-}) = \lambda(t|\h_{t_j^-}) \exp \left( -\int_{t_j^-}^t \lambda(s|\h_s) ds \right) \label{eqn:condpdf}
\end{equation}
\end{minipage}%
\begin{minipage}{.5\linewidth}
\begin{equation}
    F(t|\h_{t_j^-}) = 1 - \exp \left( -\int_{t_j^-}^t \lambda(s|\h_s) ds \right), \label{eqn:condcdf} \\
\end{equation}
\end{minipage}
\newline \newline
The conditional \gls{pdf} and \gls{cdf} transform the conditional intensity function, which is difficult to interpret for downstream decision-making, into a probability or probability density, a standard representation in decision-making frameworks \cite{shachter1992decision}.

Throughout the paper, we assume parametric models for \glspl{tpp}, including the \gls{hpp}, \gls{nhpp} with \gls{pl} intensity, and self-exciting processes (\gls{hwkpp} with differing kernel functions). By defining a parametric conditional intensity function, we derive the conditional \gls{pdf}, \gls{cdf}, and cumulative intensity function. The model parameters of the \gls{tpp} are denoted by $\theta$. We focus on parametric self-exciting processes given the theoretical supposition that mounting distress and changes in physiological arousal can produce aggressive behaviors in autistic youth \cite{lindsay2000antecedent,scarpa2015physiological,cohen2011assessing}. 

To simplify notation, we use $*$ to denote the conditional intensity function, conditional cumulative function, conditional \gls{cdf}, and conditional \gls{pdf} conditioned on the history of aggressive behavior onsets $\h_t$, as $\lambda^*(t)=\lambda(t|\h_t)$, $\Lambda^*(t) = \Lambda(t|\h_t), f^*(t)=f(t|\h_t)$, and $F^*(t)=F(t|\h_t)$ respectively. 


\subsubsection{Data Preprocessing}
\label{subsec:datapreprocessing}
There are three separate underlying \glspl{tpp} in the collected data, corresponding to \gls{sib}, \gls{ed}, and \gls{ato}, i.e., one for each annotated behavioral code (\cref{fig:dataaggregate}). We define these events as aggressive behaviors, either discrete points in time or continuous activity windows. For this paper, we design  \gls{tpp} models wherein \gls{sib}, \gls{ed}, and \gls{ato} are collapsed into a single class.  In this scenario, at each time point, we define an aggressive behavior event occurrence whenever \gls{sib}, \gls{ed}, or \gls{ato} is active, as shown in the first row of \cref{fig:dataaggregate}. The left boundary of a continuous window of aggression marks the onset of an aggressive behavior, while the right boundary denotes its offset. We focus on modeling onsets of aggressive behavior. Accordingly, when there is a continuous window of aggressive behavior, we collapse the continuous window to the left boundary as the aggressive behavior onset event as shown in \cref{fig:datapreprocesscollapse}.

The aggressive behavior onset timestamps were recorded in calendar time. We normalize them by subtracting the observation session's start time from each aggressive behavior onset timestamp within the corresponding observation session (\cref{fig:datapreprocessnormalize}). This ensures that the start of the study, for the realization of the \gls{tpp}, is set to 0 at the beginning of the observation session. Each observation session is independent of other observation sessions. We do not concatenate all observation sessions of a given participant into a single \gls{tpp} realization, as ignoring the unobserved gaps between sessions could introduce significant and uncontrollable biases in model parameter estimation \cite{filimonov2015apparent}. 


\subsubsection{Likelihood Function}
Multiple recorded observation sessions were conducted for multiple participants, leading to a dataset of observed sessions $\mathbb{S}=\{S_i\}_{i=1}^{I}$ for some given observation duration $T=\{T_i\}_{i=1}^{I}$. For a given session $S_i$, the likelihood function is a joint density function of all aggressive behavior onsets observed within the observation window $[0,T_i)$, factorized into conditional densities of each aggressive behavior onset given all the aggressive behavior onsets prior to it \cite{rasmussen2018lecture}:
\begin{align}
    L(\theta | S_i) = f(t_1,\ldots, t_{J_i}| \theta) (1-F(T_i | t_1, \ldots, t_{J_i}) = f(t_1 | \h_0,\theta) f(t_2 | \h_{t_1},\theta) \cdots f(t_{J_i}|\h_{t_{J_i - 1}},\theta) (1-F(T_i|\h_{t_{J_i}},\theta)) \label{eqn:session_likelihood}
\end{align}
where the last term $(1-F(T_i|\h_{t_{J_i}}))$ corresponds to the probability that no aggressive behavior onsets are observed between $[t_{J_i},T_i)$. Using \cref{eqn:condcdf} and \cref{eqn:condpdf}, it is seen that \cref{eqn:session_likelihood} becomes
\begin{align}
    L(S_i | \theta) = \biggr( \prod_{j=1}^{J_i} \lambda_\theta^*(t_j) \biggr) \exp \left( -\int_0^{T_i} \lambda_\theta^*(s) ds \right) && t_0=0.
\end{align}

Then, the joint likelihood function of all observed sessions (and therefore all aggressive behavior onsets), assuming independence between the observation sessions, is 
\begin{align}
    L(\theta \mid \mathbb{S}) = \prod_{i=1}^{I} L(S_i | \theta) \label{eqn:wholelikelihood}
\end{align}


% Thus, by parameterizing the conditional intensity function $\lambda^*(t)$ with suitable functional forms, we can develop expressive models that capture the temporal dependencies underlying aggressive behavior onsets. 

In the following sections, we explore different choices for the conditional intensity function and their implications for aggressive behavior onset analysis.

\subsection{Model Choices}
\label{subsec:modelchoice}

Unlike typical \gls{ml} and \gls{dl} models, we employ several parametric \glspl{tpp} that are capable of modeling complex random sequences of aggressive behavior onsets while remaining interpretable to clinicians. We aim to assess the feasibility of modeling and forecasting aggressive behavior onsets using only the history of previous onsets within an observation session. Since our focus is on event timing rather than additional attributes, we consider unmarked \glspl{tpp}, which rely solely on onset timestamps. Future work will incorporate physiological signals and contextual variables.

\subsubsection{Homogeneous Poisson Process}
The intensity function remains constant over time, meaning it does not change throughout the observation session. Additionally, the \gls{hpp} assumes independence from past aggressive behavior onsets, implying that the timestamps of future aggressive behavior onsets are independent of previous onsets. Therefore, for a \gls{hpp} , the aggressive behavior onsets within an observation session of length $T$ are distributed uniformly at random over the interval $[0, T]$, where the number of aggressive behavior onsets in the session follows a Poisson distribution with rate $\mu$ \cite{gallager1997discrete}. The \gls{hpp} is described by the single parameter, $\mu$, giving the conditional intensity and cumulative conditional intensity functions

\noindent\begin{minipage}{.5\linewidth}
\begin{equation}
    \lambda^*(t) = \mu
\end{equation}
\end{minipage}%
\begin{minipage}{.5\linewidth}
\begin{equation}
    \Lambda^*(t) = \mu t
\end{equation}
\end{minipage}
\newline

\noindent where $\theta= [ \mu ]$, $\theta \geq 0$, and  $\mu$ is the average number of aggressive behavior onsets per unit time. We use the \gls{hpp} model for comparison to formulate the intensity function $\mu$ via \gls{mle}, i.e., $\mu = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{I} \sum_{j=1}^{J_i} t_j^{(i)}}{\sum_{i=1}^{I} J_i}\,\cdot$


\subsubsection{Non-Homogeneous Poisson Process}
The \gls{nhpp} has many parameterizations, but we choose the \gls{pl} characterization to describe conditional intensity and conditional cumulative intensity functions. The conditional intensity function is independent of past aggressive behavior onsets, but it varies over time throughout the observation session. We choose the \gls{pl} parameterization because it is flexible enough to model a conditional intensity function which is either increasing, decreasing, or remaining constant over time.

\noindent\begin{minipage}{.5\linewidth}
\begin{equation}
    \lambda^*(t) = \alpha k t^{k-1} 
\end{equation}
\end{minipage}%
\begin{minipage}{.5\linewidth}
\begin{equation}
    \Lambda^*(t) = \alpha t^{-k}
\end{equation}
\end{minipage}
\newline 

\noindent where    $\theta= [ \alpha, k ]$, $\theta \geq 0$, $\alpha$ is the scale parameter and $k$ is the shape parameter. The parameter $k$ controls the shape of the conditional intensity function: when $k > 1$, the intensity increases over time; when $k < 1$, the intensity decreases; and when $k = 1$, the intensity is constant, which reduces the model to the \gls{hpp}. The parameter $\alpha$ scales the overall conditional intensity function, affecting the steepness of the intensity decay or growth over time. 

\subsubsection{Self-Exciting Point Processes: Hawkes Process}
The \gls{hwkpp} is a self-exciting point process that relaxes the independence assumptions of traditional models mentioned previously by allowing future aggressive behavior onsets to depend on past occurrences within an observation session. This dependence results in a  stochastic process, where both event times and intensity function are evolving random variables conditioned on the history of previous onsets \cite{laub2024hawkes}.

The conditional intensity function of a  \gls{hwkpp} is defined as:
\begin{equation}
    \lambda^*(t) = \colorunderbrace[blue]{\mu}{\text{baseline intensity}} + \colorunderbrace[red]{\sum_{t_j < t} \phi(t - t_j)}{\text{excitation trigger}}, \label{eqn:generalhwk}
\end{equation}
where $\mu$ represents baseline intensity, ensuring events occur even in the absence of prior onsets, and the summation term captures the self-exciting nature of the process. Another way to interpret this is that \(\mu\) models the exogenous onset occurrences, while the triggering kernel \(\phi(t)\) captures endogenous onsets. Each past event $t_j$ contributes to the intensity at time $t$ through the triggering kernel $\phi(t - t_j)$, which is nonnegative and causal, meaning it only influences future events ($\phi(t) \geq 0$ for $t \geq 0$, and $\phi(t) = 0$ for $t < 0$). Typically, $\phi(\cdot)$ is chosen to be a monotonically decreasing function, ensuring the influence of past events diminishes over time.
This formulation models the cascading effect of aggressive behavior, where each onset increases the likelihood of subsequent onsets, with this effect gradually decaying. As a result, the \gls{hwkpp} captures the temporal clustering of aggressive behavior onsets, making it a suitable framework for forecasting onset occurrences based on historical patterns.

We evaluate two kernel functions of a \gls{hwkpp}: the Omori-Law (or \gls{pl}) kernel $\phi(t) = k (c+t)^{-p}$ and the exponential kernel $\phi(t) = \alpha \beta \exp(-\beta t)$. The \gls{hwkexp} is Markovian, enabling the conditional intensity at a given onset to be computed from the previous onset and the current onset without considering past events. It is suitable for processes with rapid self-excitation decay, where the process quickly ``forgets'' past events. The parameter $\beta$ governs the delay density $\beta \exp(-\beta t)$, determining the time between excitations. A larger $\beta$ leads to faster decay of influence from previous onsets and shorter inter-event times between onsets, while a smaller $\beta$ results in slower decay and longer influence of past onsets \cite{hawkes1971point}. The parameter $\alpha$ is known as the infectivity factor, which is the average number of new aggressive behavior onsets triggered by any given onset occurrence in the infinite future horizon \cite{hawkes1971point}.

\noindent\begin{minipage}{.5\linewidth}
\begin{equation}
    \lambda^*(t) = \mu + \alpha \beta \sum_{t_j < t} \exp \left(-\beta (t - t_j) \right) \label{eqn:hwkci} 
\end{equation}
\end{minipage}%
\begin{minipage}{.5\linewidth}
\begin{equation}
    \Lambda^*(t) = \mu t + \alpha \sum_{t_j < t} \big(1 - \exp \left(-\beta (t - t_j) \right) \big) \label{eqn:hwkcci}
\end{equation}
\end{minipage}
\newline 

where $\theta = [\mu,\alpha,\beta]$ and $ \theta \geq 0 $.

\noindent In contrast, the \gls{hwkpl} is used when self-excitation follows a heavy-tailed distribution, making it suitable for processes with long-term triggering effects, such as epidemics or earthquakes. Being non-Markovian, it requires more computation but exhibits scale-free behavior, effectively modeling long-range temporal dependencies and varying event frequencies. The parameter $p$ controls the decay rate of self-excitation, with slower decay allowing past events to influence future occurrences over extended periods.  The time constant $c$ regularizes the behavior of the power law kernel at very short times \cite{filimonov2015apparent}.
% The parameter $c$ is a constant which introduces a offset to the time $t$, which is practical when a process does not exhibit immediate self-excitation at time zero, or when an initial delay before the effects of an onset are realized.

\noindent\begin{minipage}{.5\linewidth}
\begin{equation}
    \lambda^*(t) = \mu + k \sum_{t_j < t} \left(c+t-t_j \right)^{-p} \label{eqn:hwkplci} 
\end{equation}
\end{minipage}%
\begin{minipage}{.5\linewidth}
\begin{equation}
    \Lambda^*(t) = \mu t + \sum_{t_j < t} \frac{k}{p-1} \left( c^{1-p} - (c+t-t_j)^{1-p}\right) \label{eqn:hwkplcci}
\end{equation}
\end{minipage}
\newline

where $\theta = [\mu,k,c,p]$ and $ [\mu,k,c] \geq 0  , p>1$

The Omori-Law kernel can be approximated by a weighted sum of exponential kernels, combining the Markovian computation of the conditional intensity from the exponential kernel with the scale-free behavior of the Omori-Law kernel. We use a \gls{hwk2exp} model to capture multiple temporal patterns, such as high- and low-frequency aggressive behavior onsets, which a single exponential kernel \gls{hwkpp} struggles to represent. By incorporating multiple kernels with different temporal scales, the multi-kernel \gls{hwkpp} models distinct behavior patterns, such as rapid bursts or sporadic aggression, improving onset prediction across timescales \cite{lee2024multi}. Adding more than two exponential kernels yields only marginal gains while increasing model complexity.



\noindent\begin{minipage}{.5\linewidth}
\begin{equation}
    \lambda^*(t) = \mu + \sum_{k=1}^2 \alpha_k \beta_k \sum_{t_j < t} \exp \left(-\beta_k (t - t_j) \right)
\end{equation}
\end{minipage}%
\begin{minipage}{.5\linewidth}
\begin{equation}
    \Lambda^*(t) = \mu t + \sum_{k=1}^2 \alpha_k \sum_{t_j < t} \big(1 - \exp \left(-\beta_k (t - t_j) \right) \big) 
\end{equation}
\end{minipage}
\newline
where $\theta=[\mu,\alpha_1,\beta_1,\alpha_2,\beta_2] $ and $\theta \geq 0$

In our dataset, we observe different intensity functions, as shown in \cref{fig:intensty_fns}. 

We acknowledge that biases inherent to the data collection process, such as ``edge effects'' from finite observation session durations, are unavoidable and may impact model parameter inference. Edge effects in \glspl{tpp} arise when events before the observation window remain unobserved, leading to incomplete event histories. In \glspl{hwkpp}, this can result in underestimating self-excitation parameters and overestimating baseline intensity, as future events depend on past occurrences \cite{filimonov2015apparent}. However, if past events have only short-term influence — in keeping with our hypothesis that aggressive behavior onsets influence proximal events — edge effects have minimal impact on parameter inference because the triggering kernel decays rapidly, making transient periods short. In preliminary analyses, we performed parameter inference using a modified exponentially decaying \gls{hwkpp} \cite{laub2024hawkes}:  
\begin{align}
    \lambda^*(t) = \mu + (\mu_0 - \mu) \beta \exp(-\beta t) + \alpha \beta \sum_{t_j < t} \exp(-\beta (t-t_j) )
\end{align}  
where the conditional intensity function starts at $\mu_0$. Unlike traditional formulations assuming a clear system start at \( t = 0 \), this approach acknowledges that many real-world processes have no well-defined beginning and are only observed from a certain point onward. Here, \( t=0 \) marks the start of the recording of the observation session rather than the process itself. Our preliminary analyses supported the assumption that edge effects do not alter the infectivity factor or time scale; however, the baseline intensity $\mu$ decreases from $0.022$ to $0.018$. This outcome is expected, as edge effects, such as left truncation, are known to upwardly bias baseline intensity. Introducing $\mu_0$ may help compensate for this bias. However, we find that while the additional parameter provides only marginal benefits to the model, it significantly alters the interpretation of the model parameters. For this reason, we opted to not perform further experiments with this formulation or present further results on this \gls{tpp} parameterization.

\subsection{Model Inference}
After preprocessing the data (\secref{subsec:datapreprocessing}), we perform Bayesian inference using \gls{mcmc} to estimate the posterior distribution of the \gls{tpp} model parameters. By Bayes' theorem, the posterior distribution of the model parameters $\theta$ given the observed sessions $S$ is  $\pi(\theta \mid S) \propto L(\theta \mid \mathbb{S}) p(\theta)$,
where $L(\theta \mid \mathbb{S})$ is the likelihood function (\cref{eqn:wholelikelihood}), and $p(\theta)$ is the prior \gls{pdf}. We assign Gamma priors to all \gls{tpp} model parameters, except for the \gls{hwkpl}, as the Gamma distribution serves as a conjugate prior for the Poisson Process and offers flexibility over the positive real line. For the \gls{hwkpl}, we use wide uniform priors. Since direct computation of the posterior is often intractable due to high-dimensional integration or non-analytical integrals required for the partition function, we approximate it using \gls{mcmc}. \gls{mcmc} constructs an infinite dimension Markov chain whose stationary distribution converges to the target posterior: $p(\theta' | \mathbb{S}) = \int_\theta p(\theta' | \theta) p(\theta | \mathbb{S}) d\theta$. The transition function, $p(\theta' | \theta)$ of the Markov Chain is denoted as the transition kernel.  Thus, each sampled state of the Markov chain represents a specific realization of the \gls{tpp} model parameters sampled from the posterior \gls{pdf}.

To generate samples from this Markov chain, we employ the \gls{nuts} \cite{hoffman2014no}, an adaptive variant of \gls{hmc}, as the transition kernel. In \gls{mcmc}, the transition kernel defines the probability of moving from one state to another while ensuring ergodicity and convergence to the posterior. Unlike random-walk-based methods, \gls{nuts} leverages Hamiltonian dynamics and gradients of the log-posterior to explore the posterior efficiently, proposing new states that are both distant from the current state (reducing autocorrelation in the \gls{mcmc} samples) and have high posterior probability (exploring the typical set of the posterior distribution \cite{betancourt2017conceptual}). For more details on the \gls{mcmc} sampling process, we refer readers to \cite{barber2012bayesian,hoffman2014no}. \gls{nuts}'s adaptive approach improves convergence and sampling efficiency in \gls{mcmc}, especially for posteriors with strong correlations, poor geometry, and nonlinear dependencies \cite{phan2019composable}. However, efficient mixing of the sampling chains and accurate posterior distribution sampling must still be validated using \gls{mcmc} diagnostics. 

The  \gls{ess} measures the number of effectively independent samples. The bulk \gls{ess} captures exploration of the high-density region of the posterior, while the tail \gls{ess} assesses sampling efficiency at the 5\% and 95\% quantiles \cite{carpenter2017stan} of the posterior. The \gls{ess} directly impacts the \gls{mcse}, which quantifies the uncertainty in the expectation estimates of the \gls{tpp} model parameters concerning the posterior distribution due to finite sampling: $ \text{MCSE} \approx \frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{\text{\gls{ess}}}} $, where $\sigma$ represents the standard deviation of the posterior samples. Higher \gls{ess} reduces \gls{mcse}, leading to more accurate estimates of the posterior mean of the \gls{tpp} model parameters. Conversely, low \gls{ess} increases \gls{mcse}, indicating strong autocorrelation in the samples. The Gelman-Rubin statistic ($\hat{R}$) compares within-chain and between-chain variances of the \gls{mcmc} sample chains to assess whether the Markov Chain explored the entire posterior distribution and has reached a stationary distribution. Values close to 1 suggest well-mixed chains. We also monitor divergences, which indicate numerical instabilities when the sampler struggles to explore the posterior, typically due to regions of high curvature or poor adaptation. 

