\subsection{Model Validation}
\label{sec:modelvalidation}

A \gls{tpp} model for aggressive behavior onsets should effectively handle irregular inter-onset intervals, flexibly capture complex temporal dependencies among onsets within an observation session, maintain interpretability, and provide probabilistic forecasting of future onsets. Evaluation of these qualities relies on both \gls{gof} and forecasting performance metrics. \gls{gof} tools, such as \gls{qq} plots, raw residual plots, and count distributions, assess how well the \gls{tpp} captures the data-generating process of aggressive behavior onsets \cite{wu2021diagnostics}. Forecasting performance is evaluated using metrics like \gls{psis-loo}, \gls{elpd}, and \gls{mape}, which quantify the model’s ability to provide accurate probabilistic predictions of future onsets \cite{ludke2023add,vehtari2017practical}.

\subsubsection{QQ Plots}

A \gls{qq} plot is a residual analysis tool for comparing the quantiles of two distributions, often used to assess whether a dataset follows a specific theoretical distribution, such as an exponential distribution. The plot visualizes the relationship between observed data and the expected distribution by plotting the quantiles of the observed data against those of the theoretical distribution. If the scatter plot of the observed and theoretical quantiles closely follows a 45-degree line, it indicates strong alignment, suggesting that the observed data closely matches the theoretical distribution. To apply the \gls{qq} plot to \gls{tpp} models that are not \gls{hpp} and may have complex theoretical inter-onset distributions, we utilize the \gls{rtc} theorem \cite{brown1988simple}. The \gls{rtc} theorem asserts that for a \gls{tpp} characterized by a conditional (over the history of aggressive event onset times $t_1, \dots, t_J$) intensity function $\lambda^*(t)$ the transformed times $\tau(t_1), \dots, \tau(t_J)$, using the mapping function
\vspace{-0.5em}
\begin{align} 
\tau(t) = \int_0^t \lambda^*(s) ds = \Lambda^*(t), \label{eqn:rtc} 
\end{align}
will follow a standard Poisson process. In this case, the time differences $\tau(t_{j+1}) - \tau(t_j)$ are \gls{iid} and exponentially distributed with mean = 1 (representing one aggressive behavior onset per transformed time unit). Thus, the empirical distribution of inter-onset times in the observed data corresponds to the transformed times, while the theoretical distribution is an exponential distribution with mean = 1.

\subsubsection{Raw Residual Plots}

The raw residual plot is based on the Doob-Meyer decomposition theorem \cite{andersen2012statistical}, which defines a counting process \( N(t) \) characterized by the conditional intensity function \( \lambda^*(t) \), the raw residual process as
\begin{align}
M(t) = N(t) - \Lambda(t) \label{eqn:rawresidual}
\end{align}
where \( \Lambda(t) = \int_0^t \lambda^*(s) \, ds \) is the cumulative intensity function. 
According to the theorem, the raw residual process \( M(t) \) is a martingale whose expected value is zero at all times \cite{wu2021diagnostics}. Thus, the raw residuals should be centered around 0. To assess model fit, we evaluate the raw residual process at the end of the observation period, \( M(T) \), where \( T \) is the final time of the session. 

\subsubsection{Count Distribution}
If the \gls{tpp} accurately captures the data-generating process, the distribution of event counts per observation session in the generated data should align with that of the observed data. Let \( [0,T_1), [0,T_2), \dots, [0,T_i),[0,T_I) \) denote a set of time windows corresponding to each observation session. Within each time window \( [0,T_i) \), let \( N(T_i) \) represent the number of aggressive behavior onsets observed within that time window. Then, the observed empirical count distribution is the probability density function over the observed counts \( N(T_1), N(T_2), \ldots, N(T_I) \). The true count distribution represents the number of aggressive behavior onsets per observation session across all sessions. In contrast, the \gls{tpp} model count distribution is obtained by simulating onsets within a given time window. We generate the sample count distribution as
\vspace{-0.5em}
\begin{align}
T_i &\sim U(\{T_1, T_2, \ldots T_I\}) \nonumber \\
\theta &\sim p(\theta | S) && \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad
t_1 &\sim f_\theta(t_1 | \h_{t_0}), \quad t_2 \sim f_\theta(t_2 | \h_{t_1}), \quad \dots, \quad t_j \sim f_\theta(t_j | \h_{t_{j-1}}) \quad \text{for } t_j < T_i \nonumber \\
n_i &= N(T_i) \nonumber
\end{align}
\noindent where $U$ is a uniform distribution , \( \theta \) are the model parameters, \( t_j \) are sampled event times conditioned on past history \( \h_{t_{j-1}} \), and \( n_i \) is the total number of events within the window \( T_i \). If the \gls{tpp} model fits well, the \gls{kde} of the true count distribution and the sample count distribution should be similar or completely overlap \cite{ludke2023add}. The difference between the true empirical versus generated empirical count distributions is quantified via the \gls{wd}, where a lower \gls{wd} is better \cite{kolourioptimal}. For two discrete probability distributions, the \gls{wd} measures the minimum total cost of moving probability mass from one distribution to another, where the cost is given by the weighted sum of distances between points, optimized over all possible transport plans. 

Unlike benchmark metrics such as log-likelihood, \gls{aic}, and \gls{bic}, that provide only relative model performance comparisons, \gls{qq} plots, raw residual plots, and count distribution plots assess how well the chosen model approximates the true generative process (in the present case, aggressive behavior onsets).


\subsubsection{Pareto Smoothed Importance Sampling - Leave One Out Expected Log Predictive Density}
Informally, \gls{psis-loo} \gls{elpd} can be interpreted as the ability of the \gls{tpp} model to predict aggressive behavior onsets in a new session based on prior sessions. A higher \gls{psis-loo} \gls{elpd} value indicates a better predictive model. The \gls{psis-loo} \gls{elpd} is an estimate of the LOO cross-validation \gls{elpd}, as though the model were refit on all sessions except the holdout observation session, and then evaluated on the holdout session. Formally, the \gls{elpd} is defined as
\noindent\begin{minipage}{.4\linewidth}
\begin{equation}
    \text{ELPD}_{\text{loo}} = \sum_{i=1}^{N} \log p(S_i \mid S_{-i}) \\
\end{equation}
\end{minipage}%
\begin{minipage}{.6\linewidth}
\begin{equation}
    p(S_i \mid S_{-i}) = \int p(S_i \mid \theta) p(\theta \mid S_{-i}) d\theta \approx \frac{1}{\sum_{m=1}^{M} p(S_i \mid \theta^{(m)})}
\end{equation}
\end{minipage}
\newline 
where $M$ is the number of post-warmup \gls{mcmc} samples from the posterior distribution $p(\theta \mid S)$, and $S_{-i} = S \backslash S_i$ represents all observation sessions except observation session $i$. The \gls{psis} stabilizes importance sampling estimates by fitting a generalized Pareto distribution to the upper tail of the importance ratios. It then replaces the upper tail with evenly sampled quantiles of the Pareto distribution at the upper tail, smoothing extreme values and improving reliability of the \gls{psis-loo} \gls{elpd} estimate, especially when dealing with heavy-tailed distributions. However, log-likelihood values may be challenging to interpret; thus, we also include the \gls{mape} calculation for better interpretability.

\subsubsection{Mean Absolute Percentage Error}
To evaluate forecasting performance, we assess the \gls{tpp} model's ability to predict the number of aggressive behavior onsets within a time window $[t,t+\Delta t]$. A starting point $t_{\text{start}} \in [\Delta t, T-\Delta t]$ is randomly selected, with aggressive behavior onsets in $[0,t_{\text{start}}]$ forming the history $\h$, and those in $[t_{\text{start}}, t_{\text{start}} + \Delta t]$ constituting the forecast goal. The \gls{mape} is calculated as
\begin{align}
\text{MAPE}(N^*(t,t+\Delta t), \tilde{N}^*(t,t + \Delta t)) = \frac{ | N^*(t,t + \Delta t) - \tilde{N}^*(t,t+\Delta t) | }{\max(1,N^*(t,t+\Delta t))} \times 100
\end{align}
where $N^*(t,t+\Delta t)$ is the observed number of aggressive behavior onsets in $[t,t+\Delta t)$, and $\tilde{N}^*(t,t+\Delta t)$ is the median predicted count in $[t,t+\Delta t)
$, conditioned on the history $\h$. Note that the denominator of \gls{mape} has $\max(1,N^*(t,t+\Delta t))$ to ensure that 0 is not a divisor. The median prediction is
\vspace{-0.5em}
\begin{align}
\tilde{N}^*(t,t+\Delta t) = \text{med}\left( \tilde{N}^*(t,t+\Delta t)_m \mid m = 1:M \right).
\end{align}
For each observation session $S_i$, we sample 25 random $t_{\text{start}}$ locations and their corresponding forecasting windows to compute the \gls{mape}. A lower \gls{mape} indicates a better predictive model for the number of aggressive behavior onsets within a future time window.
