\section{Results}

\subsection{Count Distribution}

\Cref{fig:countdistribution} shows the result comparing count distributions between \gls{hpp}, \gls{nhpp}, \gls{hwkpl}, \gls{hwkexp}, and \gls{hwk2exp} and the true count distribution. \cref{fig:countdistribution}(a,b) compares the count distributions of aggressive behavior onsets per session for the true data and the \gls{hpp} and \gls{nhpp} models. The empirical distribution (orange) is highly right-skewed, resembling an exponential distribution with a large probability mass at zero and a long, heavy tail. In contrast, the \gls{hpp} and \gls{nhpp} model-generated distributions (blue) exhibit a truncated Gaussian-like shape, centered around 15-16 counts per session, failing to capture the zero-inflation and heavy tail of the true distribution. This discrepancy suggests that both models struggle to account for the bursty and sparse nature of aggressive behavior onsets observed in the data. For instance, in a 100-minute observation session with no aggressive behavior onsets, the \gls{hpp} model would still predict an average of 16 occurrences, highlighting its inability to capture zero-inflation in the data. However, as shown in \cref{fig:countdistribution} (c,d,e), the distributions generated by the self-exciting \gls{tpp} models—\gls{hwkpl}, \gls{hwkpp}, and \gls{hwk2exp} (blue)—closely match the empirical distribution (orange). Notably, the \gls{kde} of the observed and generated session counts exhibit complete overlap for the \gls{hwkpp} and \gls{hwk2exp}, demonstrating the models' ability to accurately capture the true count distribution. Quantitatively, using the \gls{wd} metric, \cref{tab:metric} shows that the self-exciting processes (\gls{hwkpl}, \gls{hwkexp} and \gls{hwk2exp}) clearly lead to better estimations of the underlying count distribution compared to \gls{hpp} and \gls{nhpp}, with \gls{hwk2exp}  leading to the smallest \gls{wd}. This metric was computed from 40 \gls{mc} trials.

