\subsection{QQ Plots}

\Cref{fig:qqplot} presents the \gls{qq} plots for the \gls{hpp}, \gls{nhpp}, \gls{hwkpl}, \gls{hwkexp}, and \gls{hwk2exp} models, illustrating the inter-onset time quantiles of the transformed times obtained via the \gls{rtc} theorem (\cref{eqn:rtc}) against the expected exponential distribution with a mean rate of 1. \Cref{fig:qqplot}(a,b) illustrates that the \gls{hpp} and \gls{nhpp} fail to capture the bursty nature of aggressive behavior onsets, as their inter-onset distributions allocate excessive probability density to larger onset counts compared to the true data. This leads to overestimating the number of aggressive behavior onsets during low-intensity periods (when few or no onsets occur over extended durations) and underestimating them during self-excitation phases, highlighting their limitations in modeling self-excitatory dynamics. \cref{fig:qqplot}(c,d) shows that the \gls{hwkpl} and \gls{hwkexp} tend to generate slightly shorter inter-onset times than observed aggressive behavior onsets. This deviation is particularly pronounced in the tail of the distribution, where the model underestimates longer inter-onset times, leading to a bias toward shorter predicted intervals. However, the \gls{hwkpl} captures the tail of the distribution more effectively than the \gls{hwkexp} due to the scale-free nature and long tail of the \gls{pl} kernel. This suggests that in sessions with extended gaps between aggressive events, the \gls{hwkexp} tends to assume that subsequent events will occur sooner than they do.  \cref{fig:qqplot}(e) shows that the \gls{hwk2exp} effectively corrects the tail deviation observed in \cref{fig:qqplot}(c,d), reducing the underestimation of longer inter-onset times. This improvement stems from the \gls{hwk2exp}'s use of two scaled exponential kernels—one capturing short-term and the other long-term dependencies—enabling rapid and gradual variations in the intensity function. Additionally, the Markovian property of the exponential kernel facilitates better parameter inference. Consequently, the \gls{hwk2exp} provides a more flexible representation of temporal dynamics, better aligning with the observed inter-onset structure.

\subsection{Raw Residual Plots}

\Cref{fig:rawresidual} displays the raw residuals against the number of aggressive behavior onsets per session for each \gls{tpp} model. By the Doob-Meyer decomposition theorem, residuals,~\cref{eqn:rawresidual}, should be centered around 0, and are represented by the orange line. \cref{fig:rawresidual}(a,b) shows that the \gls{hpp} and \gls{nhpp} models overestimate onsets in sessions with few occurrences and underestimate them in bursty sequences, consistent with the \gls{qq} plots. In contrast, \cref{fig:rawresidual}(c,d,e) indicates that the \gls{hwkpl}, \gls{hwkexp}, and \gls{hwk2exp} residuals are closer to 0. However, they exhibit a slight positive drift in raw residual error as the number of onsets in an observation session increases.

