\subsection{Pareto Smoothed Importance Sampling - Leave One Out Expected Log Predictive Density}

The \gls{psis-loo} of the \gls{elpd} for each model, along with the standard deviation computed across the observation sessions, is shown in \cref{tab:metric}. For the calculations in \cref{tab:metric}, 100\% of the Pareto \( k \)-diagnostic values were in the ``good`` range for the \gls{hwkpl}, \gls{hwkexp}, \gls{hwk2exp}, and \gls{tpp} models, while about 99\% of the values for the \gls{hpp} and \gls{nhpp} models were in the ``good" range, with the remainder in the ``ok" range (except 1 sample being in the ``bad" range for the \gls{nhpp} model). These ranges of ``good,'' ``ok,'' and ``bad'' are chosen based on the fitted Pareto distributions $k$ parameter when performing \gls{psis-loo} \gls{elpd}, with more information found in  \cite{vehtari2017practical,vehtari2024pareto}. This indicates that the \gls{psis-loo} of the \gls{elpd} provides a reliable and accurate estimate of the LOO \gls{elpd}, as if the model were actually refitted and evaluated using a LOO cross-validation strategy. The \gls{hwk2exp} model demonstrates the best predictive capability, followed closely by the \gls{hwkpp} model, and then the \gls{hwkpl}. However, the \gls{hpp} and \gls{nhpp} models have nearly $2\times$ lower predictive capability. 

\subsection{Mean Absolute Percentage Error}

\noindent We evaluate the \gls{mape} for the forecasted number of aggressive behavior onsets across time windows of $\Delta t = 1, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25$ minutes (\cref{tab:metric}). As the forecasting window increases, the performance gap between the \gls{nhpp} and \gls{hpp} models versus the self-exciting \glspl{tpp} widens. This is because the \gls{hpp} and \gls{nhpp} models do not incorporate the history of aggressive behavior onsets or account for self-excitation. Consequently, when many observation sessions have no aggressive behavior onsets, the \gls{hpp} and \gls{nhpp} models tend to overestimate the number of future onsets significantly. In contrast, the \gls{hwkpl}, \gls{hwkexp}, and \gls{hwk2exp} models, with a low background intensity $\mu$, forecast fewer aggressive behavior onsets when there is little or no history of such onsets. 

Although \gls{mape} may be high for long-horizon probabilistic forecasts, \cref{fig:forecastcount} demonstrates the potential of estimating future aggressive behavior onset counts using only the history of past onsets. Notably, the median of the probabilistic forecast closely follows the true aggressive behavior counting process. Additionally, the upper bound of the 90\% credible interval for the \gls{hwk2exp} model is significantly higher than its lower bound, in contrast to the \gls{nhpp}. This discrepancy may be attributed to the branching factor, or infectivity, of the \gls{hwk2exp} being in the critical (\(\approx 1\)) or above-critical (\(>1\)) regime \cite{hardiman2013critical}, which leads to a nonzero probability of an infinite number of triggered onsets, otherwise stated as an explosion. In the next section, we further discuss the branching factor of the \gls{hwk2exp} and \gls{hwkpl}.

A corresponding analysis using the \gls{rocauc} \cite{narkhede2018understanding}, instead of the \gls{mape}, is provided in the \suppref{appsec:classification}. Rather than estimating the number of onsets occurring within a randomly sampled window of size $\Delta t$, we predict the probability of at least one onset occurring in the window using \cref{eqn:condcdf}. The ground truth label is 1 if any onsets occur within the window and 0 otherwise.

\subsection{Branching Factor}

The branching factor of a \gls{hwkpp} is the average number of ``daughter'' (endogenous) events triggered by each ``parent'' (exogenous) event. It is a key parameter that determines the self-exciting behavior of the process. Mathematically, the branching factor is defined as \( BF = \int_{0}^{\infty} \phi(t) dt \), where $\phi(t)$ is the triggering kernel in \cref{eqn:generalhwk}. The branching factor equation for the \gls{hwkexp}, \gls{hwk2exp}, and \gls{hwkpl} is shown in the x-axis label of \cref{fig:bf} (a),(b),(c), respectively. The branching factor \( BF \) determines the stability of a \gls{hwkpp}. If \( BF < 1 \), the process is subcritical, meaning each onset triggers fewer than one new onset on average, leading to a stable process. If \( BF = 1 \), the process is critical, where each onset triggers exactly one onset on average, maintaining a delicate balance between exploding versus stability of onset generation. If \( BF > 1 \), the process is supercritical, where each onset triggers more than one onset on average, potentially resulting in an explosive cascade of events. Understanding \( BF \) is essential for assessing the long-term behavior and stability of a \gls{hwkpp}. 

Estimating the branching factor requires careful consideration of potential biases inherent in data collection and model parameter estimation processes. As shown in \cite{filimonov2015apparent}, branching factor estimates, particularly for \gls{hwkpl} and \gls{hwk2exp}, can be inflated by outliers. To verify that our branching factor estimation is not biased by outliers, we follow the approach in \cite{filimonov2015apparent} by comparing quantile ratios (95\%/90\%, 99\%/95\%, and max/90\%) between synthetic and empirical inter-onset sequences, using inverse \gls{cdf} interpolation for observation sessions with at least five events. Additionally, we define outliers as inter-onset times exceeding $2\times$ the 99\% quantile \cite{filimonov2015apparent}. As shown in \cref{tab:branchingfactor}, no substantial evidence suggests that outliers significantly affect our branching factor estimation. However, even if there were outliers, the branching factor estimate for the \gls{hwkexp} model has been shown to remain robust to a significant number of outlier inter-onsets in the data \cite{filimonov2015apparent}. Another potential source of bias in branching factor estimation is edge effects \cite{filimonov2015apparent}. However, we assume their impact on model parameter estimation is minimal (refer to \secref{subsec:modelchoice} for further details).

When the branching factor is in the subcritical regime, the $BF$ is interpreted as the percentage of onsets in an observation session triggered by an exogenous onset. Since the \gls{hwkexp} has an average branching factor of $0.85 - 0.95$, this suggests that $85\%-95\%$ of all aggressive behavior onsets are triggered by a past aggressive behavior onset rather than external factors.  For both \gls{hwkpl} and \gls{hwk2exp}, there is a non-zero probability of the $BF$ exceeding 1, though only slightly. 

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

We employed \glspl{tpp} to model aggressive behavior onset in inpatient youths with autism. Our \gls{gof} statistics, such as the \gls{qq} plot (\cref{fig:qqplot}), raw residual plot (\cref{fig:rawresidual}), and count distribution (\cref{fig:countdistribution}), indicate that self-exciting point processes, such as the \glspl{hwkpp}, may closely capture the data generation process of irregular and seemingly random aggressive behavior onset times. This was expected, as clinical observation by domain experts and caregiver reports suggests that aggressive behavior onsets happen in temporal clusters, and further prior data exploration using the Ripley K estimator \cite{karlis2023proposer} indicated high temporal clustering in many of the observation sessions (analysis of Ripley K estimator done on sessions with a minimum of 15 aggressive behavior onset events, \suppref{appsec:ripley}). Our predictive metrics, such as \gls{psis-loo},  \gls{elpd}, and \gls{mape} indicate that the predictive performance of the \glspl{hwkpp} performed best. We observe from the \gls{mape} and \cref{fig:forecastcount} that there is potential to use \gls{hwkpp} to forecast the number of aggressive behavior onsets in larger sized future windows. However, improvements are still needed (and possible). We note that the \gls{gof} statistics and predictive metrics evidence a slight modeling gap, which may be due to the data sample size or the need for more advanced \glspl{tpp} (e.g., more advanced kernel functions in the self-exciting process, \gls{dl}-based \gls{tpp} models, inclusion of physiological covariates). 

From a knowledge discovery viewpoint, the interpretation of the model parameters of the \gls{hwkpp} shows that the branching factor is close to 1 (\cref{fig:bf}), the critical regime, which indicates that once an aggressive behavior onset is observed, a cascade of aggressive behavior onsets is highly likely to follow. Thus, interventions that prevent the first instance or mitigate early occurrences of aggressive behavior onsets, or ``mother'' onsets, should be prioritized. This suggests that careful interactions and interventions should be provided to inpatient youths with autism to prevent a cascade of subsequent aggressive behavior onsets .These insights also introduce new research questions, such as whether we can generate the branching structure of the cluster representation of a \gls{hwkpp} to identify all the ``mother'' onsets and, through post-analysis, understand what triggers them.

Lastly, clinicians may find the probability of a subsequent aggressive behavior onset occurring within the next $t$ minutes, given the history of known aggressive behavior onsets up to $t$ (\cref{fig:probability_fns}), more useful for immediate intervention planning and decision-making, as probability is a standard representation in decision-making frameworks \cite{shachter1992decision}. This could enable pushing alerts to caregivers when the probability of an event occurring exceeds a given threshold or more nuanced triage based on multiple probability decision thresholds. Unfortunately, quantifying the performance of \gls{tpp} in decision-based frameworks using this probabilistic viewpoint is still an open research area  \cite{shchur2021neural}. An important open domain is creating an interpretable and meaningful metric for clinicians to quantify the performance of \gls{tpp} in their specific decision-making/intervention frameworks.

\section{Limitations and Future Work}
Following this initial analysis, we intend to explore three future directions. First, we plan to divide our sample into sub-samples based on key variables such as the number of observation sessions, total observation time, and total aggressive behavior onsets, duration, and intensity. By analyzing differences among these sub-samples, we aim to gain deeper insights into the variability of aggressive behavior and its recurrence. Second, we will investigate statistical differences in inferred model parameters and examine variations in modeling choices based on the type of condition label, including \gls{sib}, \gls{ed}, and \gls{ato}. Thirdly, we will utilize a hierarchical \gls{hwkpp} to account for between-person heterogeneity and disparities in data sample sizes among participants.

The distribution of aggressive behavior onsets per participant appears to follow a power-law pattern. Specifically, one participant accounts for 26.5\% of the aggressive behavior onsets (1,287 onsets), two participants contribute 25\% (475 to 910 onsets), five participants contribute 20.8\% (106 to 476 onsets), and 62 participants account for 27.7\% (0 to 105 onsets). This distribution suggests the existence of subpopulations within the data, conceptualized as chronically aggressive, hyper-aggressive, aggressive, and mildly aggressive. Given the substantial disparity in onsets exhibited by individual participants, hierarchical \gls{hwkpp} could address intra-individual and inter-individual variability. A single-level hierarchical \gls{hwkpp} could refine the analysis of the branching factor by modeling inter-individual heterogeneity and homogeneity. In contrast, a two-level hierarchical model would capture intra-individual and inter-individual session variability, further enhancing the analysis of edge effects, as discussed with the modified \gls{hwkpp} in \secref{subsec:modelchoice}. This two-level model would also enable the inference of a $\mu_0$ per observation session. Finally,  we aggregated three distinct behaviors into a single aggression onset label. A more nuanced analysis would involve modeling \gls{ato}, \gls{sib}, and \gls{ed} separately as each type of aggression may follow a distinct generative process, with potentially different baseline intensities and infectivity factors.

While our approach demonstrates strong promise in learning a \gls{tpp} that closely captures the generative process of aggressive behavior onsets, it still has limitations in predictive performance, mainly when answering questions like ``how many events will occur within the next 5 minutes?''. Another limitation is that we do not include physiological covariates in our analysis, which have been shown to be strong predictors of aggressive behavior onsets \cite{imbiriba2023wearable}. Another limitation is that although we may learn the generative process of aggressive behavior onsets with reasonable accuracy, this does not necessarily translate into improved predictive capability. To illustrate with a simple example, consider inferring the probability of heads in a fair coin toss. Estimating this probability as 0.5 from multiple flips is straightforward. However, even with an accurate estimate, it does not enable precise prediction of the outcome of the next flip or a sequence of flips. Our model may effectively capture the underlying generative process of aggressive behavior onsets but may still struggle to predict proximal occurrence given the history of onsets within an observation session. Another limitation is that \gls{mcmc} inference is notoriously slow, with online parameter update mechanisms still under research. However, we may explore online inference methods, such as Cubature Kalman Filters, for estimating the parameters of the intensity function in a \gls{hwkpp}. This would allow us to perform online updates of the model parameters with streaming data, bringing us a step closer to enabling just-in-time preventative interventions to reduce aggressive behavior in autistic youth.


