    %\vspace{0.22cm}
\includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{figures/simulant_workspace.png}
    \caption{ An image taken from the camera on the mast shows the diversity in material and visual properties of the simulant workspace.
    }
   \label{fig:owlat3}
   % \vspace{-3 mm}
\end{figure} 

To aid in the development of algorithms a simulator version of the testbed is provided. The simulator leverages the DARTS physics engine \cite{jain2020darts} and represents a majority of the subsystems and functionalities available on the hardware testbed. The simulator provides feedback modeled after the sensors used on the system, including force-torque sensor values, system and subsystem faults, and camera images. Users interact with this simulator testbed the same way they would interact with the hardware testbed, making the transition from interfacing with the simulated testbed to hardware testbed seamless. The availability of the simulated testbed can provide a platform to do higher risk development and testing. This allows users to collect initial verification of expected testbed performance as seen in Fig.~\ref{fig:owlat4}, and more safely deploy autonomy algorithms onto the physical hardware as depicted in Section~\ref{sec:recal}.
