\vspace{-.5cm}
\section{Introduction}
Let $\sl_N = \sl_N(\C)$ acting in its natural representation on $\C^N$.
The irreducible representations of $\sl_N$ can be constructed as $S^\lambda \C^N$, where $S^\lambda$ is a \emph{Schur functor} and $\lambda$ ranges over partitions of length at most $N$. A major open problem in representation theory is the \emph{plethysm problem} \cite{StanleyList, Mystery}: how does $S^\nu S^\mu \C^N$ decompose into irreducible representations? More precisely, the goal is to understand the coefficients $a^\lambda_{\nu[\mu]}$ occurring in the following equation:
\[
S^\nu S^\mu \C^N = \bigoplus\nolimits_{\lambda} \big(S^\lambda\C^N\big)^{\oplus a^\lambda_{\nu[\mu]}}.
\]

When $\lambda = (n)$ is a one-row partition, the Schur functor $S^{(n)}$ is the more familiar symmetric power functor $\Sym^n$.
Although it appears much simpler than the general problem, the following conjecture remains widely open \cite{McKay, EvseevPagetWildon, PW2, CIM}. We write $U\leq V$ when $U$ is isomorphic to a subrepresentation of $V$.
\begin{conjecture}[Foulkes \cite{Foulkes}]
    If $m \le n$ then $ \Sym^m \Sym^n \C^N \le \Sym^n \Sym^m \C^N$.
\end{conjecture}
For $\sl_2$, the conjecture holds (with equality) by Hermite reciprocity:
\[
\Sym^m \Sym^n \C^2 \cong \Sym^n \Sym^m \C^2.
\]
Several generalisations of Foulkes' conjecture are proposed by Doran \cite{Doran}, Ab\-des\-se\-lam--Chipalkatti \cite{AC}, and 
Bergeron \cite{Bergeron}. 
For $\sl_2$, these assert:
\begin{conjecture}[Generalised Foulkes conjecture for $\sl_2$] \label{conj:A} \, \\
    Fix $a, b, c, d \in \N$, $a$ being the smallest, $ab=cd$. Then
    \(
    \Sym^a\Sym^b\C^2 \le \Sym^c\Sym^d\C^2.
    \)
\end{conjecture}
The cases where $a = 2$ \cite{AC} and $a = 3$ \cite{Vessenes, Zanello} of the conjecture are known to hold. We prove Conjecture \ref{conj:A} for a large family of parameters, including all prime values of $a$.
\begin{mainthm}\label{thm:main}
Fix $a, b, c, d \in \N$ such that $a$ is the smallest, $ab=cd$, and $a$ divides $c$ or $d$. Then
		\(\Sym^a\Sym^b\C^2\) is an $\sl_2$-subrepresentation of \(\Sym^c\Sym^d\C^2.\)
\end{mainthm}

\begin{note}
    By Hermite reciprocity, the conjecture is symmetric in $c$ and $d$; in our proofs, we assume that $a$ divides $c$. Given that $ab=cd$ and $a | c$, we also have $d | b$.  
\end{note}\medskip

Our proof is short and direct.
It hinges on a specific model of the representation $\Sym^m\Sym^n\C^2$ as a $\C$-algebra of symmetric polynomials $\Lambda_{\le n}[\mathbf{x}_m]$ of degree at most $n$ in each of $m$ variables. Then the $\frac{b}{d}$-fold plethystic substitution (defined in \S\ref{sec:main})
\begin{align*}
\Lambda_{\le a}[\mathbf{x}_b] &\to \Lambda_{\le c}[\mathbf{y}_d]\\
f(\mathbf{x}_b) &\mapsto \textstyle f\big[\frac{b}{d}\mathbf{y}_d\big]
\end{align*}
is seen to be an injective homomorphism of $\sl_2$-representations. This approach was first developed by the authors, Martínez, and Wildon in \cite{GMSW}; it brings symmetric functions into representation theory \emph{without taking characters.}\medskip

The matrix of this map has several remarkable properties which are studied in~\S\ref{sec:matrix}, after the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:main} and its corollaries.

\subsection{Gaussian coefficients}
Let $q$ be an indeterminate.
The study of $\sl_2$ plethysms is intimately related to the study of $q$-analogues.
The \emph{$q$-analogue of $n\in\mathbb{Z}$} is $\qinteger{n} = \frac{1-q^n}{1-q} = 1+q+\cdots+q^{n-1}$. The \emph{Gaussian coefficients} are defined by
\[
\qbinom{n}{k} = 
\frac{\qinteger{n}\qinteger{n-1}\cdots\qinteger{n-k+1}}{\qinteger{k}\qinteger{k-1}\cdots\qinteger{1}}
\]
for $0\le k \le n$. It is not immediately apparent, but the Gaussian coefficient $\qbinom{n}{k}$ is in fact a polynomial in $q$ with non-negative integer coefficients. 

The $\gl_2$-character of $\Sym^m\Sym^n\C^2$ is the principal specialisation
\[
s_{(m)}(1,q,\ldots,q^{n-1}) = \qbinom{n+m}{n}
\]
of the Schur function $s_{(m)}$ indexed by the partition $(m)$.
In Corollary~\ref{cor:q-Foulkes}, we settle the following conjecture of Bergeron \cite{BergeronSlides} whenever $a$ divides $c$ or $d$.
\begin{conjecture}[$q$-Foulkes conjecture for Gaussian coefficients]\label{conj:B}{\,}\\ 
Fix $a, b, c, d \in \N$, $a$ being the smallest, $ab=cd$. Then $\qbinom{c+d}{c} - \qbinom{a+b}{a} \in \N_0[q]$.
\end{conjecture}
\noindent
In \S\ref{sec:Troyka}, we settle multiple cases of the following conjecture of Troyka \cite{Troyka}.
\begin{conjecture}[Generalised $q$-Foulkes conjecture for Gaussian coefficients] \label{conj:C}
    Fix $a, b, c, d \in \N$, $a$ being the smallest, $ab\le cd$. Then $\qbinom{c+d}{c} - \qbinom{a+b}{a} \in \N_0[q]$.
\end{conjecture}

A sequence $(a_0, a_1, \ldots, a_n)\in\N_0^n$ is \emph{unimodal} if $a_1 \le a_2 \le \cdots \le a_k \ge \cdots\ge a_n$ for some $k$.
We settle the following conjecture of Zanello \cite{Zanello} when $a\mid c$ or $a\mid d$.
\begin{conjecture} \label{conj:D}
Fix $a, b, c, d \in \N$, $a$ being the smallest, $ab=cd$. Then the coefficients in $q$ of $\qbinom{c+d}{c} - \qbinom{a+b}{a}$ form a unimodal sequence.
\end{conjecture}

The coefficients of $\qbinom{n}{k} = a_0 + a_1q + \cdots + a_{n(n-k)}q^{n(n-k)}$ were conjectured to form a unimodal sequence by Cayley in 1856. Sylvester proved this in 1878, but a combinatorial proof would only come 112 years later as a very celebrated breakthrough of O'Hara \cite{OHara}. Before the present paper, the state-of-the-art results towards Conjectures~\ref{conj:A}, \ref{conj:B} and~\ref{conj:D} relied on an algebraisation of O'Hara's proof (see \cite{Zanello}).
Further refinements of O’Hara’s result remain an active area of research ---see \cite[\S2]{GutCrystals} for a recent survey. 

Given $f_0, f_1\in \N_0[q]$, we write $f_0 \le_q f_1$ if $f_1-f_0\in\N_0[q]$. A sequence $(f_0, f_1, \ldots, f_n)\in\N_0[q]^n$ is $q$-\emph{unimodal} if $f_1 \le_q \cdots \le_q f_k \ge_q \cdots\ge_q f_n$. Our results are related in spirit to those of Butler \cite{Butler}, who showed that $\qbinom{n}{0}, \qbinom{n}{1}, \ldots, \qbinom{n}{n}$ is a $q$-unimodal sequence. That is,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Butler}
\qbinom{n}{k} - \qbinom{n}{k-1} \in \N_0[q] \quad\text{for all }0\le k \le \Big\lfloor \frac{n}{2}\Big\rfloor.
\end{equation}


\section{Preliminaries}\label{sec:preliminaries}
\subsection{Symmetric functions}
We follow \cite[Ch.~7]{StanleyEC2} for background on symmetric functions, with the distinction that we work over $\C$ rather than $\ZZ$ or $\Q$.

An \emph{alphabet} is a set $\mathbf{x}_n = \{x_1, \ldots, x_n\}$ of variables.
Let
\(
\Lambda[\mathbf{x}_n] = \C[x_1, \ldots, x_n]^{S_n}
\)
be the \emph{$\C$-algebra of symmetric polynomials in $\mathbf{x}_n$}, which are polynomials in $x_1, \ldots, x_n$ invariant under any permutation of variables. 
Given an infinite alphabet $\mathbf{x}_\infty = \{x_1, x_2, \ldots\}$, the \emph{$\C$-algebra of symmetric functions in $\mathbf{x}_\infty$} is the colimit (or direct limit) as $n\to\infty$ of the $\C$-algebras of symmetric polynomials in $\mathbf{x}_n$.

A \emph{partition} $\lambda$ is a weakly decreasing finite sequence of non-negative integers $(\lambda_1, \lambda_2, \ldots, \lambda_k)$, called the \emph{parts} of $\lambda$. Its \emph{length} $\ell(\lambda)$ is the number of non-zero parts, and its \emph{size} is $|\lambda|=\lambda_1+\cdots+\lambda_k$.
We write $\Par$ for the set of partitions, $\Par_{\le h}$ for $\{\lambda\in\Par : \ell(\lambda)\le h\}$, and $L(h,w)$ for $\{\lambda\in\Par_{\le h} : \lambda_1\le w\}$.
The \emph{Young diagram $Y(\lambda)$} of a partition $\lambda$ is the set $\{(i,j) : 1\le j \le \lambda_i\}$, drawn as a top-left aligned array of boxes with $\lambda_i$ boxes in row $i$. The \emph{transpose $\lambda'$ of $\lambda$} is defined by $Y(\lambda') = \{(i,j) : (j,i)\in Y(\lambda)\}$. If $\lambda\in L(h,w)$, the \emph{box-complement partition} of $\lambda$ in $L(h,w)$ is defined by $\lambda^\square = (w-\lambda_h, w-\lambda_{h-1}, \ldots, w-\lambda_1)$. 
We write $\mu\subseteq\lambda$ if $Y(\mu)\subseteq Y(\lambda)$ and $\lambda\cap\mu$ for the partition such that $Y(\lambda\cap\mu) = Y(\lambda)\cap Y(\mu)$.
A \emph{skew partition} $\lambda/\mu$ is a pair of partitions such that $\mu\subseteq\lambda$. Its Young diagram is $Y(\lambda/\mu) = Y(\lambda)\setminus Y(\mu)$.
\medskip

Given a tuple of non-negative integers $(\alpha_1, \ldots, \alpha_n)$, let $\mathbf{x}_n^\alpha = x_1^{\alpha_1}\cdots x_n^{\alpha_n}$.
The \emph{monomial symmetric polynomial} $m_\lambda(\mathbf{x}_n)$ indexed by $\lambda$ is the sum $\sum_{\alpha \in S_n.\lambda} \mathbf{x}_n^\alpha$ over all permutations of $(\lambda_1, \ldots, \lambda_{\ell(\lambda)}, 0, \ldots, 0) \in \N_0^n$. The \emph{power sum symmetric polynomial} indexed by $k$ is $p_k(\mathbf{x}_n) = x_1^k + \cdots + x_n^k$. For a partition $\lambda$, set $p_\lambda(\mathbf{x}_n) = \prod_{1\leq i\leq \ell(\lambda)} p_{\lambda_i}(\mathbf{x}_n)$. The \emph{Schur polynomial} indexed by $\lambda$ is 
\[
s_\lambda(\mathbf{x}_n) = \det(x_j^{\lambda_i+n-i})_{1\le i,j\le n}/\det(x_j^{n-i})_{1\le i,j\le n}.
\]
\begin{thm}
    The families $\{m_\lambda(\mathbf{x}_n)  : \lambda\in\Par_{\le n}\}, \{p_\lambda(\mathbf{x}_n)  : \lambda\in\Par_{\le n}\},$ and \break$\{s_\lambda(\mathbf{x}_n)  : \lambda\in\Par_{\le n}\}$ of symmetric polynomials are $\C$-bases of $\Lambda[\mathbf{x}_{n}]$.
\end{thm}
By taking colimits or (direct limits) of these families, we obtain $\C$-bases \break$\{m_\lambda  : \lambda\in\Par\}$, $\{p_\lambda  : \lambda\in\Par\},$ and $\{s_\lambda  : \lambda\in\Par\}$ of $\Lambda[\mathbf{x}_\infty]$.

We remark that $s_\lambda(1, \stackrel{n}{\ldots}, 1)$ counts the number of \emph{semistandard Young tableaux $T\in\mathrm{SSYT}_n(\lambda)$} in $n$ letters and of shape $\lambda$ --- see \cite[\S7.10]{StanleyEC2} for the necessary definitions. The \emph{hook-content formula} is
\[
\#\mathrm{SSYT}_n(\lambda) = \prod_{c\in Y(\lambda)}\frac{n+c(u)}{h(u)},
\]
where for $u = (i,j) \in Y(\lambda)$, the number $h(u) = \lambda_i+\lambda'_j-i-j+1$ is its \emph{hook length}, and $c(u) = j-i$ its \emph{content}. The product is taken to be $0$ if $\mathrm{SSYT}_n(\lambda)=\emptyset$.
\medskip

The \emph{Hall scalar product} is the bilinear form $\langle\cdot\,,\cdot\rangle$ on $\Lambda[\mathbf{x}_\infty]$ for which the Schur functions are orthonormal: $\langle s_\lambda, s_\mu \rangle = \delta_{\lambda,\mu}$, where $\delta$ is the Kronecker delta. It follows that every $f \in \Lambda[\mathbf{x}_\infty]$ has a unique expansion $f = \sum \langle f, s_\lambda\rangle s_\lambda$. The basis orthonormal to monomial symmetric functions is denoted $\{h_\lambda  : \lambda\in\Par\}$.

The numbers $c_{\nu\mu}^\lambda = \langle s_\nu s_\mu, s_\lambda\rangle$ are the \emph{Littlewood--Richardson coefficients}. The \emph{skew Schur function} indexed by $\lambda/\mu$ is defined by $s_{\lambda/\mu} = \sum c_{\nu\mu}^\lambda s_\nu$.\medskip

In what follows, we consider alphabets $\mathbf{x}$, $\mathbf{y}$, $\mathbf{z}$ which may be either finite or infinite. 
The \emph{Cauchy identity} states
\[
\sum_{\lambda\in\Par} s_\lambda(\mathbf{x}) s_\lambda(\mathbf{y}) = \prod_{i,j}(1-x_i y_j)^{-1}.
\]
The \emph{addition} and \emph{product} of $\mathbf{x}$ and $\mathbf{y}$ are the alphabets
\[
\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{y} = \{x_1, y_1, x_2, y_2, \ldots\}
\quad\text{and}\quad
\mathbf{xy} = \{x_1y_1, x_1y_2, x_2y_1, \ldots\}.
\]
Given an alphabet $\mathbf{z}$, the map $f\mapsto f[\mathbf{z}]$ is a $\C$-algebra homomorphism \cite[\S2.3]{LoehrRemmel}. The \emph{plethystic addition formula} \cite[\S3.2]{LoehrRemmel} asserts that
\begin{equation}
    s_\lambda[\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{y}] = \sum\nolimits_{\mu} s_\mu(\mathbf{x}) s_{\lambda/\mu}(\mathbf{y}).
\label{eq:plet addition}
\end{equation}
Define the \emph{Kronecker coefficients} $g_{\lambda\mu\nu}$ 
as the constants appearing in
\[
s_\lambda[\mathbf{xy}] =
\sum\nolimits_{\mu,\nu} g_{\lambda\mu\nu} ~s_\nu(\mathbf{x}) s_\mu(\mathbf{y}).
\]
The \emph{Kronecker product} is defined by $s_\lambda * s_\mu(\mathbf{x}) = \sum_\nu g_{\lambda\mu\nu} ~s_\nu(\mathbf{x})$ on the Schur basis, and extended bilinearly to $\Lambda[\mathbf{x}]$. Combining these expressions we obtain the \emph{generalised Cauchy identity},
\(
s_\lambda[\mathbf{x}\mathbf{y}] = \sum\nolimits_\mu (s_\lambda * s_\mu)(\mathbf{x}) \cdot s_\mu(\mathbf{y}).
\)

Kronecker coefficients $g_{\lambda\mu\nu}$ are invariant under permutations of $\lambda, \mu, \nu$. A necessary condition for $g_{\lambda\mu\nu}\neq 0$ is $\ell(\nu)\leq\ell(\lambda)\ell(\mu)$. A stronger result states that if $|\lambda|=|\mu|$ then $\max\{\ell(\nu): g_{\lambda\mu\nu}\neq0\}=|\lambda\cap\mu'|$ \cite{Dvir}.


\subsection{Representations as polynomial rings}

We defined $\sl_2 = \sl_2(\C)$ to be the Lie algebra of $2\times2$ complex matrices with trace 0 and Lie bracket $[u,v] = uv-vu$.
It has a basis given by $\mathbf{e} = \left(\begin{smallmatrix}
        0 & 1 \\ 0 & 0
    \end{smallmatrix}\right)$, $\mathbf{h} = \left(\begin{smallmatrix}
        1 & 0 \\ 0 & -1
    \end{smallmatrix}\right)$, and $\mathbf{f} = \left(\begin{smallmatrix}
        0 & 0 \\ 1 & 0
    \end{smallmatrix}\right)$.
We refer the reader to \cite{ErdmannWildon} for an introductory text. 

The irreducible highest weight representations of $\sl_2$ are indexed by $n\in\N$ and classically constructed in two ways. The first is as the space $\CC_n[x,z]$ of degree~$n$ homogeneous polynomials in two variables. Crucially, by sending $z$ to $1$, this becomes isomorphic to the space $\CC_{\le n}[x]$ of  polynomials of degree at most $n$ in one variable. The second classical construction is as the symmetric power $\Sym^n\CC^2$.

While the first model is convenient for computations, the second is better suited for constructing the plethysms $\Sym^m\Sym^n\CC^2$ appearing in Conjecture~\ref{conj:A}. Combining both constructions gives
\[
\Sym^m \Sym^n\CC^2 \cong \Sym^m (\CC_{\le n}[x]) \cong \Lambda_{\le n}[\mathbf{x}_m],
\]
where $\Lambda_{\le n}[\mathbf{x}_m]$ is the 
$\C$-space 
of symmetric polynomials in $\mathbf{x}_m$, with degree at most $n$ in each of $m$ variables.
This approach was first used in \cite{GMSW}, where the following theorem is shown.
\begin{thm}\label{thm: SymSym as polynomials}
    There is an isomorphism of $\sl_2$-representations $\Sym^m \Sym^n\CC^2 \cong \Lambda_{\le n}[\mathbf{x}_m]$ and $\mathbf{f} = \left(\begin{smallmatrix}
        0 & 0 \\ 1 & 0
    \end{smallmatrix}\right)$ acts on $\Lambda_{\le n}[\mathbf{x}_m]$ as the operator $\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_m} = \frac{\partial}{\partial x_1} + \cdots + \frac{\partial}{\partial x_m}$. 
\end{thm}

\begin{note}\label{note: sl2 invariance}
To show that a linear map $\varphi:\Lambda_{\le a}[\mathbf{x}_b]\to\Lambda_{\le c}[\mathbf{y}_d]$ is $\sl_2$-equivariant it suffices to show that it intertwines with the actions of $\mathbf{e}$ and $\mathbf{f}$. Indeed, by the relation $[\mathbf{e},\mathbf{f}]=\mathbf{h}$, the intertwining of $\mathbf{h}$ is implied; by linearity, intertwining with the elements of a basis is sufficient.

Since one can repeat the construction above (but setting $x=1$ instead) to obtain $\Lambda_{\le n}[\mathbf{z}_m]$, in practice $\sl_2$-equivariance amounts to $\nabla_{\mathbf{y}_d} \varphi = \varphi \nabla_{\mathbf{x}_b}$.
\end{note}\medskip

\noindent
Recall $L(h,w) = \{\text{partitions fitting in a $w\times h$ box}\} = \{\lambda\in\Par_{\le h} : \lambda_1 \le w\}$. 
\begin{thm}
    The following are bases of $\Lambda_{\le w}[\mathbf{x}_h]$:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\{m_\lambda(\mathbf{x}_h)  : \lambda \in L(h,w)\}$,
        \item $\{s_\lambda(\mathbf{x}_h)  : \lambda \in L(h,w)\}$.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}{\, }
\begin{enumerate}
    \item 
    We know that $\{m_\lambda(\mathbf{x}_h)  : \lambda \in L(h,w)\}$ is a linearly independent set in $\Lambda[\mathbf{x}_h]$. In fact, each $m_\lambda(\mathbf{x}_h)$ is in $\Lambda_{\le w}[\mathbf{x}_h]$ because $\lambda_1 \le w$. Finally, if \break$f = \sum_{\alpha_i \le w~\forall i} c_\alpha \mathbf{x}_h^\alpha \in \Lambda_{\le w}[\mathbf{x}_h]$ then 
    $f = \sum_{\lambda\in L(h,w)} c_\lambda m_\lambda(\mathbf{x}_h),$ showing it is a generating set.
    \item
    We know that $\{s_\lambda(\mathbf{x}_h)  : \lambda \in L(h,w)\}$ is a linearly independent set in $\Lambda[\mathbf{x}_h]$. Since $\{m_\lambda(\mathbf{x}_h)  : \lambda\in\Par_{\le h}\}$ is a basis of $\Lambda[\mathbf{x}_h]$, we can define $K_{\lambda,\mu}$ by $s_{\lambda}(\mathbf{x}_h) = \sum_{\mu} K_{\lambda, \mu}m_\mu(\mathbf{x}_h)$. We have $K_{\lambda,\mu} = 0$ unless $\mu_1 + \cdots + \mu_i \le \lambda_1 + \cdots + \lambda_i$ for all $i = 1, \ldots, h$ (see \cite[Prop.~7.10.5]{StanleyEC2}). In particular: unless $\mu_1 \le \lambda_1 \le w$. Hence, $\{s_\lambda(\mathbf{x}_h)  : \lambda \in L(h,w)\}$ is in the span of $\{m_\mu(\mathbf{x}_h)  : \mu \in L(h,w)\}$, which is $\Lambda_{\le w}[\mathbf{x}_h]$ by the previous part. Since it is linearly independent and of the same size, it forms a basis.\qedhere
\end{enumerate}
\end{proof}

\section{Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}}
\label{sec:main}
	In this section, we prove major cases of Conjectures~\ref{conj:A},~\ref{conj:B}, and~\ref{conj:D}. For this, we define the product of an alphabet $\mathbf{x}$ with a scalar $k\in\N$ as the alphabet
    \[
    k\mathbf{x} = \{x_1, \stackrel{k}{\ldots}, x_1, x_2, \stackrel{k}{\ldots}, x_2, \ldots\}.
    \]
	\begin{de}\label{de:k-fold map}
		The \emph{$k$-fold plethystic substitution}, or the \emph{$k$-fold map}, is defined as
		\begin{align*}
			\Lambda[\mathbf{x}_{kn}] &\to  \Lambda[\mathbf{y}_{n}] \\
			f(\mathbf{x}_{kn}) &\mapsto
			f[k\mathbf{y}_n] = f(y_1, \stackrel{k}{\ldots}, y_1, y_2, \stackrel{k}{\ldots}, y_2, \ldots).
		\end{align*}
	\end{de}
	Equivalently, the $k$-fold map 
	sends each $x_i$ to $y_{\lceil i/k\rceil}$ for all $i$. It is a $\C$-algebra homomorphism. The definition extends to infinite alphabets.\medskip

    Recall that we assume $a\mid c$ and $ab=cd$. Let $k = c/a = b/d$.
    Our goal is to show that the $k$-fold map realises an $\sl_2$-equivariant injection $\Lambda_{\leq a}[\mathbf{x}_b]\hookrightarrow\Lambda_{\leq c}[\mathbf{x}_d]$.

	\begin{p}\label{p:inj} 
        Let $k = b/d$.
		The $k$-fold map $\Lambda_{\leq a}[\mathbf{x}_{b}] \hookrightarrow  \Lambda_{\leq c}[\mathbf{y}_{d}]$ is injective.
	\end{p}
	\begin{proof}
		Recall that $\{p_\lambda : \lambda\in\Par_{\le n}\}$ is a basis of $\Lambda[\mathbf{x}_n]$.
        Note that with respect to this basis, the $k$-fold map is simply given by
        \begin{align*}
        \Lambda[\mathbf{x}_{b}] & \to \Lambda[\mathbf{y}_{d}]\\
        p_\lambda &\mapsto k^{\ell(\lambda)}p_\lambda,
        \end{align*}
        which is injective.
        Pre-compose with the injection
        $\Lambda_{\le a}[\mathbf{x}_b] \ni s_\lambda \mapsto s_\lambda \in \Lambda[\mathbf{x}_b]$
        defined for $\lambda \in L(b,a)$. We obtain an injection $\Lambda_{\le a}[\mathbf{x} _b] \ni f \mapsto f[k\mathbf{y}_d] \in \Lambda[\mathbf{y}_d]$.

		It remains to show that the image of this map lies in $\Lambda_{\le c}[\mathbf{y}_d]$. Since the $k$-fold map is linear, it suffices to consider the image of a single monomial:
		\begin{multline*}
			\Big(x_1^{a_1} x_2^{a_2} \cdots x_{k}^{a_k}\Big)
			\Big(x_{k+1}^{a_{k+1}} \cdots x_{2k}^{a_{2k}}\Big)
			\cdots 
			\Big(x_{b-k+1}^{a_{b-k+1}} \cdots x_{b}^{a_{b}}\Big)
			\mapsto \prod_{i=1}^d y_i^{a_{(i-1)k+1} + \cdots + a_{ik}}.
		\end{multline*}
		Since $a_j\leq a$ for all $j$, the degree of $y_i$ in the image is
		\[
		{a_{(i-1)k+1} + \cdots + a_{ik}} \le k \cdot a = \frac{b}{d}\cdot a = c,
		\]
		for each $i = 1, \ldots, d$, as claimed.
	\end{proof}

	\begin{p}
		Let $k=b/d$. The $k$-fold map $\Lambda_{\leq a}[\mathbf{x}_{b}] \hookrightarrow  \Lambda[\mathbf{y}_{d}]$ is $\sl_2$-equivariant.
	\end{p}
	\begin{proof}
		By Note~\ref{note: sl2 invariance}, it is enough to show
		\[
		(\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_b} f)[k\mathbf{y}_d] = \nabla_{\mathbf{y}_d}(f[k\mathbf{y}_d])
		\]
		for $f\in\Lambda_{\leq a}[\mathbf{x}_b]$. By linearity of the derivative, it suffices to check the above on a single monomial $\prod_{i=1}^b x_i^{a_i}$ appearing in $f$.

		Let $s_i=a_{(i-1)k+1} + \cdots + a_{ik}$. We compute directly:
		\begin{align*}
		  \left(\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_b} \prod_{i=1}^b x_i^{a_i}\right) [k\mathbf{y}_d]& = \left(\sum_{i=1}^b a_ix_i^{a_i-1}\prod_{j\neq i} x_j^{a_j} \right)[k\mathbf{y}_d]\\
          &= \sum_{i=1}^d s_i y_i^{s_i-1}\prod_{j\neq i}y_j^{s_j}
          = \nabla_{\mathbf{y}_d} \prod_{i=1}^d y_i^{s_i}. \qedhere
		\end{align*}
	\end{proof}

	\setcounter{mainthm}{0}
	\begin{mainthm}
        Fix $a, b, c, d \in \N$ such that $a$ is the smallest, $ab=cd$, and $a$ divides $c$ or $d$. Then
		\(\Sym^a\Sym^b\C^2\) is an $\sl_2$-subrepresentation of \(\Sym^c\Sym^d\C^2.\)
	\end{mainthm}
	\begin{proof}
		Recall from Theorem~\ref{thm: SymSym as polynomials} that the $\sl_2$-equivariant injection $\Lambda_{\leq a}[\mathbf{x}_{b}] \hookrightarrow  \Lambda_{\leq c}[\mathbf{y}_{d}]$ can be identified with an injective homomorphism $\Sym^b\Sym^a\mathbb{C}^2\hookrightarrow\Sym^d\Sym^c\mathbb{C}^2$. By Hermite reciprocity applied to both sides, we obtain the desired injection.
	\end{proof}
	\begin{note}
		Since $ab=cd$, the injection above lifts to an injection of $\gl_2$-representations $\Sym^a\Sym^b\CC^2 \hookrightarrow \Sym^c\Sym^d\CC^2$. The argument is standard, see \cite[Lem.~3.5]{PW3}.
	\end{note}
	\begin{cor}\label{cor:q-Foulkes}
		Conjectures~\ref{conj:B} and~\ref{conj:D} hold in the case where $a$ divides $c$ or $d$. Namely, fix $a, b, c, d \in \N$ such that $a$ is the smallest, $ab=cd$, and $a$ divides $c$ or $d$. Then
		$\qbinom{c+d}{c} - \qbinom{a+b}{a}$ is a polynomial in $q$ with non-negative coefficients which form a unimodal and symmetric sequence.
	\end{cor}
	\begin{proof}
		Consider the injection of $\sl_2$-representations $\Sym^a\Sym^b\CC^2 \hookrightarrow \Sym^c\Sym^d\CC^2$.
		Since $\sl_2$ is semisimple, there is a complement
		\[
		\Sym^a\Sym^b\CC^2 \oplus C \cong \Sym^c\Sym^d\CC^2.
		\]
		The degrees of both sides match, that is $ab=cd$, so $C$ lifts to a complement of $\gl_2$-representations. 
		The $\gl_2$-character of $C$ is $\qbinom{c+d}{c} - \qbinom{a+b}{a}$, and thus the coefficients are positive.
		The coefficients of a polynomial $f\in\N_0[q]$ form a symmetric and unimodal sequence if and only if $f$ is the character of a $\gl_2$-representation of homogeneous degree \cite[Thm.~2.1]{StanleySL2}.
	\end{proof}

\section{On the generalised \texorpdfstring{$q$}{q}-Foulkes conjecture}\label{sec:Troyka}

In this section, we settle \emph{almost all} cases of Conjecture~\ref{conj:C} (see Note~\ref{note:comparison}).
In the proofs, we use the \emph{$q$-Pascal identity}
\[
\qbinom{n}{k} - \qbinom{n-1}{k} = q^{n-k}\qbinom{n-1}{k-1},
\]
as well as Butler's $q$-unimodality result \eqref{eq:Butler}.
Our first proposition is independent of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.
\begin{p}\label{p:c+d>a+b}
Fix $a, b, c, d \in \N$ such that $a$ is the smallest, $ab=cd$, and $a+b\le c+d$. Then
		$\qbinom{c+d}{c} - \qbinom{a+b}{a}$ is a polynomial in $q$ with non-negative coefficients which form a unimodal and symmetric sequence.
\end{p}
\begin{proof}
	Suppose that $c \le d$ without loss of generality.
	The hypotheses imply $a \le c \le d$ and so $0\leq i\leq \lfloor\frac{c+d}{2}\rfloor$ for $i=a+1,\ldots,c$.
	Then Butler's $q$-unimodality result gives
	\[
	\begin{array}{rccl}
		\displaystyle 
		\qbinom{c+d}{c} &-& 
		\displaystyle 
		\qbinom{c+d}{c-1} &\in\N_0[q], \\[1em]
		\displaystyle 
		\qbinom{c+d}{c-1} &-& 
		\displaystyle 
		\qbinom{c+d}{c-2} &\in\N_0[q], \\[1em]
		&\vdots\\
		\displaystyle 
		\qbinom{c+d}{a+1} &-& 
		\displaystyle 
		\qbinom{c+d}{a} &\in\N_0[q].
	\end{array}
	\]
	Summing these up, $\qbinom{c+d}{c} - \qbinom{c+d}{a} \in \N_0[q]$. To conclude, use the same technique with the $q$-Pascal identity $(c+d)-(a+b)$ times to~obtain $\qbinom{c+d}{c} - \qbinom{a+b}{a} \in \N_0[q]$.
\end{proof}

Using Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, we can push the results even further. The strategy is simple: given $a, b, c, d$, we look for an additional pair of numbers $(x,y)$ such that
\[
ab = xy
\quad\text{and}\quad
x+y \le c+d.
\]
We require the tuple $(x,y,c,d)$ to satisfy the conditions of Proposition~\ref{p:c+d>a+b}, to obtain
\[
\qbinom{c+d}{c} - \qbinom{x+y}{x} \in \N_0[q],
\]
and we require the tuple $(a,b,x,y)$ to satisfy the conditions of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, to obtain
\[
\qbinom{x+y}{x} - \qbinom{a+b}{a} \in \N_0[q].
\]
Adding the two expressions, $\qbinom{c+d}{c} - \qbinom{a+b}{a} \in \N_0[q]$. This shows the next proposition.

\pagebreak
\begin{p}\label{p:all-cond}
	Let $a,b,c,d\in\mathbb{N}$ such that $a=\min(a,b,c,d)$ and $ab\le cd
    $. If there exists a divisor $\ell$ of $b$ satisfying 
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\min(a,b,a\ell, b/\ell)=a$,
        \item $\min(a\ell, b/\ell, c, d)\in\{a\ell, b/\ell\}$, and
        \item $a\ell+b/\ell\le c+d$,
    \end{enumerate}
    then
	$\qbinom{c+d}{c} - \qbinom{a+b}{a}$ is a polynomial in~$q$ with non-negative coefficients.
\end{p}

\begin{note}
	Since $\min(a,b,c,d)=a$, condition (1) simplifies to $\ell\le b/a$. Similarly, conditions (2) and (3) together translate to $\ell \in I \setminus J$, where
    \begin{align*}
        I = \Bigg[\frac{c+d-\sqrt{(c+d)^2-4ab}}{2a},& ~~\frac{c+d+\sqrt{(c+d)^2-4ab}}{2a}\Bigg],\\
        J = \Big(\min\big(\sqrt{b/a}, ~~c/a, ~~d/a\big),& ~~\max\big(\sqrt{b/a}, ~~b/c, ~~b/d\big)\Big).
    \end{align*}
\end{note}


\begin{note}\label{note:comparison}
	A natural question is 
	\begin{quote}\itshape
		how many cases of Conjecture~\ref{conj:C} do these results cover?
	\end{quote}
	The short answer is: \textit{almost all of them}.\medskip

	For instance, fix $(a,b)=(11,65)$. We plot below all possible choices of \break$11 \le c,d \le 120$. The blue points correspond to the cases of the conjecture settled by Propositions~\ref{p:c+d>a+b} and~\ref{p:all-cond}; the red points remain open. We also plot the line $c+d=a+b$ and the curve $cd=ab$.
