\section{Concluding remarks and future directions}

We have used the action of $\sl_2(\C)$ on symmetric functions to give direct, short, and elegant proofs of conjectures that many believed would remain open for years to come. Our explicit $k$-fold map proving the $q$-Foulkes conjecture depends critically on the assumption that $a$ divides $c$ or $d$, and we believe that more machinery would need to be developed to use the $k$-fold map for the remaining cases of the conjectures.

On the other hand, the simplicity of our arguments and the appealing form of the matrices of the $k$-fold map suggest there might be \emph{enumerative} proofs of the conjectures when $a$ divides $c$ or $d$. Such an argument could potentially be derived from our constructions and formulas such as those in \S\ref{sec:matrix}. We leave this for future exploration. 

