\section*{Abstract}
Evolutionary graph theory is the study of population structure, in the form of graphs, on evolutionary dynamics. It is a well-known problem in evolutionary graph theory that the spread of mutation, as measured by fixation probability, is impacted by the graph type chosen and the update rule. For example, considering the two typical update rules, birth-death with fitness on birth (Bd) and death-birth with fitness on birth (dB), a star is an amplifier of natural selection for Bd but a suppressor of natural selection for dB. A continuous-time EGT model has been found to replicate Bd and dB results as special cases. Using this model, we show that the natural death rate (the intrinsic death rate) causes a shift from Bd to dB dynamics, and therefore can shift graphs from amplifying natural selection to suppressing natural selection. Taking the well-known star graph, we derived an expression of the difference between the fixation probability for the star and the well-mixed population in terms of the natural death rate and mutant's fitness. From this, we find the critical value where the star graph changes from amplifying to suppressing natural selection. Assuming the mutant has a fitness advantage over the resident, we show that if the natural death rate is greater than $\frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}$ the star is a suppressor, where $N$ is the number of nodes, providing an upper bound on the critical value. As $N \longrightarrow \infty$, the critical value of  the natural death rate $\longrightarrow 0$, so as the size of the graph increases, the star graph is likely to be suppressing for any non-zero natural death rate. 
