%%%%%% Abstract %%%%%%
\begin{abstract}
High-order harmonic generation (HHG) in gases driven by intense laser fields has become a cornerstone technique for producing attosecond pulses and probing ultrafast electronic motion in matter. By manipulating the microscopic and macroscopic dynamics involved in HHG, it is possible to tailor the temporal and spectral properties of attosecond light pulses. In this work, we control the carrier-to-envelope phase (CEP) of ultrashort femtosecond laser pulses to generate short trains of attosecond pulses that we characterize using two-color laser-assisted photoionization. We observe strong and unexpected variations of the photoelectron spectra as a function of the photoelectron kinetic energy as we change the CEP of the driving pulses. To interpret our results, we carried out HHG simulations that include microscopic and macroscopic effects. We find that the time-dependent phase-matching of the harmonics and the temporal chirp of the attosecond pulses play a major role in our observation, opening new perspectives for temporally resolving and controlling the HHG process.
\end{abstract}

%%%%%% Main Text %%%%%%

\section{Introduction}
High-order Harmonic Generation of an intense laser in gas targets has enabled the production of short pulses of light at the attosecond time scale in the extreme-ultraviolet (XUV) range \cite{Ferray_1988}. This process arises from two coexisting physical phenomena: the highly non-linear interaction of the intense laser with an isolated atom (or molecule) - a microscopic phenomenon known as single-atom response \cite{Kulander_1992,Lewenstein_1994,Corkum_1993}, and the in-phase propagation of the generated XUV radiation and the driving laser field - a macroscopic phenomenon known as phase matching \cite{LHuillier_1992,Balcou_1997,Weissenbilder_2022}. The latter is central in determining the efficiency, coherence and spatiotemporal structure of the XUV emission. Consequently, considerable effort has been devoted to manipulating phase-matching conditions to control the properties of attosecond light sources (flux, conversion efficiency, etc... \cite{Jullien_2008,Ferrari_2010}) including the generation of single attosecond pulses. However, insight into the ultrafast temporal evolution of phase-matching has so far remained primarily theoretical \cite{PhysRevA.92.063839,Tao_2017}. Here, we identify experimental conditions that provide access to the temporal build-up of the phase-matching in each optical cycle using a few-cycle driver laser.

Laser-assisted photoionization schemes such as Streaking \cite{Hentschel_2001,Goulielmakis_2008} and RABBIT (Reconstruction of Attosecond Beatings by Interference of Two-photon transitions) \cite{agostini_RABBIT} are commonly used to characterize the temporal properties of isolated attosecond pulses and attosecond pulse trains (APTs). In RABBIT, an interferometric technique, multiple coherent broadband electron wavepackets created by the XUV harmonic comb interact with an near-infrared (NIR) probe field. The interaction results in additional peaks called sidebands between each pair of consecutive harmonics formed due to the absorption/emission of additional NIR photons. By scanning the delay between the XUV and the NIR, it is possible to extract the spectral (and thus temporal) properties of the XUV and probe fields \cite{mairessse&Quere}.

In this work, we study laser-assisted ionization of helium atoms using an attosecond pulse train generated by few-cycle laser pulses in a gas of argon atoms. Due to the short laser duration ($\leq$6 fs), the APTs contain a few attosecond pulses \cite{YuCheng_PNAS} and their number depends on the CEP of the driving laser. For certain CEP values, the photoelectron spectra exhibit features that cannot be explained by the structure of the APTs predicted by the single-atom response alone. Supported by complete 3D simulation and a 1D-model, we gain insights into the time-dependent build-up of HHG in the nonlinear medium, and show that laser-assisted photoionization spectra provide a fingerprint of the generated APTs in the time and frequency domain.


\section{Methods}

\begin{figure}[ht]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[scale=0.45]{Images/Experiment/Setup/Setup_schematic.pdf}
    \caption{(A) Schematic of the experimental setup. Laser pulses from a Ti:Sapphire oscillator are amplified using two nonlinear optical parametric amplification (NOPA) stages. The relative CEP of the output pulses is controlled shot-to-shot through the feedback of a stereo Above-Threshold-Ionization (SATI) device to a wedge pair in the oscillator \cite{Wittmann2009}. The CEP-controlled pulses are separated into a probe arm and a variably delayed pump arm by a beam splitter (BS). Short attosecond pulse trains are produced by the pump using HHG and recombined with the probe before being focused into the sensitive region of a 3D electron spectrometer.
    (B) The controllable relative CEP $\Delta \varphi_{\mathrm{CEP}}$ of the $\leq$ 6 fs laser pulses. (C) Momentum projected along $p_x$ and $p_z$ of the photoemitted electrons. The electrons can be distinguished between those emitted with $p_z>0$ ("Up") and those emitted with $p_z<0$ ("Down"), where $z$ is the axis along the detector and of the polarization of the light. BS: Beam splitter; SATI: Stero-ATI; SHG: Second harmonic generation; AF: Aluminium filter; RM: Recombination mirror. MCP: Micro-channel plate.}
    \label{fig:exp_schematic}
\end{figure}

The experiment, shown in Figure \ref{fig:exp_schematic}, was performed using an optical parametric chirped pulse amplification (OPCPA) system, providing sub-6 fs long pulses at a central wavelength of 850 nm with up to 10 $\mathrm{ \mu }$J pulse energy at a repetition rate of 200 kHz \cite{Mikaelsson_setup}. A stereo-ATI (SATI) setup installed in the beam path stabilized the CEP of the pulses on a shot-to-shot basis, providing a stability of around 160 mrad ($\sim$10$^\circ$) \cite{Wittmann2009}. Furthermore, the CEP was controlled by a wedge pair in the oscillator (not shown in the schematic). The laser beam was then split into a pump and a probe arm by a beamsplitter (BS). In the pump arm, the beam was tightly focused into an argon gas jet to generate a comb of phase-locked odd-order harmonics. The low-order harmonics, below $\sim$15 eV, and the generating field were filtered out with an aluminum foil (AF) of 200 nm. The pump was recombined with the probe using a holey mirror (RM). The delay $\tau$ between the XUV and the probe arm can be controlled using a delay stage in the pump arm. Once recombined, both fields were focused by a toroidal mirror into a helium gas jet; the probe intensity was estimated to be $\sim 10^{11}$ W/cm$^2$. A reaction microscope was used to measure the three-dimensional momentum of the photoelectrons created by the absorption of the XUV-only or the XUV+NIR radiation. In our experiments, the delay between the XUV light and the NIR probe was scanned for different CEP settings. Changing the CEP allowed us to vary the number of pulses in the train, typically between two and four \cite{YuCheng_PNAS}.


\section{Results and Discussion}
Figure \ref{fig:exp_PI}A and B presents photoelectron spectra as a function of the delay for two different CEP values separated by 90$^{\circ}$. In the figure, only electrons emitted in a solid angle of $+ 2\pi$ sr ("up") along the polarization axis of both XUV and NIR fields are shown (see Figure \ref{fig:exp_schematic})~\cite{YuCheng_PNAS}. At large delays, when the fields do not overlap, the photoelectron peaks are separated by a kinetic energy of $2\hbar\omega$ ($\hbar$ is the reduced Planck constant and $\omega$ is the laser frequency) due to single-photon ionization by the comb of harmonics. At small delays, the XUV and the NIR overlap, leading to additional features, which depend on the kinetic energy and the CEP.  Above 10 eV in A, and between 7 and 10 eV in B, the kinetic energy of the photoelectrons oscillates as a function of the delay with a periodicity equal to the laser period $\sim$2.6 fs. In other spectral regions, additional electron peaks (sidebands) are observed at kinetic energies between those due to the absorption of consecutive harmonics. In particular, in Figure \ref{fig:exp_PI}B above 12 eV, the pattern looks like a 'chessboard'. This remarkable energy dependence comes from the small number of attosecond pulses in the train. In this regime, the photoelectron spectra can be interpreted in terms of interference between a few electron wave packets, analog to multiple-slit interferences~\cite{YuCheng_PNAS}. Oscillations arise from the interference between two electron wave packets, with similar behavior as in streaking measurements \cite{Hentschel_2001, Goulielmakis_2008}. Additional electron peaks are due to interference of more than two electron wave packets, leading to sidebands, similar to RABBIT measurements performed with multiple cycle pulses~\cite{agostini_RABBIT, Mairesse_Science}.

\begin{figure}[ht]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[scale=0.30]{Images/Experiment/PI_exp_single_atom_CEP_60_CEP_150_deg_other.pdf}
    \caption{(A and B) Experimental photoelectron spectra as a function of the XUV-NIR delay for two different CEP values separated by 90$^\circ$. Only electrons photoemitted in the upper hemisphere around the polarization axis of the light fields are shown. (C  and D) Simulated photoelectron spectra within the single-atom response at the same relative CEP values.}
    \label{fig:exp_PI}
\end{figure}

Figure \ref{fig:exp_PI}C and D presents calculated photoelectron spectra within the single-atom response for two different CEP, separated by a certain value. The attosecond pulse train is obtained using the prediction of the semi-classical model \cite{single_atom_Corkum, kulander_proceeding} for the amplitude and phase variation of each pulse in the train \cite{ChenGuo_model}. The laser-assisted photoionization is then calculated within the strong field approximation (SFA) \cite{mairessse&Quere,YuCheng_PNAS}. While the experimental observation in A is well reproduced in C, the oscillations at low energies followed by the chessboard pattern at high energies in B cannot be observed, see for example D.

The minimum intensity $I_{\mathrm{min}}(q)$ required for a harmonic order $q$ to reach the plateau region can be obtained from the cut-off law $q\hbar\omega= I_p+ 3.17 U_p$, where $I_p$ is the ionization energy of argon and $U_p$ is the ponderomotive energy equal to $\alpha h I/(m_e\omega^2)$. In this expression, $\alpha$ is the fine structure constant, $I$ the laser intensity and $m_e$ the electron mass. The minimum intensity, expressed as 
  \begin{equation}
   I_{\mathrm{min}}(q)=\frac{m_e \omega^2}{3.17 \alpha h}(q\hbar\omega-I_p),
  \end{equation}
increases linearly as a function of the harmonic order $q$. For our few-cycle pulses ($\leq$ 6 fs), this implies that the highest-order harmonics are generated only during a few oscillations of the electric field at the center of the pulse. Consequently, the number of attosecond pulses in the train depends on a chosen spectral range. It decreases as the central photon energy increases. With our ultrashort pulses, only two half-cycles (or less) reach the intensity for the generation of the highest orders. On the other hand, lower order harmonics are generated over many half-cycles. The above argument would always result in RABBIT-like features at low photoelectron kinetic energies and a oscillatory pattern (streaking-like pattern) at higher photoelectron energies, independent of the CEP. From these arguments based on the single-atom response, the experimental observation in Figure \ref{fig:exp_PI}B cannot be explained.

To go beyond the single-atom response, we performed advanced HHG simulations that account for both microscopic and macroscopic effects. Specifically, we compute the far-field harmonic emission by solving the Schr\"odinger equation within the SFA \cite{Lewenstein_1994} and by including the propagation and absorption of the fundamental and harmonic fields in the nonlinear medium while accounting for the longitudinal phase-matching of the XUV radiation \cite{Hernandez-Garcia2010}. We also include sub-cycle ionization dynamics with the Yudin-Ivanov CEP-dependent model described in \cite{Yudin_Ivanov_etafe}. The driving laser field is defined as $E(t) =E_0 f(t) \exp[ i ( \omega t + \varphi_{\mathrm{CEP}} ) ] $, where $E_0$ is the amplitude of the electric field, $f(t)=\sin^2{( \pi t/t_{\mathrm{p}} )}$ (for $0 \leq t \leq t_{\mathrm{p}}$) and $\varphi_{\mathrm{CEP}}$ is the CEP. The pulse full-width at half maximum employed in the simulations is $4.85$ fs, the beam waist is $w_0 = 5$ $\mathrm{\mu m}$, and the peak intensity is $\sim 1.5\times10^{14}$ W/cm$^2$. The length of the medium is 36 $\mathrm{\mu m}$, and the argon pressure $\sim$4 bar \cite{Mikaelsson_setup}. In these conditions, reshaping of the driving laser is expected to be negligible. The calculated APTs are then used to simulate laser-assisted photoionization spectra using SFA as in the earlier case. The results are shown in Figure \ref{fig:full_vs_1D_model}A and B and reproduce the experimental observations, in particular the CEP dependence of the interference pattern. The inversion of the oscillatory behavior and the chessboard pattern only happens for a small range of $\varphi_{\mathrm{CEP}}$ around 160$^\circ$. This implies that CEP-dependent macroscopic effects influence in a non-trivial way the spectral content of the individual attosecond pulses. 

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[scale=0.3]{Images/Results/Simulation/SFA_1d_vs_3d.pdf}
    \caption{Photoionization spectrograms as a function of the kinetic energy of the electrons and of the XUV-NIR delay simulated using (A and B) the full model and (C and D) the 1D analytical model. For the 3D model, a CEP of (A) 80° and (B) 160° was used. For the 1D model, a CEP of (C) 40° and (D) 120° was used. The relative CEP was chosen close to the experimental one.}
    \label{fig:full_vs_1D_model}
\end{figure}

To gain insight into the influence of macroscopic effects on the pulse train, we developed a one-dimensional model of time-dependent harmonic phase-matching, including CEP-dependent sub-cycle variations. The total phase mismatch $\Delta k(q,t)$ depends on the medium properties and on the laser parameters and can be written as \cite{Weissenbilder_2022}
\begin{equation}
    \Delta k(q,t) \! =\! \Delta k_{\mathrm{foc}}(q) \! + \! \Delta k_{\mathrm{at}}(q) \! + \! \Delta k_{\mathrm{dip}}(q,t) \! + \! \Delta k_{\mathrm{fe}}(q,t) 
\end{equation}
where $\Delta k_{\mathrm{foc}}(q) = - q / z_\mathrm{r}$ comes from the laser focusing (through the variation of the Gouy phase over the Rayleigh length $z_\mathrm{r}$), $\Delta k_{\mathrm{at}}(q)$ is due to the dispersion of the neutral medium, $\Delta k_{\mathrm{dip}}(q,t)$ comes from the variation of the dipole phase with the time-dependent intensity, $\Delta k_{\mathrm{fe}}(q,t)\propto - q \rho\eta_{\mathrm{fe}}(t)$ is due to the free electrons and depends on the time-dependent ionization degree $\eta_{\mathrm{fe}}(t)$. The ionization degree is often estimated using a cycle-averaged approximation \cite{Weissenbilder_2022}. Here, we account for the sub-cycle variation of the ionization degree, allowing us to include CEP effects. In addition, we neglect the CEP-slip across the focus as the medium length is small compared to the Rayleigh length. Figure \ref{fig:dk_WD_1d}A and B shows the temporal variation of $\Delta k(q,t)$ for four harmonic orders and CEPs of 40$^\circ$ and 120$^\circ$. $\Delta k(q,t)$ strongly varies both with time and CEP and differently for the different orders. Perfect phase-matching ($\Delta k =0$) is in general achieved only during a short time, less than one laser period, leading to temporal confinement of the harmonic emission. This time interval, in the conditions examined in the present work, moves to a later time as the harmonic order increases. 

For on-axis propagation in a uniform gas density, the intensity of the $q$th harmonic field at the exit of the medium can be expressed as \cite{Constant_1999,Ruchon_et_al}
\begin{equation}
    I(q,t)\! \propto \! [\rho d(q,t)]^2  \frac{\cosh{\left[ \kappa_q L \right]} - \cos{\! \left[ \Delta k(q,t)L \right ] }  }{\kappa_q^2 \!+\! \Delta k^2(q,t)} e^{-\kappa_qL},\!
\end{equation}
where $d(q,t)$ is the dipole moment, $\rho$ is the atomic gas density, $L$ is the medium length and $\kappa_q$ is the absorption coefficient. We then derive the attosecond pulse train $\mathscr{E}_{\mathrm{APT}}(t)$ as the sum of each harmonic  component \cite{Mairesse_Science, Varju_JMO,Varju_PRL}
\begin{equation}
    \mathscr{E}_{\mathrm{APT}}(t)= \sum_{q=q_i}^{q=q_f} \sqrt{I(q,t)} e^{ -i[ q\omega t - \Phi_\mathrm{dip}(q,t) ] },
\end{equation}
where $\Phi_\mathrm{dip}(q,t)$ is the dipole phase calculated using the semi-classical three-step model \cite{kulander_proceeding,single_atom_Corkum}. Finally, the obtained APTs are used to simulate photoelectron spectra as previously. Figure \ref{fig:full_vs_1D_model}C and D presents results obtained at two CEP values separated by 80$^\circ$. The experimental observations, with alternation between chessboard and oscillation patterns, are reproduced by this simple model, which captures the essential physics of the interference between EWPs. 

To further investigate the interference mechanism, we calculate the Wigner-Distribution (WD) of the APTs, given by:
\begin{equation}
    W(t,\Omega) =\! \int_{-\infty}^{+\infty}\!\! \! \mathscr{E}_{\mathrm{APT}}\left(t+\tfrac{\tau}{2}\right)  \mathscr{E}^*_{\mathrm{APT}}\left(t-\tfrac{\tau}{2}\right) e^{-i \Omega \tau} \,d\tau,
    \label{eq:WD}
\end{equation}
where $\Omega$ is the XUV frequency. The simultaneous spectral and temporal resolution of the WD is only limited by the uncertainty principle. Figure \ref{fig:dk_WD_1d}C and D, obtained at the same CEPs as Figure \ref{fig:full_vs_1D_model}C and D shows mainly three attosecond pulses (denoted $m=1,2,3$) that vary in strength with energy and that are tilted, mainly due to the attosecond chirp (attochirp) \cite{Varju_JMO, Mairesse_Science}. Each pulse has a distinct spectral content, as expected from HHG with few-cycle pulses \cite{Hammond_2016,ChenGuo_model}. In both cases, the $m=2$ pulse has the highest cut-off energy. In C, the main two pulses are $m=2$ and $m=3$. At low energies, the APT consists of three pulses separated by approximately the same time. However, above 35 eV, the first attosecond pulse vanishes. Consequently, the photoelectron spectrum changes from a RABBIT-like pattern (interference of multiple temporal slits) at low energies to an oscillation pattern (interference of two temporal slits) at high energies. In D, the main two pulses are now $m=1$ and $m=2$. The third pulse $m=3$ is delayed relative to $m=2$ by $T_\mathrm{IR}/2+\delta T(\Omega)$, where $T_\mathrm{IR}$ is the laser period. The additional delay $\delta T(\Omega)$ is due to the chirp of the harmonics, called femtochirp, which increases with the harmonic order \cite{Varju_JMO}. 

\begin{figure}[ht]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[scale=0.27]{Images/Results/Simulation/dktot_WD_CEP_40_CEP_120_deg.pdf}
    \caption{(A and B) Total phase mismatch as a function of time in units of the laser period ($T_\mathrm{IR}$) for four harmonic orders $q$ and the CEP values of Figure \ref{fig:full_vs_1D_model}A and B. The horizontal black dashed line corresponds to perfect phase-matching. (C and D) Wigner-distribution of the APT.}
    \label{fig:dk_WD_1d}
\end{figure}

This study shows that the photoelectron spectra in laser-assisted photoionization with short pulses strongly depends on the composition of the APTs, which is influenced by phase-matching and the temporal chirp of the emitted radiation. To verify the conclusion from the above analysis (Figure \ref{fig:dk_WD_1d}), we construct an artificial APT consisting of either (i) three attosecond pulses, (ii) two attosecond pulses and (iii) three attosecond pulses, of which the third one is weaker and delayed relative to the second pulse by an amount larger than the time difference between the first two pulses, depending on the spectral range. Figure \ref{fig:interference_model}A and B shows the photoelectron pattern obtained using these artificial APTs. The composition chosen is indicated on the right of the figure. This simple model reproduces well the experimental observations (Figure \ref{fig:exp_PI}A and B). In particular the interference pattern shown in B, which could not be explained by the single-atom response, results from the interference of three EWPs at low and high energy, with oscillations in-between due to interference with two EWPs. We note that the RABBIT-like patterns at low kinetic energies are slightly different in the experiment because our APTs can consist of three pulses with different amplitudes or four pulses with two weaker satellite pulses.
\begin{figure}[ht]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[scale=0.25]{Images/Results/Simulation/interference_cartoon_.3_1._.4.pdf}
    \caption{(A and B) Interference pattern of EWPs produced by a combination of attosecond pulses in different spectral ranges. EWPs produced by (i) two attosecond pulses of similar amplitudes separated by $T_\mathrm{IR}/2$, (ii) three attosecond pulses separated by $T_\mathrm{IR}/2$ with the first and the last having the same amplitude and (iii) three attosecond pulses of different ratios where the last one is shifted by an additional 0.15 fs.}
    \label{fig:interference_model}
\end{figure}

\section{Conclusion}
In conclusion, laser-assisted photoionization spectra exhibit pronounced energy-dependent features originating from the interference of the EWPs in the time domain, which can be controlled by the CEP of the driving field. We found that the composition of the APTs is influenced by: the time-dependent phase matching of the harmonics and by the temporal properties (femtosecond chirp). The former alters the spectral amplitude of the harmonics at each laser half-cycle, thereby affecting in a non-trivial way the number of dominant attosecond pulses at different energies. The latter changes the separation between adjacent attosecond pulses at high energies, resulting in the distortion of the RABBIT-like patterns. Our study provides to our knowledge the first experimental insight into sub-cycle phase-matching for a short laser pulse and a small number of attosecond pulses. This enables a comprehensive understanding of laser-assisted photoionization spectra and opens new routes for temporally controlling the HHG process.

