\begin{abstract} We present a Stone  duality for bitopological spaces in analogy to the duality between Stone spaces and Boolean algebras, in the same vein as the duality between d-sober bitopological spaces and spatial d-frames established by Jung and Moshier. Precisely, we introduce the notion of d-Boolean algebras and prove that the category of such algebras is dually equivalent to the category of Stone bitopological spaces, which are compact and zero-dimensional bitopological spaces satisfying the $T_0$ separation axiom. \vskip 2pt 

\noindent  {Keywords}: Bitopological space; d-Boolean algebra;  d-frame; Stone duality     

\noindent  {MSC(2020)}:   54E55, %(bitopologies) 
18F70, %(Frames and locales, pointfree topology, Stone duality)
06E75 %(generalizations of Boolean algebras)
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction} 
By topologizing the set of prime ideals of a Boolean algebra  Marshall Stone   \cite{Stone36,Stone37a} established a duality  between Boolean algebras and   compact and zero-dimensional Hausdorff spaces (which are now known as Stone spaces).  
$$ \bfig
\morphism(0,0)|a|/@{->}@<3pt>/<660,0>[{\bf Bool}^{\rm op}`{\bf Stone};\Spec ]
\morphism(0,0)|b|/@{<-}@<-3pt>/<660,0>[{\bf Bool}^{\rm op}`{\bf Stone}; \Clop] \efig$$
This duality has a far-reaching influence (see  \cite[Introduction]{Johnstone}) and has led to the discovery of many dualities in mathematics. One example is the duality 
$$\bfig
\morphism(0,0)|a|/@{->}@<3pt>/<700,0>[{\bf SpFrm}^{\rm op}`{\bf SobTop};\pt]
\morphism(0,0)|b|/@{<-}@<-3pt>/<700,0>[{\bf SpFrm}^{\rm op}`{\bf SobTop};\CO]  
\efig$$
between  spatial frames and sober topological spaces, see e.g. \cite{Johnstone,PP2012}.

The duality between Boolean algebras and Stone spaces is closely related to the duality between spatial frames and sober spaces. First, the duality between spatial frames and sober spaces cuts down to a duality between compact and zero-dimensional frames and Stone spaces. Second, sending each Boolean algebra to the frame of its ideals defines an equivalence between the category of Boolean algebras and the category {\bf KZFrm} of compact and zero-dimensional frames. This equivalence is a point-free version of the Stone representation of Boolean algebras. The duality between Boolean algebras and Stone spaces is the  composite of the duality between compact, zero-dimensional frames and Stone spaces and the equivalence between Boolean algebras and compact, zero-dimensional frames.
$$\bfig \morphism(0,0)|a|/@{->}@<3pt>/<600,0>[{\bf Bool}^{\rm op}`{\bf KZFrm}^{\rm op}; ] \morphism(0,0)|b|/@{<-}@<-3pt>/<600,0>[{\bf Bool}^{\rm op}`{\bf KZFrm}^{\rm op};  ] \morphism(600,0)|a|/@{->}@<3pt>/<600,0>[{\bf KZFrm}^{\rm op}`{\bf Stone}; ] \morphism(600,0)|b|/@{<-}@<-3pt>/<600,0>[{\bf KZFrm}^{\rm op}`{\bf Stone};  ] \efig$$

The purpose of this paper is to establish an analogy of the duality between Boolean algebras and Stone spaces in the realm of bitopological spaces. 

In 2006 Jung and Moshier \cite{JM2006} introduced the category of d-frames and presented a duality between spatial d-frames and d-sober bitopological spaces. 
$$\bfig
\morphism(0,0)|a|/@{->}@<3pt>/<800,0>[{\bf SpdFrm}^{\rm op}`{\bf SobBiTop};\dpt]
\morphism(0,0)|b|/@{<-}@<-3pt>/<800,0>[{\bf SpdFrm}^{\rm op}`{\bf SobBiTop};\dO] 
\efig$$
The relationship between d-frames and bitopological spaces is parallel to that between frames and topological spaces, so d-frames are `algebraic duals' of bitopological spaces \cite{JM2006,Jakl}.  

The duality we wish to establish is in the same vein as the duality of Jung and Moshier. Precisely, we wish to present a dual equivalence $$\bfig
\morphism(0,0)|a|/@{->}@<3pt>/<750,0>[{\bf dBool}^{\rm op}`{\bf BiStone};\dSpec ]
\morphism(0,0)|b|/@{<-}@<-3pt>/<750,0>[{\bf dBool}^{\rm op}`{\bf BiStone}; \dClop] \efig$$ between a category  that is an analogue of the category of Boolean algebras and a category that is an analogue of the category of Stone spaces. For {\bf BiStone} we take the category of Stone bitopological spaces (see Section \ref{Stone bitopological spaces} for definition), these spaces are called pairwise Stone spaces in \cite{BBGK}. Our main task is to find the category {\bf dBool}, of which the objects will be called d-Boolean algebras. In a nutshell,  d-Boolean algebras are to bitopological spaces what Boolean algebras  are to topological spaces. In particular, the duality between d-Boolean algebras and Stone bitopological spaces is obtained by making the set of prime d-ideals of a d-Boolean algebra into a bitopological space as in the classical context. 

The duality concerned in this paper is also related to topological representation of  distributive lattices. In 1937, Stone \cite{Stone37b} proved that the category {\bf DisLat} of distributive lattices is dually equivalent to the category {\bf CohSp} of coherent spaces and coherent maps (see \cite[page 66]{Johnstone}). In 1970, Priestley \cite{Priestley70,Priestley72} proved that {\bf DisLat} is dually equivalent to the category {\bf Pries} of Priestley spaces  (also known as ordered Stone spaces) and order-preserving continuous maps (see \cite[page 75]{Johnstone}). In 2010,  G. \& N. Bezhanishvili,   Gabelaia  and Kurz \cite{BBGK} proved that {\bf DisLat} is dually equivalent to the category {\bf BiStone} of Stone bitopological spaces (called pairwise Stone spaces therein) and continuous maps. Thus, the category {\bf CohSp} of coherent spaces, the category {\bf Pries} of Priestley spaces and the category {\bf BiStone} of Stone bitopological spaces are equivalent to each other, all of them have a topological nature. Are there natural categories of an algebraic nature that are equivalent to the category of distributive lattices? The category of d-Boolean algebras provides such an example. 

The contents are arranged as follows. In Section 2 we briefly review the duality between Boolean algebras and Stone spaces. In Section 3 we recall some basic concepts of bitopological spaces and introduce the notion of Stone bitopological spaces. In Section 4 we introduce the central notion of this paper, that of d-Boolean algebras. Structures of such algebras are examined; in particular, it is proved that the category of d-Boolean algebras is equivalent to the category of compact and zero-dimensional d-frames (Theorem  \ref{dbool vs dfrm}). In Section 5 we introduce the notion of prime d-ideals for d-Boolean algebras and make the set of all prime d-ideals of a d-Boolean algebra into a bitopological space, called the spectrum of the d-Boolean algebra. We prove  that the spectrum of each d-Boolean algebra is a Stone bitopological space and every Stone bitopological space is the spectrum of a unique (up to isomorphism) d-Boolean algebra, arriving at the desired duality.  

\section{Review of the Stone representation of Boolean algebras}  
In this section, we briefly review the duality between Boolean algebras and Stone spaces established by Stone \cite{Stone36,Stone37a}, for comprehensive accounts of the story we refer to the monograph of Johnstone   \cite{Johnstone}, and the monograph of Gehrke and van Gool \cite{GG2024}. Our reference for category theory is \cite{AHS}, for domain theory is \cite{Gierz2003}, and for general topology is \cite{Engelking}.

Throughout this paper, if not otherwise specified,  $L$ always denotes a bounded distributive lattice $(L,\leq,0,1)$. 
An element $a$ of $L$ is \emph{complemented} if there is some $b\in L$ such that $a\vee b=1$ and $a\wedge b=0$. Such $b$,  when exists, is necessarily unique and is called the \emph{complement} of $a$. A Boolean algebra is a distributive lattice of which all elements are complemented.  

The clopen (closed and open) sets of a topological space form a Boolean algebra. Sending each topological space to this algebra gives rise to a contravariant functor  $$\Clop\colon{\bf Top}\longrightarrow{\bf Bool}^{\rm op} $$ from the category {\bf Top} of topological spaces and continuous maps to the category {\bf Bool} of Boolean algebras and lattice homomorphisms.  The celebrated Stone representation of Boolean algebras says that restricting the domain of the functor $\Clop$ to the subcategory of Stone spaces yields an equivalence of categories. To see this we need several notions.

A \emph{Stone space} \cite[page 70]{Johnstone} is a compact topological space $X$ that satisfies one, hence all, of the following equivalent conditions:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\rm(\roman*)] \setlength{\itemsep}{0pt} 
\item $X$ is $T_0$ and zero-dimensional in the sense that clopen  sets of $X$ form a base for the topology.
\item $X$ is totally separated in the sense that whenever $x$ and $y$ are distinct points of $X$, there is a clopen  set of $X$ containing $x$ but not $y$.
\item $X$ is Hausdorff and totally disconnected in the sense that  connected subsets of $X$ are single points.
\end{enumerate} The full subcategory of {\bf Top} composed of Stone spaces is denoted by {\bf Stone}. 

An \emph{ideal} of a distributive lattice $L$ is a non-empty subset $I$ of $L$ such that $a\in I$ and $b\leq a$ implies $b\in I$, and that $a,b\in I$ implies $a\vee b\in I$. The set of all ideals of $L$ is denoted by $\idl L$.  For an ideal $I$ of $L$, we say that \begin{itemize} \setlength{\itemsep}{0pt} \item $I$ is \emph{proper} if $1\notin I$. \item  $I$ is \emph{prime} if it is proper and  $a\wedge b\in I\implies a\in I$ or $ b\in I$. \end{itemize}

The notions \emph{filter}, \emph{proper filter} and \emph{prime filter} are defined dually. In any distributive lattice, the complement of a prime ideal is a prime filter and vice versa.

For each Boolean algebra $L$, let $\Spec L$ be the set of all prime ideals of $L$. The collection  of subsets 
$$\Phi(I)=\{J\in\Spec L:I\not\subseteq J\},\quad I\in\idl L $$ is a topology on $\Spec L$, the resulting topological space is called the  \emph{spectrum}  of $L$. The spectrum of each Boolean algebra is a Stone space, so we obtain a contravariant functor  $$\Spec\colon{\bf Bool}^{\rm op}\longrightarrow{\bf Stone}.$$ The functors $\Clop\colon{\bf Stone}\longrightarrow{\bf Bool}^{\rm op} $ and $\Spec\colon{\bf Bool}^{\rm op}\longrightarrow{\bf Stone}$ witness that the category of Boolean algebras is dually equivalent to the category of Stone spaces.   

\section{Stone bitopological spaces} \label{Stone bitopological spaces}
A \emph{bitopological space} \cite{Kelly} is a triple $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$, where $X$ is a set, $\tau_+$ and $\tau_-$ are topologies on $X$. A  \emph{continuous}  (also called bicontinuous in the literature) map
$$f\colon (X,\tau_+,\tau_-) \longrightarrow (X',\tau_+',\tau_-')$$ is a map $f\colon X\longrightarrow X'$ such that both $f\colon (X,\tau_+)\longrightarrow (X',\tau_+')$ and $f\colon (X,\tau_-)\longrightarrow (X',\tau_-')$ are continuous. The category of bitopological spaces and continuous maps is denoted by  $${\bf BiTop}.$$ 

The assignment $(X,\CT)\mapsto (X,\CT,\CT)$ defines a full and faithful functor $$\omega\colon {\bf Top}\lra{\bf BiTop}.$$ The functor $\omega$ embeds the category of topological spaces as a simultaneously reflective and coreflective full subcategory of the category of bitopological spaces; the {\bf Top}-coreflection of a bitopological space $(X,\tau_+, \tau_-)$ is given by the topological space $(X,\tau_+\vee\tau_-)$ \cite{HZ2025}.  

\begin{defn}\label{compact bitop}
A bitopological space $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$ is
\begin{enumerate}[label=\rm(\roman*)] \setlength{\itemsep}{0pt}
\item (\cite{Mu})  $T_0$ if for each pair of distinct points of $X$, there is a $\tau_+$-open set or a
$\tau_-$-open set containing one of the points, but not the other.

%\item (\cite{Reilly72})  pairwise $T_1$  if  for each pair of distinct points $x$ and $y$, there exist  $U\in \tau_+$ and $V\in \tau_-$   such that $x\in U$, $y\notin U$ and $y\in V$, $x\notin V$.

%\item (\cite{Kelly})  pairwise Hausdorff  if  for each pair of distinct points $x$ and $y$, there is a $\tau_+$ neighborhood $U$ of $x$ and a $\tau_-$ neighborhood $V$ of $y$ such that  $U\cap V=\emptyset$.  

 \item (\cite{Swart}) \label{join compactness}  compact if every subset of $\tau_+\cup\tau_-$  covering $X$ has a finite subset that covers $X$.

\item \label{zero-dimensionality} (\cite{Reilly}) zero-dimensional (called pairwise zero-dimensional in \cite{Reilly}) if $\tau_{+}$ has a base of $\tau_{-}$ closed sets and $\tau_{-}$ has a base of $\tau_{+}$ closed sets. 
\end{enumerate}
\end{defn}

Let $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$  be a bitopological space. Then, $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$ is $T_0$  if and only if it is join $T_0$ \cite{Lal} in the sense that the topological space $(X,\tau_{+}\vee\tau_{-})$ is $T_0$;  and, under the assumption of the Axiom of Choice,  $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$ is compact if and only if it is join compact in the sense that the topological space $(X, \tau_{+}\vee \tau_{-})$ is compact.  

For each topological space $X$, the bitopological space $\omega(X)$ is $T_0$,   compact, and zero-dimensional if and only if so is $X$  (as a topological space),  respectively.

For each bitopological space $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$, we write $\leq_+$ and $\leq_-$ for the specialization order of the topological spaces $(X,\tau_+)$ and $(X,\tau_-)$, respectively;  and write $\leq$ for the intersection of $\leq_+$ and the opposite of $\leq_-$; that is, $\leq\thinspace = \thinspace\leq_+\cap \geq_-.$ 

\begin{defn}\label{order separated}  (\cite[Definition 3.7] {JM2008})
A  bitopological space $(X;\tau_{+},\tau_{-})$ is order-separated provided that the binary relation $\leq$ (which is $\leq_+\cap \geq_-$) is a partial order, and  that if $x\not\leq  y$ then there exist a $\tau_+$-neighborhood $U$ of $x$ and a $\tau_-$-neighborhood $V$ of $y$ such that  $U\cap V=\emptyset$.   
\end{defn}

For each topological space $X$, the bitopological space $\omega(X)$ is order-separated if and only if  $X$ is Hausdorff. Lemma 3.8 of Jung and Moshier \cite{JM2008} says that in an order-separated bitopological space $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$, the specialization order $\leq_+$ of $(X,\tau_+)$ is dual to the specialization order $\leq_-$ of  $(X,\tau_-)$, i.e., $\leq_+\thinspace  = \thinspace \geq_-$, hence $\leq\thinspace=\thinspace\leq_+= \thinspace \geq_-$.

\begin{defn}
A  bitopological space $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$ is totally order-separated provided that the binary relation $\leq$  is a partial order, and that if $x\not\leq y$ then there is a $\tau_+$-open  and $\tau_-$-closed set  containing $x$ but not $y$.\footnote{If a bitopological space $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$ is totally order-separated, then the  ordered topological space  $(X, \tau_+\vee\tau_-, \leq)$ is  \emph{totally order-separated}  in the sense of \cite[page 74]{Johnstone}.} 
\end{defn}

A totally order-separated bitopological space is clearly order-separated. For each topological space $X$, the bitopological space $\omega(X)$ is totally order-separated if and only if  $X$ is totally separated in the sense of \cite[page 69]{Johnstone}.


\begin{prop}\label{Stone bitopological space}
For each bitopological space $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$, the following  are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\rm(\arabic*)] \setlength{\itemsep}{0pt} 
\item $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$ is  $T_0$, compact and zero-dimensional. 
\item $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$ is compact and totally order-separated.
\end{enumerate}
\end{prop}

\begin{proof}
$(1)\Rightarrow(2)$ We proceed with two steps.

{\bf Step 1}.   $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$ is order-separated. 

First  we show that $\leq_+  =\thinspace \geq_-$. This equality is the content of Lemma 3.3 in \cite{BBGK}. We include a proof here for convenience of the reader. If $x\nleq_+y$,  by zero-dimensionality there is a $\tau_+$-open  and $\tau_-$-closed set $U$ containing $x$ but not $y$. Then $X\setminus U$ is a $\tau_-$-open set containing $y$ but not $x$, from which one infers that $y\nleq_- x$. Likewise, $y\nleq_-x$ implies  $x\nleq_+y$. Therefore $\leq_+  =\thinspace  \geq_-$. 

Next we show that $\leq$ is a partial order. For this we show that  $\leq_+$ is a partial order. Let $x,y$ be a pair of distinct points of $X$. Since   $(X,\tau_{+}, \tau_{-})$ is $T_0$, there is $U\in\tau_+\cup\tau_-$ that contains one of the points, but not the other, so, either $x\nleq_+ y$, or  $y\nleq_+ x$, or  $x\nleq_- y$, or  $y\nleq_- x$. Since $\leq_+  =\thinspace \geq_-$, then either $x\nleq_+ y$ or  $y\nleq_+ x$, hence $\leq_+$ is a partial order.

Now we show that if $x\nleq y$ then there exist a $\tau_+$-neighborhood $U$ of $x$ and a $\tau_-$-neighborhood $V$ of $y$ such that  $U\cap V=\emptyset$.  Since $\leq\thinspace =\thinspace \leq_+$,  there is a $\tau_+$-open set $W$ containing $x$ but not $y$. By zero-dimensionality there is a $\tau_+$-open  and $\tau_-$-closed set $U$ such that $x\in U\subseteq W$. Then $U$ is a $\tau_+$-neighborhood  of $x$  and $V\coloneqq X\setminus U$ is a $\tau_-$-neighborhood of $y$ such that  $U\cap V=\emptyset$.

{\bf Step 2}.  $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$ is totally order-separated. 

It suffices to check that if $x\not\leq y$, then there is a $\tau_+$-open  and $\tau_-$-closed set containing $x$ but not $y$.  Since $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$ is order-separated, there is a $\tau_+$
open set $U$ containing $x$ but not $y$. Since $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$ is zero-dimensional, there is a $\tau_+$-open  and $\tau_-$-closed set $V$ such that $x\in V\subseteq U$. Thus $V$ is a $\tau_+$-open  and $\tau_-$-closed set containing $x$ but not $y$. 

$(2)\Rightarrow(1)$   It suffices to show that $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$ is zero-dimensional. Let $x\in X$ and $U$ be a $\tau_+$-open set containing $x$. We find a $\tau_+$-open and  $\tau_-$-closed set $V$ such that $x\in V\subseteq U.$ 
For each $y\in X\setminus U$, we have $x\nleq y$, so there is a $\tau_+$-open  and $\tau_-$-closed set $U_y$ containing $x$ but not $y$. Choosing one such $U_y$ for each $y\in X\setminus U$ we obtain a $\tau_-$-open cover $\{X\setminus U_y:y\in X\setminus U\}$ of $X\setminus U$. By  compactness of $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$, $X\setminus U$ can be covered by finitely many elements of $\{X\setminus U_y:y\in X\setminus U\}$, say, $X\setminus U_1, X\setminus U_2,\cdots, X\setminus U_n$. Let $V=\bigcap_{i\leq n}  U_i$.
Then $V$ satisfies the requirement. This shows that $\tau_+$ has a base of $\tau_-$-closed sets.
Likewise, $\tau_-$ has a base of $\tau_+$-closed sets. Therefore  $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$ is zero-dimensional.
\end{proof} 

It is clear that in a $T_0$ and zero-dimensional  bitopological space,  connected sets (in the sense of Pervin \cite{Pervin}) are single points. We do not know whether the requirement being zero-dimensional in Proposition \ref{Stone bitopological space}\thinspace(1) can be weakened to that connected sets are single points.

A bitopological space satisfying the equivalent conditions of Proposition \ref{Stone bitopological space} is called a  \emph{Stone bitopological space}. The category of Stone bitopological spaces and continuous maps is denoted by
$$\bf{BiStone}.$$ A topological space $X$ is a Stone space   if and only if $\omega(X)$ is a Stone bitopological space. So restricting the domain and codomain of the functor $\omega\colon {\bf Top}\lra{\bf BiTop}$ yields a functor $\omega\colon {\bf Stone}\lra{\bf BiStone}$. 

\begin{rem} The argument of Proposition \ref{Stone bitopological space} shows that if $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$ is a Stone bitopological space, then it is bi-$T_0$ in the sense that both $(X,\tau_+)$ and $(X, \tau_-)$ are $T_0$ topological spaces. So, by Lemma 2.5 of \cite{BBGK} one sees that Stone bitopological spaces are precisely  \emph{pairwise Stone spaces} in  \cite[Definition 2.10]{BBGK}. \end{rem}

\section{d-Boolean algebras}

Suppose   $(L,\leq,0,1)$ is a bounded distributive lattice; $\dbt$ and $\dbf$ are a complementary pair of elements, that means, $\dbt\vee\dbf=1$ and $\dbt\wedge\dbf=0$. The  assignment $$a\mapsto (a\wedge\dbt,a\wedge\dbf)$$ is then an order isomorphism from $L$ to the product lattice $L_+\times L_-$, where  $L_+= [0,\dbt]$ is the interval of $L$ consisting of elements between $0$ and $\dbt$, $L_-= [0,\dbf]$ is the interval of $L$ consisting of elements between $0$ and $\dbf$. The inverse  isomorphism takes each element $(a,b)$ of $L_+\times L_-$ to the join $a\vee b$ in $(L,\leq)$. In this paper we always identify $L$ with $L_+\times L_-$; in particular, $1$ with $(\dbt,\dbf)$, $0$ with $(0,0)$, $\dbt$ with $(\dbt,0)$ and $\dbf$ with $(0,\dbf)$.

Now we use the decomposition   $L\cong L_+\times L_-$ to define a new order $\sqsubseteq$ on $L$: $$(a_1,b_1)\sqsubseteq (a_2,b_2)\iff a_1\leq a_2,~b_1\geq b_2.$$ Then $(L,\sqsubseteq)$ is a distributive lattice with $\dbt$ as top element and $\dbf$ as bottom element. The meet $x\sqcap y$ and the join $x\sqcup y$ in $(L,\sqsubseteq)$ can be computed in terms of the meet and join operations of $(L,\leq)$ as follows:
$$x\sqcap y=(x\wedge \dbf)\vee (y\wedge \dbf)\vee (x\wedge y);$$
$$x\sqcup y=(x\wedge \dbt)\vee(y\wedge \dbt)\vee(x\wedge y).$$ The lattice structure $(L,\sqcap,\sqcup)$ is already known in  \cite[page 751]{BK47}. Following Jung and Moshier \cite{JM2006, JM2008} we call $\leq$ and $\sqsubseteq$ the \emph{information order} and the \emph{logic order} of $(L;\dbt,\dbf)$, respectively.\footnote{We have flipped notations from   \cite{JM2006,JM2008}, the symbols $\sqsubseteq,\sqcap$ and $\sqcup$, instead of $\leq,\wedge$ and $\vee$, are used for the logic order here.} 

If $\{\dbt,\dbf\}=\{1,0\}$, it is easily seen that the logic order $\sqsubseteq$ coincides with the order $\leq$ or its opposite, so, in order to avoid degeneracy, in this paper we always assume that the complementary pair $\{\dbt,\dbf\}$ is not $\{1,0\}$.

Let $\mathbb{B}$ be the Boolean algebra $\{0,1,\dbt,\dbf\}$, with $0$ being the bottom element, $1$ being the top element, $\dbt$ and $\dbf$ being complement of each other, as visualized below: \[\bfig \morphism(0,0)/@{-}/<-250,-250>[1`\dbt;]  \morphism(0,0)/@{-}/<250,-250>[1`\dbf;] \morphism(-250,-250)/@{-}/<250,-250>[\dbt`0;]  \morphism(250,-250)/@{-}/<-250,-250>[\dbf`0;] \efig\] With the logic order $\mathbb{B}$ becomes \[\bfig \morphism(0,0)/@{-}/<-250,-250>[\dbt`1;]  \morphism(0,0)/@{-}/<250,-250>[\dbt`0;] \morphism(-250,-250)/@{-}/<250,-250>[1`\dbf;]  \morphism(250,-250)/@{-}/<-250,-250>[0`\dbf;] \efig\]

\begin{defn}
A d-lattice  is a structure $(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$, where  $L$ is a bounded distributive lattice, $\dbt$ and $\dbf$ are a complementary  pair of elements of $L$,  {\bf con}  and {\bf tot}  are subsets of $L$ (called the  consistency predicate  and the  totality  predicate, respectively), subject to the following conditions:
\begin{itemize} \setlength{\itemsep}{0pt}		
\item ({\bf con}--$\dbt,\dbf$) $\dbt,\dbf\in{\bf con}$; 

\item ({\bf tot}--$\dbt,\dbf$) $\dbt,\dbf\in{\bf tot}$;

\item {\bf con} is a Scott closed set and {\bf tot} is an upper set of $(L,\leq)$; %that is, 

%({\bf con}--$\downarrow$) $\alpha\leq\beta$ \& $\beta\in{\bf con}\implies \alpha\in{\bf con}$

%({\bf con}--$\bigvee^\uparrow$) For each  directed subset  $D$ of {\bf con}  w.r.t. the information order  $\leq$,   $\bigvee  D\in{\bf con}$ whenever $\bigvee D$ exists 

%({\bf tot}--$\uparrow$) $\alpha\leq\beta$ \& $\alpha\in{\bf tot}\implies \beta\in{\bf tot}$;

\item   {\bf con} and {\bf tot} are sublattices of $(L,\sqsubseteq)$; %that is, 

%({\bf con}--$\sqcap,\sqcup$) $\alpha,\beta\in{\bf con}\implies \alpha\sqcap\beta,\alpha\sqcup\beta\in{\bf con}$ 

%({\bf tot}--$\sqcap,\sqcup$) $\alpha,\beta\in{\bf tot}\implies \alpha\sqcap\beta,\alpha\sqcup\beta\in{\bf tot}$;

\item ({\bf con}--{\bf tot}) $\alpha\in{\bf con}$, $\beta\in{\bf tot}$,  $(\alpha\wedge\dbt=\beta\wedge\dbt\text{ or }\alpha\wedge\dbf= \beta\wedge\dbf)\implies \alpha\leq\beta$.
\end{itemize} 
\end{defn}

\begin{rem} A notion of d-lattices has been introduced in \cite{KJM}. The difference is that the consistency predicate {\bf con} of a d-lattice  in \cite{KJM} is not required to be closed under directed joins. The above  postulation  is a direct extension of that of (reasonable) d-frames in \cite{JM2006,JM2008} (see Definition \ref{defn of d-frame} below). \end{rem}

\begin{exmp} \label{B as d-lattice} There is a unique way to make the Boolean algebra $\mathbb{B}=\{0,1,\dbt,\dbf\}$ into a d-lattice; that is, ${\bf con}=\{0,\dbt,\dbf\}$ and ${\bf tot}=\{1,\dbt,\dbf\}$. In this paper we always view $\mathbb{B}$ as a d-lattice (in this way). \end{exmp}

Let $\mathcal{L}=(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$ and $\mathcal{M}=(M;\dbt_{M},\dbf_{M};{\bf con}_{M},{\bf tot}_{M})$ be d-lattices. A d-lattice homomorphism
$f\colon\mathcal{L}\longrightarrow\mathcal{M}$
is a lattice homomorphism $f\colon L\longrightarrow M$ that preserves $\dbt$, $\dbf$, ${\bf con}$ and ${\bf tot}$ in the sense that
$$f(\dbt)=\dbt_{M},\quad f(\dbf)=\dbf_{M},\quad f({\bf con})\subseteq{\bf con}_{M},\quad f({\bf tot})\subseteq{\bf tot}_{M}.$$
The category of d-lattices and d-lattice homomorphisms is denoted by  $$\dLat.$$ 

%Let $(L,\leq,0,1)$ be a bounded distributive lattice and $\dbt,\dbf$ be a complementary pair of elements. Then, $(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$  is a d-lattice, where   ${\bf con}=\thinspace\downarrow\!\dbt\thinspace\cup\! \downarrow\!\dbf$ and ${\bf tot}=\thinspace\uparrow\!\dbt\thinspace\cup\! \uparrow\!\dbf$.   

Let $L$ be a distributive lattice and $a\in L$. We say that $a$ is \emph{pseudo-complemented} if there is an element $\neg a$ of $L$ (which is necessarily unique when exists) such that for all $b\in L$, $a\wedge b=0$ if and only if $b\leq \neg a$. Every complemented element is  pseudo-complemented, but not conversely. Every element of a Heyting algebra is pseudo-complemented.

\begin{prop}\label{omegaH} Let $H$ be a distributive lattice of which every element is  pseudo-complemented. Then the structure  $\omega(H)\coloneqq(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$  is a d-lattice, where  \begin{itemize} \setlength{\itemsep}{0pt}
\item   $L_+=H$, $L_-=H$;   \item $\dbt=(1,0)$, $\dbf=(0,1)$; \item ${\bf con}=\{(a,b)\in H\times H: a\wedge b=0\}$;  \item ${\bf tot}=\{(a,b)\in H\times H: a\vee b=1\}$. \end{itemize} 
\end{prop}

\begin{proof} We only need to check the ({\bf con}--{\bf tot}) condition and that {\bf con} is Scott closed. 

To see ({\bf con}--{\bf tot}), without loss of generality we assume that $(a_1,b_1)\in{\bf con}$, $(a_2,b_2)\in{\bf tot}$, and that $(a_1,b_1)\wedge(1,0)=(a_2,b_2)\wedge(1,0)$. Then, $a_1=a_2$ and $$b_1=b_1\wedge(a_2\vee b_2)= (b_1\wedge a_1)\vee (b_1\wedge b_2)=b_1\wedge b_2,$$  so $b_1\leq b_2$ and $(a_1,b_1)\leq(a_2,b_2)$.

Since {\bf con} is clearly a lower set of $H\times H$, to see that it is Scott closed we check that {\bf con} is closed under directed joins.  
By definition, for all $a,b\in H$ it holds that $$a\leq\neg b\iff a\wedge b=0\iff b\leq\neg a.$$

Suppose  $\{(a_i,b_i)\}_{i\in D}$ is a directed subset of {\bf con} with a join $(a,b)$ in $H\times H$. First, we show that for each $j\in D$, $\neg a_j$ is an upper bound of $\{b_i\}_{i\in D}$, hence $b\leq\neg a_j$. For each $i\in D$, pick some $k\in D$ such that $(a_i,b_i) \leq (a_k,b_k)$ and $(a_j,b_j)\leq (a_k,b_k)$. Then  $b_i\leq b_k\leq \neg a_k \leq \neg a_j, $  which shows that $\neg a_j$ is an upper bound of $\{b_i\}_{i\in D}$ in $H$. Now we show that   $(a,b)\in{\bf con}$. Since $\neg a_i\geq b$ for all $i\in D$, it follows that $\neg b$ is an upper bound of $\{a_i\}_{i\in D}$ in $H$, then $a\leq \neg b$ and $(a,b)\in{\bf con}$. \end{proof}

The construction of the d-lattice $\omega(H)$ is a generalization of the construction of a d-frame out of a frame in Jung and Moshier \cite[page 47]{JM2006}. Consider the d-lattice $\omega(H)$. Then, $${\bf con}\cap{\bf tot}= \{(a,b)\in H\times H: a\vee b=1, a\wedge b=0\}.$$ That means, ${\bf con}\cap{\bf tot}$ is the set of all complementary pairs of $H$. Furthermore, if $B$ is the sublattice of $H$ composed of complemented elements, which is readily verified to be a Boolean algebra, then   ${\bf con}\cap{\bf tot}$ is equal to $\{(a,\neg a):a\in B\}$, which is an anti-chain (i.e., a discrete set) with respect to the information order of $H\times H$, and   isomorphic to the Boolean algebra $B$ with respect to the logic order. 

\begin{defn} (\cite{JM2006,JM2008}) \label{defn of d-frame}
A  d-frame   is a  d-lattice  $(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$  such that $L$ is a frame. \end{defn}
Actually, d-frames in the sense of the above definition are called \emph{reasonable d-frames} in Jung and Moshier \cite{JM2006,JM2008}. Following  \cite{Jakl,JJP} we simply call them d-frames. 
Let $\mathcal{L}=(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$ and $\mathcal{M}=(M;\dbt_{M},\dbf_{M};{\bf con}_{M},{\bf tot}_{M})$ be d-frames. By a d-frame homomorphism $g:\mathcal{L}\longrightarrow\mathcal{M}$ we mean  a frame homomorphism $g:L\longrightarrow M$ that preserves $\dbt$, $\dbf$, ${\bf con}$ and ${\bf tot}$ \cite{JM2006,JM2008}.
The category of d-frames and d-frame homomorphisms is denoted by  $$\bf{dFrm}.$$  

For each frame $L$, the d-lattice $\omega(L)$ in Proposition \ref{omegaH} is a d-frame. This gives rise to a full and faithful functor  $\omega\colon {\bf Frm}\lra{\bf dFrm}.$ 

 \begin{exmp}\label{dO}(\cite{JM2006,JM2008}) For each bitopological space $(X, \tau_+, \tau_-)$, the structure  $$\dO(X, \tau_+, \tau_-)\coloneqq (L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$$  is a d-frame, where  \begin{itemize} \setlength{\itemsep}{0pt}
 \item $L$ is the product frame $\tau_+\times\tau_-$; \item $\dbt=(X,\emptyset)$, $\dbf=(\emptyset,X)$; \item ${\bf con}=\{(U,V)\in \tau_+\times\tau_-: U\cap V=\emptyset\}$;   \item ${\bf tot}=\{(U,V)\in \tau_+\times\tau_-: U\cup V=X\}$. \end{itemize} 

In this way we obtain a contravariant functor $$\dO\colon {\bf BiTop}\lra {\bf dFrm}^{\rm op}$$  that makes the following square commutative: 
$$\bfig \square<650,450>[{\bf Top}`{\bf Frm}^{\rm op}`{\bf BiTop}`{\bf dFrm}^{\rm op};\CO `\omega`\omega`\dO ] \efig$$
\end{exmp}

In order to introduce the central notion of this paper, that of d-Boolean algebras, we  need the notion of d-complement in a d-lattice.
\begin{lem}\label{uniqueness of adag} Let $(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$ be a d-lattice. Then for each $a\in L_+$, there is at most one element $b$ of $L_-$ for which $(a,b)\in {\bf con}\cap {\bf tot}$. Likewise, for each $b\in L_-$, there is at most one element $a$ of $L_+$ for which $(a,b)\in {\bf con}\cap {\bf tot}$.   \end{lem} 

\begin{proof} Suppose   $b_1$ and $b_2$ are elements of $L_-$ such that both $(a,b_1)$ and $(a,b_2)$ belong to the intersection ${\bf con}\cap {\bf tot}$. Since $$(a,b_1\vee b_2)\wedge (\dbt,0)=(a,0)=(a,b_1\wedge b_2)\wedge (\dbt,0), $$ it follows from  ({\bf con}--{\bf tot}) that $b_1\vee b_2\leq b_1\wedge b_2$, hence $b_1=b_2$. \end{proof}

%For each d-lattice $(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$, let $${\bf cot}$$ be the intersection of  {\bf con} and {\bf tot}; that is, ${\bf cot}={\bf con} \cap {\bf tot}$. It is clear that {\bf cot} contains both $\dbt$ and $\dbf$ and is a sublattice of $(L,\sqsubseteq)$, where $\sqsubseteq$ is the logic order of $L$. 

\begin{prop} Let $(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$ be a d-lattice.  Then for any $(a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\in{\bf con} \cap {\bf tot}$, $a_1\leq a_2$ implies that $b_1\geq b_2$. Therefore, ${\bf con} \cap {\bf tot}$ is an anti-chain of $L$ under the information order $\leq$. \end{prop}

\begin{proof} Since ${\bf con} \cap {\bf tot}$ is a sublattice of $L$ with respect to the logic order $\sqsubseteq$, it follows that  $$(a_1\vee a_2, b_1\wedge b_2)=(a_1,b_1)\sqcup(a_2,b_2)\in{\bf con} \cap {\bf tot}.$$  If $a_1\leq a_2$, then $a_1\vee a_2=a_2$, hence $b_2= b_1\wedge b_2$ by Lemma \ref{uniqueness of adag}, which implies $b_1\geq b_2$. \end{proof}

Let $(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$ be a d-lattice.  We say that an element $x$ of $L$ is \emph{d-complemented} if either $x\in L_+$ and $(x,x^\dag)\in{\bf con} \cap {\bf tot}$ for some (necessarily unique)  $x^\dag\in L_-$, or $x\in L_-$ and $(x^\dag,x)\in{\bf con} \cap {\bf tot}$ for some (necessarily unique) $x^\dag\in L_+$. In this case $x^\dag$ is called a \emph{d-complement} of $x$.  In other words, an element of a d-lattice is d-complemented if and only if it is \emph{well-inside} itself in the sense of   \cite{JM2006,JM2008,KJM}.  


Since both $(\dbt,0)$ and $(0,\dbf)$ belong to ${\bf con}\cap{\bf tot}$, the bottom element $0$ (the only common element of $L_+$ and $L_-$) has two d-complements: one is $\dbf$ (when $0$ is viewed as an element of $L_+$), the other is $\dbt$ (when $0$ is viewed as an element of $L_-$). Each element other than $0$ has at most one d-complement. Both $\dbt$ and $\dbf$ are d-complemented: the d-complement of $\dbt$ is $0$, not $\dbf$; the d-complement of $\dbf$ is $0$, not $\dbt$.

\begin{defn} A   d-lattice  $(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$  is called a d-Boolean algebra  if  every element of $L_+\cup L_-$ is d-complemented. \end{defn} 

The full subcategory of $\dLat$ composed of d-Boolean algebras is denoted by $\dBoo.$ 
If $H$ is a Boolean algebra, then the d-lattice $\omega(H)$ in Proposition \ref{omegaH} is a d-Boolean algebra. So we obtain a full and faithful functor  $\omega\colon{\bf Bool}\lra{\bf dBool}.$  


Let $(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$ be a  d-Boolean algebra. Taking d-complement defines an order-reversing isomorphism $ ^\dagger\colon L_+\lra L_-$. It is readily verified that   $${\bf con}=\{(a,b)\in L_+\times L_-: a\leq b^\dag\}\quad \text{and} \quad {\bf tot}=\{(a,b)\in L_+\times L_-: a\geq b^\dag\}.$$ Therefore, the consistency predicate and the totality predicate of a d-Boolean algebra are determined by the order reversing isomorphism $ ^\dagger\colon L_+\lra L_-$. Conversely, we have: 

\begin{prop}\label{dbool via order reversing} Suppose $L_+,L_-$ are distributive lattices, $ ^\dag\colon L_+\lra L_-$ is an order-reversing isomorphism, the  inverse of $ ^\dag$ is also denoted by $ ^\dag$. Then  $(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$  is a d-Boolean algebra, where  \begin{itemize} \setlength{\itemsep}{0pt}
\item   $L=L_+\times L_-$; \item $\dbt=(1,0)$, $\dbf=(0,1)$; \item ${\bf con}=\{(a,b)\in L_+\times L_-: a\leq b^\dag\}$;   \item ${\bf tot}=\{(a,b)\in L_+\times L_-: a^\dag\leq b\}$. \end{itemize}  \end{prop}

\begin{proof} What we need to check is that ${\bf con}$ is closed under directed joins. Suppose  $\{(a_i,b_i)\}_{i\in D}$ is a directed subset of ${\bf con}$ with a join $(a,b)$ in $L_+\times L_-$. First, we show that each $a_j^\dag$ is an upper bound of $\{b_i\}_{i\in D}$, hence $b\leq a_j^\dag$. For each $i\in D$, pick some $k\in D$ such that $(a_i,b_i) \leq (a_k,b_k)$ and $(a_j,b_j)\leq (a_k,b_k)$. Then  $b_i\leq b_k\leq  a_k^\dag \leq  a_j^\dag, $  which shows that $ a_j^\dag$ is an upper bound of $\{b_i\}_{i\in D}$ in $L_-$. Now we show that   $(a,b)\in{\bf con}$. Since $a_i^\dag\geq b$ for all $i\in D$, it follows that $ b^\dag$ is an upper bound of $\{a_i\}_{i\in D}$ in $L_+$, then $a\leq  b^\dag$ and $(a,b)\in{\bf con}$. \end{proof}

\begin{exmp} Suppose $(X, \tau_+, \tau_-)$ is a bitopological space. Let  $$L_+=\{U\in\tau_+: X\setminus U\in\tau_-\},\quad L_-=\{V\in\tau_-: X\setminus V\in\tau_+\}.$$ Put differently, an element of $L_+$ is $\tau_+$-open and $\tau_-$-closed set; likewise for $L_-$. It is clear that for each subset $U$ of $X$, $ U\in L_+$ if and only if $X\setminus U\in L_-$, hence the correspondence $U\mapsto X\setminus U$ is an order-reversing isomorphism between $L_+$ and $L_-$. Therefore,  the structure $(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$  is a d-Boolean algebra, where  \begin{itemize}\setlength{\itemsep}{0pt}
\item $L=L_+\times L_-$; \item $\dbt=(X,\emptyset)$, $\dbf=(\emptyset,X)$; \item ${\bf con}=\{(U,V)\in L_+\times L_-: U\cap V=\emptyset\}$;   \item ${\bf tot}=\{(U,V)\in L_+\times L_-: U\cup V=X\}$. \end{itemize}  

We call $(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$   the \emph{d-Boolean algebra of d-clopen sets} of $(X, \tau_+, \tau_-)$. In this way we obtain a contravariant functor $$\dClop\colon{\bf BiTop}\longrightarrow{\bf dBool}^{\rm op}$$   that makes the following square commutative:
$$\bfig \square<650,450>[{\bf Top}`{\bf Bool}^{\rm op}`{\bf BiTop}`{\bf dBool}^{\rm op};\Clop `\omega`\omega`\dClop ] \efig$$ \end{exmp}

\begin{rem}\label{Stone-type} Make the Boolean algebra $\bbB=\{0,1,\dbt,\dbf\}$ into a bitopological space by considering as $\tau_+$ the topology generated by $\{\{\dbt,1\},\{0,\dbt\}\}$, and as $\tau_-$ the topology generated by $\{\{\dbf,1\},\{0,\dbf\}\}$. For each bitopological space $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$, it is readily verified that a map $f\colon X\lra \bbB$ is continuous if and only if \begin{itemize}\setlength{\itemsep}{0pt} \item the set $U\coloneqq\{x\in X:f(x)\geq\dbt\}$ is $\tau_+$-open and $\tau_-$-closed; and \item the set  $V\coloneqq\{x\in X:f(x)\geq\dbf\}$ is $\tau_-$-open and $\tau_+$-closed. \end{itemize} Conversely, if $U\subseteq X$ is   $\tau_+$-open and $\tau_-$-closed, $V\subseteq X$ is   $\tau_-$-open and $\tau_+$-closed, then the map $$f_{U,V}\colon X\lra\bbB,\quad f_{U,V}(x)=\begin{cases}1 &x\in U\cap V, \\ \dbt &x\in U\setminus V,\\ \dbf & x\in V\setminus U,\\ 0 &x\notin U\cup V\end{cases}$$ is continuous. The correspondence $(U,V)\mapsto f_{U,V}$ is a bijection, so elements of the d-Boolean algebra of d-clopen sets of $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$ are   continuous maps from $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$ to the bitopological space $\bbB$. \end{rem}

For each d-lattice  $(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$, let $$B_+=\{a\in L_+: a~\text{is d-complemented}\}, \quad B_-=\{b\in L_-: b~\text{is d-complemented}\}.$$ Then  $B_+$ is a sublattice of $L_+$, and $B_-$ is a sublattice  of $L_-$.  Assigning to each $a\in B_+$ the unique element $a^\dag$ of $L_-$ for which $(a,a^\dag)\in {\bf con} \cap {\bf tot}$  defines an order-reversing isomorphism between $B_+$ and  $B_-$.  Since for all $a\in B_+$ and $b\in B_-$, it holds that $$  (a,b)\in{\bf con}\iff  b\leq  a^\dag\iff (b^\dag,a^\dag)\in{\bf tot},$$   it follows from Proposition  \ref{dbool via order reversing} that if we let $$\dB L=B_+\times B_-,\quad {\bf con}_{\dB}={\bf con}\cap \dB L,\quad {\bf tot}_{\dB}={\bf tot}\cap \dB L,$$ then the structure $$\dB\mathcal{L}\coloneqq(\dB L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con}_{\dB},{\bf tot}_{\dB})$$ is a d-Boolean algebra, which is called the \emph{d-Boolean algebra of d-complemented elements} of $\mathcal{L}$. In this way we obtain a functor $$\dB\colon \dLat \longrightarrow \dBoo.$$

\begin{exmp} Let $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$ be a bitopological space. Then  a $\tau_+$-open set $U$ is d-complemented in the d-frame  $\dO(X, \tau_+, \tau_-)$   if and only if $U$ is $\tau_-$-closed.  Likewise, a $\tau_-$-open set $V$ is d-complemented in the d-frame  $\dO(X, \tau_+, \tau_-)$   if and only if $V$ is $\tau_+$-closed. Thus, the d-Boolean algebra composed of d-complemented elements of $\dO (X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$ coincides with the d-Boolean algebra of d-clopen sets of $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$. Said differently, $\dClop=\dB\circ\dO.$ \end{exmp} 

\begin{prop} The functor $\dB\colon \dLat \longrightarrow \dBoo$ is right adjoint to the inclusion functor $\dBoo\longrightarrow\dLat$, so 
the category  of d-Boolean algebras is a coreflective  subcategory of the category of d-lattices. \end{prop}

\begin{proof} We show that the $\dBoo$-coreflection of a d-lattice  $\mathcal{L}=(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$ is given by the d-Boolean algebra  $\dB\mathcal{L}\coloneqq(\dB L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con}_{\dB},{\bf tot}_{\dB})$. Since $B_+$ and $B_-$ are sublattices of $L_+$ and $L_-$, respectively, it follows that the inclusion map $i\colon B_+\times B_-\lra L_+\times L_-$ is a d-lattice homomorphism  $\dB\mathcal{L}\lra\mathcal{L}$. What remains is to check that   each d-lattice homomorphism $f\colon \mathcal{M}\lra\mathcal{L}$ with $\mathcal{M}= (M;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$ being a d-Boolean algebra factors through  $i\colon\dB\mathcal{L}\lra\mathcal{L}$. $$\bfig\ptriangle/->`<--`<-/[\dB\mathcal{L}`\mathcal{L}`\mathcal{M};i` `f]\efig$$
This follows directly from the fact that $f\colon M\lra L$ is a lattice homomorphism and that if $x\in M$ is d-completed then so is $f(x)$. \end{proof}

For each d-lattice $\mathcal{L}=(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$, let $${\bf con}_{\idl}=\{I\in\idl L: I\subseteq {\bf con}\},\quad {\bf tot}_{\idl}=\{I\in \idl L: I\cap {\bf tot}\neq\emptyset\}.$$ Then it is readily verified that 
$$\idl\mathcal{L}\coloneqq(\idl L;\downarrow\!\dbt,\downarrow\!\dbf;{\bf con}_{\idl},{\bf tot}_{\idl})$$
is a d-frame, called the \emph{d-frame of ideals} of the d-lattice $\mathcal{L}$.

For each d-lattice homomorphism $f\colon\mathcal{L}\longrightarrow\mathcal{M}$, the map
$$\idl L\longrightarrow\idl M,\quad I\mapsto f(I)=\{b\in M: b\leq f(a)\ \text{for some}\ a\in I\}$$
is a d-frame homomorphism $\idl\mathcal{L}\lra\idl\mathcal{M}$. These are the constituents of a functor
$$\idl\colon \dLat\longrightarrow\dFrm.$$ 

Since $L=L_+\times L_-$, every ideal $I$ of $L$ is the product   of an ideal $I_+$ of $L_+$ with an ideal $I_-$ of $L_-$. So we also write an ideal of $L$ as a pair $(I_+,I_-)$. Written in this way, we have $${\bf con}_{\idl}=\{(I_+,I_-): I_+\times I_- \subseteq {\bf con}\};$$
$${\bf tot}_{\idl}=\{(I_+,I_-): \exists a\in I_+,\exists b\in I_-, (a,b)\in {\bf tot}\}.$$

\begin{prop}\label{idl left adjoint to forgetful} The functor $\idl\colon \dLat\longrightarrow\dFrm$  is left adjoint to the  forgetful functor $\dFrm\longrightarrow\dLat$.  
\end{prop}

\begin{proof} For each d-lattice
$\mathcal{L} =(L;\dbt,\dbf; {\bf con},{\bf tot})$,  the assignment $a\mapsto \thinspace \downarrow\!a$  clearly defines a d-lattice homomorphism $\eta\colon \mathcal{L}\lra \idl\mathcal{L}$. So, it suffices to show that for each d-lattice homomorphism $f\colon\mathcal{L} \longrightarrow\mathcal{M}$ with $\mathcal{M}=(M;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$ being a d-frame, there is a unique d-frame homomorphism $\overline{f}\colon \idl\mathcal{L}\lra\mathcal{M}$ such that  $f= \overline{f}\circ \eta$.
$$\bfig\qtriangle/->`->`-->/[\mathcal{L}`\idl\mathcal{L}`\mathcal{M};\eta` f`\overline{f}]\efig$$

Define   $\overline{f}\colon\idl L\longrightarrow M$ by $\overline{f}(I)=\bigvee_{a\in I} f(a)$.  It is readily verified that $\overline{f}$ is the unique d-frame homomorphism $\idl\mathcal{L}\lra\mathcal{M}$   satisfying the requirement.
\end{proof}

\begin{prop}\label{one side of the duality} Each   d-Boolean algebra $\mathcal{L} =(L;\dbt,\dbf; {\bf con},{\bf tot})$   is isomorphic to the d-Boolean algebra of d-complemented elements of the d-frame of ideals of $\mathcal{L}$; that is,  $\mathcal{L}\cong\dB\circ\idl \mathcal{L} $. 
\end{prop}

\begin{proof} This follows immediately from the following lemma.
\end{proof}

\begin{lem}\label{d-complemented ideal} Let  
$\mathcal{L} =(L;\dbt,\dbf; {\bf con},{\bf tot})$ be a d-lattice. Then an ideal $I$ of the lattice $L$ is d-complemented in the d-frame of ideals of $\mathcal{L}$ if and only if $I= \thinspace \downarrow\!a$ for some d-complemented element $a$ of $\mathcal{L}$.\end{lem} 

\begin{proof} If $a$ is  d-complemented in the d-lattice $\mathcal{L}$ with d-complement $a^\dag$, it is readily verified that  $\downarrow\!a^\dag$ is a d-complement of $\downarrow\!a$ in the d-frame of ideals of   $\mathcal{L}$,  so $\downarrow\!a$ is d-complemented. 

Conversely, suppose $I$ is a d-complemented element of $\idl \mathcal{L}$. By definition either $I\subseteq \thinspace\downarrow\!\dbt$ or $I\subseteq \thinspace\downarrow\!\dbf$, without loss of generality we assume that $I\subseteq \thinspace\downarrow\!\dbt$. Since $I$ is d-complemented, there is an ideal $J$ contained in $\downarrow\!\dbf$ for which the join $I\vee J$ belongs to both ${\bf con}_{\idl}$ and ${\bf tot}_{\idl}$. So there exist $a\in I$ and $b\in J$ such that $(a,b)\in{\bf con}\cap{\bf tot}$. Now we finish the proof by  showing that $I=\thinspace\downarrow\!a$. For each $a'\in I$ take an upper bound $a''$ of $a$ and $a'$ in $I$, then $a''\vee b\in I\vee J$, which implies $(a'',b)\in {\bf con}\cap{\bf tot}$, hence $a=a''$ by Lemma \ref{uniqueness of adag} and consequently, $a'\leq a$ as desired.  \end{proof}

\begin{defn} Let   $\mathcal{L}=(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$ be a d-frame. We say that   \begin{enumerate}[label=\rm(\roman*)] \setlength{\itemsep}{0pt} \item (\cite[Definition 7.5]{JM2008}) $\mathcal{L}$ is compact if {\bf tot} is a Scott open set of $(L,\leq)$.  \item (\cite[Definition 2.3.6]{Jakl}) $\mathcal{L}$   is zero-dimensional (called d-zero-dimensional in \cite{Jakl}) if every element of $L$ is the join of a set of d-complemented elements. \end{enumerate} \end{defn}

\begin{exmp} \label{KZ of dO} (\cite[Section 2.3]{Jakl}) For each bitopological space $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$, the d-frame  $\dO(X, \tau_+, \tau_-)$ is compact and zero-dimensional  if and only if so is the bitopological space $(X,\tau_+,\tau_-)$ (in the sense of Definition \ref{compact bitop}). \end{exmp}

\begin{exmp}\label{Idl L is compact and zero-dimensional}
For each d-lattice $\mathcal{L}=(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$, the d-frame $\idl\mathcal{L}$ of ideals of $\mathcal{L}$ is compact. If $\mathcal{L}$ is moreover a  d-Boolean algebra, then $\idl\mathcal{L}$  is compact and zero-dimensional.
\end{exmp}

\begin{prop}\label{other side of the duality} If   $\mathcal{L}=(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$ is a compact and zero-dimensional d-frame, then $\mathcal{L}$ is isomorphic to the d-frame of ideals of the d-Boolean algebra of d-complemented elements of $\mathcal{L}$; that is, $\mathcal{L}\cong\idl\circ\dB \mathcal{L} $. 
\end{prop}

We prove a lemma first. We say that an element $x$ of a partially ordered set $P$ is \emph{compact} \cite[page 115]{Gierz2003} if it is way below itself, i.e., $x\ll x$; that means, for each directed subset $D$ of $P$, if $x\leq \bv D$ then $x\leq d$ for some $d\in D$.

\begin{lem}\label{each element of dBL is finite}
Let $\mathcal{L}=(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$ be a compact d-frame. Then every element of the underlying lattice $\dB L$  of the d-Boolean algebra $\dB\mathcal{L}$ is compact.  \end{lem}  

\begin{proof}
Since $\dB L=B_+\times B_-$, where $B_+$ and $B_-$ are, respectively, the sublattices composed of d-complemented elements contained in  $\downarrow\!\dbt$ and $\downarrow\!\dbf$, it suffices to show that every element of the lattice $B_+$  is compact and that every element of  $B_-$  is compact. 

Let $a$ be an element and  $D$ be a directed subset of $B_+$ with $a\leq\bv D$. Then $\{(d,a^\dag):d\in D\}$ is a directed subset of $L$ having $(\bv D, a^\dag)$ as a join. Since $(a, a^\dag)\in{\bf tot}$, it follows that $(\bv D, a^\dag)\in{\bf tot}$,  then $(d,a^\dag)\in{\bf tot}$ for some $d\in D$ because {\bf tot} is Scott open. Since $(a,a^\dag)\in{\bf con}$, it follows from ({\bf con}-{\bf tot}) that $a\leq   d$.
Likewise, every element of the lattice $B_-$ of d-complemented elements contained in $\downarrow\!\dbf$ is compact.  \end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Proposition \ref{other side of the duality}]  
Suppose $\mathcal{L}=(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$ is a compact and zero-dimensional d-frame. Define $\epsilon\colon\idl(\dB L)\longrightarrow L$ and $\kappa\colon L\lra \idl(\dB L)$ by $$\epsilon(I)=\bv I  \quad \text{and}\quad \kappa(x) = \thinspace\downarrow\!x  \cap\dB L  $$ for each ideal $I$ of $\dB L$ and each element $x$ of $L$. We claim that both   $\epsilon\colon \idl\circ\dB  \mathcal{L}  \longrightarrow \mathcal{L} $ and $\kappa\colon  \mathcal{L}\lra \idl\circ\dB  \mathcal{L} $ are d-frame homomorphisms. Here we  check  that $\kappa$ preserves {\bf tot}  for example. Suppose $x\in{\bf tot}$. Since $\mathcal{L}$ is zero-dimensional, the join of   $\kappa(x)$  (i.e. the join of the ideal $\downarrow\!x  \cap\dB L$)  is   $x$.  Since {\bf tot} is Scott open, it follows that $\kappa(x)$ meets {\bf tot}, hence $\kappa(x)\in{\bf tot}_{\idl}$. 

Since $\mathcal{L}$ is zero-dimensional, it follows that the composite $\epsilon\circ\kappa$ is the identity on $L$. Since $\mathcal{L}$ is compact and zero-dimensional, Lemma \ref{each element of dBL is finite} ensures that the composite $\kappa\circ\epsilon$ is the identity on $\idl(\dB L)$. Therefore, $\mathcal{L}$ is isomorphic to  $\idl\circ\dB \mathcal{L}$. 
\end{proof}

Now we arrive at the main result of this section, which presents a representation of d-Boolean algebras by d-frames.

\begin{thm} \label{dbool vs dfrm}
The category of d-Boolean algebras is equivalent to the category of compact and zero-dimensional d-frames.
\end{thm}
\begin{proof} Let {\bf KZdFrm} be the full subcategory of $\dFrm$ composed of compact and zero-dimensional d-frames. Then,   Proposition \ref{one side of the duality} and Proposition \ref{other side of the duality} show that the functors $\dB\colon {\bf KZdFrm}\lra\dBoo$ and $\idl\colon \dBoo\lra{\bf KZdFrm}$ witness that the categories $\dBoo$ and {\bf KZdFrm} are equivalent to each other. \end{proof}

Let {\bf KZFrm} be the full subcategory of the category {\bf Frm}  composed of compact and zero-dimensional frames; let $\Ba\colon {\bf Frm}\lra{\bf Bool}$ be the functor that sends each frame to the Boolean algebra of its complemented elements. Restricting the  domain of the functor $\Ba\colon {\bf Frm}\lra{\bf Bool}$ to {\bf KZFrm} gives rise to an equivalence between the category of Boolean algebras and the category of compact and zero-dimensional frames. This is actually the frame version (or, point-free version) of the Stone representation of Boolean algebras. 

The following diagram is commutative, so the representation of d-Boolean algebras by compact and zero-dimensional d-frames in Theorem \ref{dbool vs dfrm} is an extension of the Stone representation of Boolean algebras by compact and zero-dimensional frames. 
