\section{Spectra of d-Boolean algebras} 

In this section we establish the bitopological representation of d-Boolean algebras. The bitopological representation of a d-Boolean algebra is given by its spectrum --- a bitopological space of its prime d-ideals, which turns out to be a Stone bitopological space.

Suppose $L$ is a distributive lattice. It is readily seen that a subset $I$ of $L$ is an ideal if and only if the characteristic map $L\lra\{0,1\}$ of the complement of $I$ preserves finite joins (including the empty one); a subset $F$ of $L$ is a filter if and only if the characteristic map $L\lra\{0,1\}$ of $F$ preserves finite meets (including the empty one). This motivates the notions of d-ideal and d-filter  of  d-lattices. 
We remind the reader that the Boolean algebra $\mathbb{B}=\{0,1,\dbt,\dbf\}$ is viewed as a d-lattice with ${\bf con}=\{0,\dbt,\dbf\}$ and ${\bf tot}=\{1,\dbt,\dbf\}$. 

\begin{defn} 
Let $\mathcal{L}=(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$ be a d-lattice. A d-ideal of $\mathcal{L}$ is a map $g\colon L\lra \bbB$ subject to the following conditions: \begin{enumerate}[label=\rm(\alph*)] \setlength{\itemsep}{0pt}  
\item $g(\dbt)\leq  \dbt$, $g(\dbf)\leq \dbf$. 
\item $g$ preserves  {\bf con}  and finite joins; that is,  $g({\bf con})\subseteq\{0,\dbt,\dbf\}$  and $g(x\vee y)=g(x)\vee g(y)$ for all $x,y\in L$.  \end{enumerate}  
\end{defn}

\begin{defn} 
Let $\mathcal{L}=(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$ be a d-lattice. A d-filter of $\mathcal{L}$ is a map $f\colon L\lra \bbB$ subject to the following conditions: \begin{enumerate}[label=\rm(\alph*)] \setlength{\itemsep}{0pt}    
\item  $f(\dbt)\geq  \dbt$, $f(\dbf)\geq \dbf$.  \item $f$ preserves  {\bf tot}
and finite meets; that is, $f({\bf tot})\subseteq\{1,\dbt,\dbf\}$  and $f(x\wedge y)=f(x)\wedge f(y)$ for all $x,y\in L$.  
\end{enumerate}  
\end{defn}

It is clear that every d-ideal of  $(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$ maps $0\in L$ to $0\in \mathbb{B}$, every d-filter maps $1\in L$ to $1\in \mathbb{B}$. 

For each d-ideal $g\colon L\lra \mathbb{B}$ of a d-lattice $(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$, 
let $$ G_+=\{a\in L_+:g(a)=0\},\quad G_-=\{b\in L_-:g(b)=0\}.$$   
Then  $G_+$ is an ideal of the lattice $L_+$ and $G_-$ is an ideal of the lattice $L_-$ such that for all $(a,b)\in{\bf con}$, either $a\in G_+$ or $b\in G_-$. Conversely, we have the following 

\begin{prop} \label{structure of d-ideal} Let $(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$ be a d-lattice. If $I_+$ is an ideal of $L_+$ and $I_-$  is an ideal of $L_-$  such that for all $(a,b)\in{\bf con}$, either $a\in I_+$ or $b\in I_-$, then the map $g\colon L\lra\mathbb{B}$, given by  $$g(a\vee b)  =\begin{cases} 1 & a\notin I_+,~b\notin I_-,\\
\dbt & a\notin I_+,~b\in I_-,\\ 
\dbf & a\in I_+,~b\notin I_-,\\
0 & a\in I_+,~b\in I_- \end{cases}$$  for all $a\in L_+$ and $b\in L_-$, is the unique d-ideal of $(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$ such that $G_+=I_+$ and $G_-=I_-$.  \end{prop}
