\begin{proof}Straightforward verification. \end{proof}

Dually, for each d-filter $f\colon L\lra \bbB$ of $(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$, let   $$ 
F_+=\{a\in L_+:f(a\vee\dbf)=1\},\quad F_-=\{b\in L_-:f(\dbt\vee b)=1\}.$$ Then $F_+$ is a filter of $L_+$  and $F_-$ is a filter of $L_-$  such that for all $(a,b)\in{\bf tot}$, either $a\in F_+$ or $b\in F_-$. Parallel to Proposition \ref{structure of d-ideal}, we have the following characterization of d-filter.

\begin{prop} \label{structure of d-filter} Let $(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$ be a d-lattice. If $J_+$ is a filter of $L_+$ and $J_-$  is a filter of $L_-$  such that for all $(a,b)\in{\bf tot}$, either $a\in J_+$ or $b\in J_-$, then the map $f\colon L\lra\mathbb{B}$, given by  $$f(a\vee b)= \begin{cases}1 & a\in J_+,~b\in J_-,\\
\dbt & a\in J_+,~b\notin J_-,\\ 
\dbf & a\notin J_+,~b\in J_-,\\
0 &a\notin J_+,~b\notin J_- \end{cases}$$ for all $a\in L_+$ and $b\in L_-$, is the unique d-filter of $(L;\dbt,\dbf;{\bf con},{\bf tot})$ such that  $F_+=J_+$ and $F_-=J_-$. \end{prop}

