\begin{abstract}
  Recent discovery of unconventional superconductivity on multilayer nickelates under high pressure have stimulated great interest. The magnetic and charge configurations in the normal phase of multilayer nickelates which closely relate to the spin-charge fluctuations remain under strongly debated. In this work, we focus the normal-state magnetic, charge and orbital configurations and its evolutions with hydrostatic pressure and Sr-doping concentration of monolayer nickelate La$_2$NiO$_4$. We reveal that in the ambient pressure tetragonal La$_2$NiO$_4$ displays Neel-type antiferromagnetic order in planer Ni spins with negligible interlayer magnetic coupling; with the increase of pressure, La$_2$NiO$_4$ evolves from insulate into metallic state with the critical pressure about P = 50 GPa; with the substitution of La by Sr, the magnetic ground state evolves from G-type, A-type, to double spin striped antiferromagnetic states, as well as weak charge and orbital orders, at x=1; These results can greatly promote our understanding of the magnetic properties of Ruddlesden-Popper nickelates, and shed the light of the pairing mechanism of unconventional nickelate superconductors.

\end{abstract}

\maketitle

{\bf Motivations}
%\subsection{Importance of electronic correlation}
The discovery of  superconductivity in Ruddlesden-Popper (RP) nickelates\cite{li2019superconductivity, wang2022pressure, sun2023signatures} provides a new platform for studying unconventional high-temperature superconductivity.
As a most basic Ruddlesden-Popper (RP) compound La$_{ n+1}$Ni$_n$O$_{3n+1}$ in comparison with La$_{3}$Ni$_2$O$_7$ and La$_{4}$Ni$_3$O$_{10}$, La$_{2}$NiO$_4$ was synthesized very early soon after the discovery of cuprate superconductor La$_{2-x}$Sr$_x$CuO$_4$, however, pure and Sr-doped La$_{2}$NiO$_4$ did not found to be superconductive at ambient pressure. For such a layered nickelates, what do the electronic properties of La$_{2}$NiO$_4$ (214 phase, $n$=1) evolve with increasing pressure to high pressure? It is especially interested whether the properties are similar or very different from the bilayer La$_{3}$Ni$_2$O$_7$ and trilayer La$_{4}$Ni$_3$O$_{10}$. These implies that 214 phase deserves extensive and systematical study.
\\

%\subsection{Magnetic, orbital and charge configurations }
It is important to theoretically reveal the magnetic phase diagram of La$_{2}$NiO$_4$ under hydrostatic pressure, as well as the dependence of doping concentration. From the view of the superconducting pairing mechanism, it is very important to reveal the position and intensity of spin resonance peaks the spin fluctuation spectra of the theoretical model available and the comparison with experimental observation. However, most of the researches assumed that the coupling between Ni spins is a Neel-type antiferromagnetic interaction in La$_{2}$NiO$_4$, as in La$_{2}$CuO$_4$. From the iron-based superconductors, one may expect that the multi-orbital character and partial filling of the high-pressure phase of RP compounds will bring that its magnetic interaction and magnetic ground state differ from the simple Neel configuration in single-orbital cuprates, hence leading to outstanding the magnetic order configuration of the parent phase, the spin fluctuation spectrum, the symmetry of the superconducting pair, and the spin resonance.
\\

It is worth investigating whether tetragonal La$_{2-x}$A$_x$NiO$_4$ (A=Ca, Sr, ...) is or is not superconducting under high pressure, and also worth carrying out extensively and systematically exploring the similar or different properties of La$_{2-x}$A$_x$NiO$_4$ with respect to bilayer and trilayer nickelates. In this {\it Letter} we show that in the ambient pressure tetragonal La$_2$NiO$_4$ exhibits Neel-type antiferromagnetic order in planar Ni spins with negligible interlayer magnetic coupling; with the substitution of La by Sr, the magnetic ground state evolves from G-type to double spin striped antiferromagnetic one; with the increase of pressure, La$_2$NiO$_4$ transits from insulator to metal when P is larger than 50 GPa. These results can greatly promote our understanding of the magnetic properties of RP nickelates, and shed the light of the pairing mechanism of unconventional nickelate superconductors.
\\

%\section{Results}

{\bf Magnetic  Groundstate at Ambient Pressure}
%
We first determined the magnetic ground state of La$_2$NiO$_4$ at ambient pressure by using the correlation corrected density functional theory (DFT+U) calculations. To comprehensively evaluate the magnetic configurations, we considered five distinct magnetic states: (1) non-magnetic (NM) and four characteristic magnetic orderings as illustrated in Fig~\ref{fig1}. i.e., (2) ferromagnetic (FM), (3) A-type antiferromagnetic (A-AFM), (4) G-type antiferromagnetic (G-AFM), and (5) double spin stripe (DSS) configurations.

\begin{figure*}[htbp]
  \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{P-1.eps}
  \caption{\label{fig1} Crystal structure, Brillouin zone and four different magnetic configurations of La$_2$NiO$_4$. The red and blue arrows on the Ni sites indicate distinct spin orientations.}
\end{figure*}

The calculations were performed with the Hubbard $U$ parameters ranging from 2 to 5 eV to examine the system's sensitivity to electron correlation effects. The Hund's coupling $J$ was consistently set to 0.1$U$ in all calculations, following established practices for nickelates.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
  \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{P-2.eps}
  \caption{\label{fig2} Total energy differences (a) and magnetic moments (b) of La$_2$NiO$_4$ under different Hubbard $U$ values, $U$ = 2-5 eV, for various magnetic configurations. The A-AFM total energy is referenced to 0 eV in (a).}
\end{figure}


Fig~\ref{fig2} (a) shows the total energy differences per conventional cell (La$_4$Ni$_2$O$_8$) for all considered magnetic configurations under different Hubbard $U$. For each $U$ value, we normalized the total energy scale by setting the A-AFM configuration as the zero-energy reference, enabling direct comparison of relative stability across magnetic phases. We found that all magnetic orderings exhibit lower energies than the NM state, confirming the essential role of electron correlations in this system. Moreover, the G-AFM configuration consistently shows the lowest energy among all tested orderings across the examined $U$ range, establishing G-AFM configuration as the magnetic ground state. The energy difference between G-AFM and the second most stable DSS configuration is approximately 0.2 eV, indicating significant stability of the G-AFM phase. The near-degeneracy of FM and A-AFM energies suggests negligible interlayer magnetic coupling, consistent with the quasi-two-dimensional nature of this material.
Meanwhile,
Fig~\ref{fig2} (b) displays the calculated local magnetic moments of Ni ions, which exhibit values in the range of 1.40-1.75 $\mu_{B}$ depending on the specific configuration and Hubbard $U$ value. Notably, the moment sizes exhibit only weak dependence on the Hubbard $U$ parameter, suggesting robust local moment formation regardless of the precise correlation strength.
\\

{\bf Magnetic interactions at ambient pressure}
%
To further elucidate the magnetic behavior, we describe the spin interactions in insulating La$_2$NiO$_4$ by using a classical Heisenberg model:
\begin{eqnarray}
 \mathcal{H} = \sum_{i<j} J_{ij} \mathbf{S}_i \cdot \mathbf{S}_j
\end{eqnarray}
where $J_{ij}$ represents the exchange coupling between Ni spins $\mathbf{S}_i$ and $\mathbf{S}_j$, with the spin quantum number $S$=1 corresponding to the 3$d^{8}$ electronic configuration of Ni$^{2+}$ in the low-spin state.
The exchange parameters were derived by mapping the DFT+U energies of collinear magnetic states to the Heisenberg model.
%
\begin{table}[htbp]
  \setlength{\belowcaptionskip}{15pt}
  \caption{\label{T1} The Heisenberg exchange interactions of nearest-neighbor $J_{1}$ and next-nearest-neighbor $J_{2}$ with different Hubbard $U$.}
  \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.2}
  \begin{ruledtabular}
  \begin{tabular}{m{2cm}<{\centering}|cc|cc}
  $U$ (eV)  &  $J_{1}$ (meV) & &  $J_{2}$ (meV)    \\ \hline
  2         &      61.2     & &     2.6          \\
  3         &      52.8     & &     2.8          \\
  4         &      43.8     & &     2.1          \\
  5         &      36.2     & &     1.5          \\
  \end{tabular}
  \end{ruledtabular}
\end{table}
%
In La$_2$NiO$_4$, the minimal interlayer Ni-Ni distance is 6.8 \AA\ and the near-degeneracy of FM and A-AFM energies justify neglecting interlayer exchange $J_{\perp}$. For in-plane couplings, we restrict our analysis to Ni-Ni distances of 6.8 \AA\, retaining only nearest-neighbor $J_{1}$ and next-nearest-neighbor $J_{2}$. The calculated values $J_{1}$ and $J_{2}$ across $U$ = 2-5 eV are summarized in Table \ref{T1}. The calculated Heisenberg exchange parameters reveal several key physical insights.
The nearest-neighbor coupling $J_{1}$ is positive, ranging from 36.2 to 61.2 meV, which demonstrates strong antiferromagnetic character. The systematic decrease in $|J_{1}|$ with increasing on-site Coulomb interaction $U$ reflects the suppression of charge fluctuations as electron correlations are strengthen.
\\

In contrast, the next-nearest-neighbor coupling $J_{2}$ remains weak, with positive values ranging from 1.5 to 2.8 meV and exhibits minimal $U$-dependence, suggesting its origin may involve direct exchange or longer-range hybridization processes rather than being dominated by on-site correlations.
The small $J_{2}/J_{1}$ ratio (0.04-0.05) explains the stability of the G-type antiferromagnetic ground state observed in DFT+$U$ calculations, as this ratio is insufficient to induce significant magnetic frustration that might stabilize alternative configurations like the DSS phase.
The values of $J_{1}$ align well with experimental estimates from the others studies of similar nickelate compounds \cite{bialo2024strain}. The progressive reduction of $J_{1}$ with increasing $U$ implies that intermediate correlation strengths likely provide the best agreement with experimental observations.
\\

These results collectively demonstrate how the relative strength and $U$-evolution of exchange couplings govern the magnetic behavior in La$_2$NiO$_4$, with the dominant $J_{1}$ interaction determining the overall antiferromagnetic character while the minor $J_{2}$ contribution has limited impact on the ground state selection. The quantitative agreement between these calculated parameters and available experimental data validates the chosen computational approach while providing microscopic insight into the exchange mechanisms operating in this correlated oxide system.
\\


{\bf Pressure Evolution of Electronic Structures}
%
We also investigated the magnetic ground states at various pressures, these results reveal that the magnetic ground states consistently exhibit an intralayer antiferromagnetic phase with negligible interlayer spin interactions. Both crystallographically distinct Ni atoms within the unit cell demonstrate identical magnetic moments of 1.6 $\mu_{B}$ at ambient pressure, which gradually decrease under applied pressure to approximately 1.4 $\mu_{B}$  at 75 GPa. This spin density wave phase devoid of magnetic moment split suggests that La$_2$NiO$_4$ maintains a pure spin-ordered system across all pressure regimes up to 100 GPa, exhibiting neither charge ordering nor orbital ordering.
\\

Figure~\ref{fig3} (a-d) presents the pressure-dependent electronic structures of La$_2$NiO$_4$ in its magnetic ground state. At pressures ranging from 0 to 50 GPa, the band structure consistently reveals an insulating character, characterized by a finite energy gap near the Fermi level. The magnitude of this gap exhibits systematic pressure dependence: initially measuring approximately 1 eV under ambient conditions, it undergoes progressive closure with increasing pressure until to P =  50 GPa, and ultimately transits to a fully metallic state when P = 50-100 GPa. This gradual gap rclosing correlates with a continuous insulator-metal transition, where the material evolves from a gapped insulating state to a metallic regime through intermediate semi-metallic phases.
Notably, the pressure-driven metallization occurs without abrupt structural  phase transitions, suggesting a smooth evolution of electronic structure under hydrostatic compression. These findings align with current experimental observations \cite{Ji2024-CPL}.
\\

\begin{figure*}[htbp]
  \includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{P-3.eps}
  \caption{\label{fig3} Electronic structure and local density of states of La$_2$NiO$_4$ in the G-AFM configuration under applied pressures of (a) 0 GPa, (b) 25 GPa, (c) 50 GPa and (d) 75 GPa.}
\end{figure*}

Our DFT calculations corroborate this result through partial density of states (PDOS) results. As demonstrated in Fig~\ref{fig3} (e) and (f), the orbital-resolved density of states (DOS) for the two distinct Ni sites (Ni-spin up and Ni-spin down) under 75 GPa exhibit identical electronic occupation despite their antiparallel spin orientations. The DOS spectra across all pressure regimes consistently exhibit comparable electronic configuration characteristics, with no significant pressure-induced modifications to orbital occupation configurations. The spatial uniformity in both charge distribution and orbital configuration is thereby confirmed through this equivalent electronic occupation pattern at both nickel sites.
The two Ni atoms exhibit identical electronic configurations of ($t^{6}_{2g}d^{1}_{z^{2}}d^{1}_{x^{2}-y^{2}}$), corresponding to a +2-valence state with fully occupied $t_{2g}$ orbitals and half-filled $e_{g}$ orbitals. Despite residing in a distorted octahedral crystal field where the degeneracy between $d_{z^{2}}$ and $d_{x^{2}-y^{2}}$ orbitals is lifted through symmetry-breaking distortions, the two $e_{g}$ orbitals maintain comparable electronic configurations. This behavior arises from the strong on-site Coulomb repulsion, which suppresses higher-energy double occupancy by favoring electron localization. The preserved orbital symmetry under crystal field distortion highlights the dominant role of electronic correlations in governing the orbital occupation hierarchy, as manifested by the absence of orbital polarization between these split energy levels. Such characteristics further corroborate the significant influence of strong correlation effects in stabilizing the observed electronic structure.
\\

In contrast, our computational results demonstrate that La$_2$NiO$_4$ exhibits fundamentally distinct behavior: beyond its magnetic configuration differing from the double spin stripe phase observed in La$_3$Ni$_2$O$_7$ \cite{Zhao2025-ScienceBulletin,Ni2025-NPJ,Zhang2024-arxiv,LaBollita2024-PRM}, no charge or orbital ordering emerges in La$_2$NiO$_4$. Notably, the distorted crystal field environment  which would conventionally induce orbital differentiation-is effectively compensated by strong electronic correlations (quantified through the Hubbard $U$ parameter). This robust correlation effect preserves orbital uniformity despite symmetry-breaking lattice distortions, highlighting the material's unique position in the interplay between crystal field effects and electron correlation physics.
\\

Crucially, the energy gap is absent in nonmagnetic configurations, even when incorporating substantial Hubbard $U$ parameter values. This demonstrates that the gap-opening mechanism fundamentally originates from the unique magnetic ordering pattern in the ground state of La$_2$NiO$_4$, i.e. the Slater insulator.  The pressure-driven insulator-to-metal transition observed in La$_2$NiO$_4$ exhibits markedly distinct characteristics compared to its RP bilayer counterpart La$_3$Ni$_2$O$_7$.
While both systems undergo metallization under compression, their magnetic resilience and associated superconducting prerequisites differ fundamentally. In La$_3$Ni$_2$O$_7$, the onset of metallicity near 10 GPa coincides with rapid suppression of spin-charge-orbital ordering, accompanied by a droppeastic reduction of Ni magnetic moments from 1 $\mu_{B}$ at ambient pressure \cite{Zhao2025-ScienceBulletin,Zhang2024-arxiv,Khasanov2024-arxiv,Guo1988-JPCS} to 0.6 $\mu_{B}$ at 40 GPa \cite{sun2023signatures}-a regime where 80 K superconductivity emerges.
\\

In stark contrast, La$_2$NiO$_4$ demonstrates exceptional magnetic robustness: its Ni moments persist at 1.4 $\mu_{B}$ even under 75 GPa compression, with complete gap closure delayed until 50 GPa. This dichotomy originates from their structural dimensionality-dependent orbital physics, the bilayer configuration of La$_3$Ni$_2$O$_7$ enables pressure-enhanced interlayer $d_{z^{2}}$ orbital hybridization that destabilizes magnetic exchange pathways, whereas the monolayer RP structure of La$_2$NiO$_4$ maintains strong in-plane $d_{x^{2}-y^{2}}$ orbital dominance through pressure-resistant superexchange interactions. The persistent magnetism in La$_2$NiO$_4$ under extreme compression suggests significant challenges in achieving analogous superconducting states through pure hydrostatic pressure, though strategic chemical modifications, {\it e. g.}, interlayer engineering via Sr substitution \cite{Zhang2024-JMS,Kumar2021-MaterialsToday} might artificially enhance magnetic suppressibility while preserving correlation strength, potentially opening alternative pathways toward high-$T_{c}$ phases in monolayer nickelates.
\\

{\bf Effects of Sr-doping on La$_2$NiO$_4$}
%
Our results demonstrate that undoped La$_2$NiO$_4$ exhibits AFM insulating behavior, with its electronic structure at the Fermi surface dominated by Ni 3$d^{8}$ states in the $d_{x^{2}-y^{2}}$ orbital under high pressure. Meanwhile, one expects that Sr-doped La$_2$NiO$_4$ shows an insulator-metal transition \cite{Zhang2024-JMS}. To further understand the effects of doping, we investigate the electronic structures of La$_{2-x}$Sr$_x$NiO$_4$ with $x$ = 0, 0.5, 1 and 1.5, respectively. Through partial substitution of Sr$^{2+}$ for La$^{3+}$, we reveal what doping concentration induces simultaneous lattice distortion and charge reconstruction. The distinct ionic radii and valence states between Sr$^{2+}$ for La$^{3+}$ are expected to concurrently induce lattice distortions and electronic reconstruction, potentially altering the insulating-to-metallic transition and modulating the Ni-O bond covalency.
\\

\begin{figure*}[htbp]
  \includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{P-4.eps}
  \caption{\label{fig4} Electronic structures of La$_{2-x}$Sr$_x$NiO$_4$ with different doping level ($x$). (a) and (b) Band structures for $x$ = 0.5 and $x$ = 1.5, respectively. (c) Spin-up projected band structure of Ni for $x$ = 1. (d) and (e) PDOS for two inequivalent Ni sites for $x$ = 1.}
\end{figure*}

Based on symmetry analysis, we investigated three representative doping levels of La$_{2-x}$Sr$_x$NiO$_4$ ($x$ = 0.5, 1 and 1.5), with the corresponding relaxed structures shown in the Supplemental Material.
In all cases, the ordered substitution of La by Sr atoms breaks the original tetragonal symmetry of the parent compound ($I4/mmm$ space group), leading to a symmetry-lowering structural distortion. As a consequence, Ni sites split into two inequivalent types: one retains the ideal NiO$_2$ planar coordination, while the other experiences local distortions driven by nearby Sr dopants.
Our first-principles calculations reveal the magnetic ground states and a doping-induced sequence of magnetic transitions. Specifically, at $x = 0.5$, $1.0$, and $1.5$ the compounds stabilize in A-AFM, DSS, and FM configurations, respectively, distinct from the G-AFM order in the undoped parent phase. This evolution of magnetic order stems from a systematic reconstruction of the Ni $e_g$ orbital occupancy, driven by hole doping. The substitution of Sr$^{2+}$ for La$^{3+}$ introduces holes that preferentially affect the $d_{x^2-y^2}$ orbital, leading to anisotropic exchange interactions and a reorganization of the spin texture.
\\

As shown in Fig \ref{fig4}(a) and \ref{fig4}(b), the electronic structures at $x = 0.5$ and $x = 1.5$ exhibit a complete closure of the insulating gap present in the parent compound, with multiple bands crossing the Fermi level. This indicates a transition to a correlated multiband metallic state. The dominant contributions to the density of states at the Fermi level originate from hybridized Ni $3d$ and O $2p$ orbitals, resembling the behavior of the undoped system under applied pressure, where bandwidth broadening enhances electronic delocalization.
In contrast, LaSrNiO$_4$ retains an insulating gap, as illustrated in Figs \ref{fig4}(c). Importantly, the PDOS reveals a pronounced disparity between the two inequivalent Ni sites, indicating the emergence of charge ordering. This suggests that at this intermediate doping level, electron localization is favored on specific Ni sites, possibly driven by a site-selective Mott mechanism or Coulomb-induced symmetry breaking \cite{Greenberg2018Pressure}.
\\

Doping leads to a systematic suppression of the local magnetic moments on the Ni sites. For $x = 0.5$, the Ni moments are reduced to 1.5~$\mu_B$ and 1.2~$\mu_B$ on the two inequivalent lattice sites. With increasing doping, these values further decrease to 1.5~$\mu_B$ and 0.0~$\mu_B$ at $x = 1.0$, and to 0.9~$\mu_B$ and 0.8~$\mu_B$ at $x = 1.5$, respectively, all significantly lower than the 1.6~$\mu_B$ moment observed in undoped La$_2$NiO$_4$. This continuous reduction reflects the progressive oxidation of Ni$^{2+}$ ($d^8$, $e_g^2$, $S = 1$) to Ni$^{3+}$ ($d^7$, $e_g^1$), favoring a low-spin ($S = 1/2$) or even nonmagnetic state depending on the local environment. The presence of inequivalent moments, particularly the emergence of a nonmagnetic Ni site at $x = 1$, further supports the formation of charge and spin disproportionation. These results highlight the role of hole doping in modulating both the spin state and the local electronic configuration of Ni ions, thereby tuning the magnetic landscape of the system.
\\


{\bf Discussions and Conclusions}
%
We notice that LaSrNiO$_{4}$ consists of single layer NiO plane, and display similar electronic properties as bilayer La$_3$Ni$_2$O$_7$ which sharing the same RP structure. Up to date, in the magnetic ground state of La$_3$Ni$_2$O$_7$-a bilayer nickelate has been experimentally observed that only spin ordering persists, with no detectable signatures of charge or orbital ordering \cite{Zhao2025-ScienceBulletin,Shi2025-arxiv}. However, multiple theoretical investigations have consistently suggested the coexistence of coupled spin-charge orders in its magnetic ground state \cite{Ni2025-NPJ,Zhang2024-arxiv,LaBollita2024-PRM}. These results align with experimental evidence of magnetic moment differentiation between distinct Ni sites \cite{Zhao2025-ScienceBulletin,Shi2025-arxiv}, as magnetic ordering with notable spiltting of magnetic moments may naturally lead to concomitant weak charge ordering and  weak the orbital ordering, as shown in Table \ref{T2}.
\begin{table}[htbp]
  \setlength{\belowcaptionskip}{15pt}
  \caption{\label{T2} The charge, spin and orbital occupations of two inequivalent Ni atoms in LaSrNiO$_4$.}
  \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.2}
  \begin{ruledtabular}
  \begin{tabular}{c|ccccc}
  Atom (spin) & $d_{xy}$ & $d_{yz}$ & $d_{xz}$ & $d_{x^{2}-y^{2}}$ & $d_{3z^{2}-r^{2}}$ \\
  \hline
  Ni 1 (up)      & 0.98 & 0.98 & 0.98 & 0.71 & 0.67 \\
  Ni 1 (down)    & 0.98 & 0.98 & 0.98 & 0.71 & 0.67 \\
  Ni 2 (up)      & 0.95 & 0.96 & 0.96 & 0.42 & 0.40 \\
  Ni 2 (down)    & 0.98 & 0.98 & 0.98 & 0.92 & 0.94 \\
  \end{tabular}
  \end{ruledtabular}
\end{table}
From this table, we find that in addition to the magnetic moment difference between Ni 1 and Ni 2, there are weak charge order with $n_{Ni 1}-n_{Ni 2}$ =0.08, and weak orbital order with $n_{x^{2}-y^{2}}-n_{3z^{2}-r^{2}}$ =0.08 on atom Ni 1.
Consequently, the coexistence scenario of spin order, weak orbital and charge orders in LaSrNiO$_4$ is highly plausible. We expect further experimental verification.
\\

On the same time, though single Ni ion in La$_2$NiO$_4$ has the similar crystal field environment to those in La$_3$Ni$_2$O$_7$ and La$_4$Ni$_3$O$_{10}$, the Ni spins of the NiO$_2$ plane in 214 phase display quasi-two-dimensional characteristic. Thus, our strong correlation correction with $U$ = 5 eV to the electronic structures is justified and reasonable.
Furthermore, to some extent electron or hole doping plays similar role with the varying hydrostatic pressure, i.e. the chemical pressure. For example, Ca, Sr or other bivalent elements doping not only introduces hole carriers, but also alters the orbital occupations, as well as the band structures and Fermi surfaces. We find that the doping plays distinct role on magnetic configurations, in comparison with  the variation of the hydrostatic pressure in La$_2$NiO$_4$.
\\

In summary, we have shown that in the ambient pressure tetragonal La$_2$NiO$_4$ exhibits Neel-type antiferromagnetic order in planar Ni spins with negligible interlayer magnetic coupling; with the substitution of La by Sr, the magnetic ground state evolves from G-type to double spin striped antiferromagnetic one, as well as weak charge and orbital orders, at $x$=1; with the increase of pressure, La$_2$NiO$_4$ evolves from insulate into metallic state with the critical pressure when P is larger than 50 GPa.
These results not only favor to clarify the evolution of the groundstate magnetic structure, charge distributions and orbital structures of the parent phase La$_2$NiO$_4$ with the increasing pressure, but also are benefitful for our getting insight of the magnetic fluctuations and the superconductive pairing mechanism of the Ruddlesden-Popper compounds.

