\myAbst{%
We introduce three biharmonic Steklov problems on differential forms with Neumann boundary conditions and show that they are elliptic. We prove the existence of a discrete spectrum for each of those problems and give associated variational characterizations for their eigenvalues. We establish eigenvalue estimates known as Kuttler-Sigillito inequalities, relating the eigenvalues of these problems with the eigenvalues of the Steklov, Dirichlet and Neumann standard problems as well as the biharmonic Steklov problem with Dirichlet boundary conditions on differential forms.
}
\textit{Mathematics Subject Classification} (2020): 35A15, 35G15, 35J40, 35P15, 58C40, 58J32, 58J50



\textit{Keywords}: Riemannian manifolds with boundary, biharmonic Steklov operator, Neumann boun-\\dary conditions, discrete spectrum, eigenvalue estimates
\section{Introduction}
\subsection{Historical background}
Spectral theory is a vast field that intersects with several aspects of mathematics, such as analysis, geometry, and topology. The applications of spectral theory are closely related to physics. The Dirichlet and Neumann problems are the primary examples of spectral problems \cite{Chavel, taylor}, allowing the study of the properties of eigenfunctions and eigenvalues on geometric domains. Their applications are directly relevant to the study of vibration modes and the diffusion of sound and heat in domains with boundaries. For instance, given a planar domain ("drum"), how does its geometry influence its vibrations or the evolution of temperature within it?
Mathematically, this corresponds to studying the spectrum of a differential operator, which in our case is the Laplacian under certain boundary conditions. The Steklov problem is another example of spectral problems, introduced by the Russian mathematician Vladimir Andreevich Steklov in a talk given in 1895, see \cite{SteklovLegacy}.

In this paper we work on biharmonic Steklov operators. The study of these differential operators originated from the theory of elastic oscillating membranes and was mathematically examined for the first time on planar domains by J. Kuttler and V. Sigillito \cite{Kuttler1, Kuttler&Sigillito} and L.E. Payne \cite{Payne}. The latter gave some isoperimetric inequalities for the first eigenvalue and eigenvalue estimates were given in \cite{Liu1, Liu2}. The problem also appeared in \cite{BucurFerreroGazzola}, where some results about the first Steklov eigenvalue of the biharmonic operator in bounded domains are given. There are two distinct but related natural spectral problems, depending on the boundary conditions. Let $(M,g)$ be a compact Riemannian manifold with a smooth boundary and let $\nu$ be the inward unit normal along the boundary $\partial M$. The scalar biharmonic Steklov problem with Dirichlet boundary conditions is defined as: 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:IntroBSD}
\begin{cases}
    \Delta^2 u = 0 & \text{on}\ M \\
    u = 0 &  \text{on } \partial M\\
    \Delta u - q \partial_\nu u = 0 &  \text{on } \partial M,
\end{cases}
\end{equation} and the scalar biharmonic Steklov problem with Neumann boundary conditions is:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:IntroBSN}
\begin{cases}
    \Delta^2 u = 0 & \text{on}\ M \\
   \partial_\nu u  = 0 &  \text{on } \partial M\\
    \partial_\nu (\Delta u) - \ell u  = 0 &  \text{on } \partial M,
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $u$ is a smooth function on $M$, and $\Delta$ is the (positive) scalar Laplacian operator. In both problems, the real numbers $q$ and $\ell$ are the eigenvalues of the operator.
Following the original studies \cite{Kuttler1, Kuttler&Sigillito} on planar domains, which in particular showed the existence of a discrete spectrum consisting of real eigenvalues of finite multiplicity for problems \eqref{eq:IntroBSD} and \eqref{eq:IntroBSN}, these problems were more recently studied on higher-dimensional Euclidean domains in \cite{FerreroGazzolaWeth}. It was subsequently shown by S. Raulot and A. Savo in \cite{RaulotSavo} that the biharmonic Steklov-Dirichlet operator on forms of maximal degree is related to the Serrin problem \cite{Serrin}. The Serrin problem is the following boundary problem: given $M$ subset of ${\mathbb{R}}^n$, we look for a solution $u$ such that, for some real constant $c$ it satisfies the overdetermined problem \begin{equation}\label{eq:IntroSerrin}
\begin{cases}
    \Delta u = 1 & \text{on}\ M \\
    u = 0 &  \text{on } \partial M\\
    {\partial_\nu u}_{|\partial M}=c& \text{on} \ \partial M.
\end{cases}
\end{equation} Actually $u$ describes the velocity of an incompressible viscous fluid in a straight pipe with a circular section.

More recently, A. Hassannezhad and A. Siffert \cite{hassannezhadETsiffert} generalized some of the estimates of \cite{Kuttler1, Kuttler&Sigillito} for both scalar biharmonic Steklov operators on arbitrary Riemannian manifolds and showed that other quantities appear in their estimates that depend on the curvature of the manifold. In the same context, S. Kouzayha established in her PhD thesis \cite{KouzayhaThese} an inequality that links the first eigenvalue of the scalar biharmonic Steklov-Dirichlet problem with those of the Dirichlet and Robin Laplacians.

In a recent work \cite{BSFidaGeorgeOlaNicolas}, the biharmonic Steklov problem with Dirichlet boundary conditions \eqref{eq:IntroBSD} is generalized to differential forms of arbitrary degree. Appropriate boundary conditions are considered to make the corresponding problem well-posed. The smallest eigenvalue is characterized, and various estimates for this eigenvalue are obtained, some of which involve eigenvalues for other problems such as Dirichlet, Neumann, Robin, and Steklov. Independently, other inequalities concerning the eigenvalues of these latter problems are established.

In this work we extend the biharmonic Steklov problem with Neumann boundary condition \eqref{eq:IntroBSN} to differential forms of arbitrary degree. This extension is not unique, as there are several possible choices of boundary conditions. We provide the variational characterization of the eigenvalues for three possible choices of such extensions. This allows us to generalize Kuttler-Sigillito spectral inequalities, which relate eigenvalues of different problems on a bounded planar domain \cite{Kuttler&Sigillito}. Some of these inequalities on functions were also established by A. Hassannezhad and A. Siffert on compact Riemannian manifolds with $C^2$ boundary. 

From now on, we will refer to the Biharmonic Steklov problem with Dirichlet boundary condition as BSD and the Biharmonic Steklov problem with Neumann boundary condition as BSN.

\subsection{Biharmonic Steklov problems on functions}
 In this section, we introduce the BSD and BSN operators and review on the existence of a discrete spectrum for both operators for the scalar case on Riemannian manifolds as in \cite{hassannezhadETsiffert}. Here and from now on, we define the scalar Laplacian for all functions $u$ on a Riemannian manifold $(M,g)$ by $\Delta u=-\tr (\Hess(u))$. The bi-Laplacian will be defined as the square of the Laplacian. 


\begin{definition}
    We define the BSD operator denoted by $BSD^{func}$, as follows:
\begin{align*}
BSD^{func}:L^2(\partial M) &\longrightarrow L^2(\partial M)\\
f &\longmapsto \Delta u
\end{align*}
where $u$ is related to $f$ by: $\Delta^2 u = 0$ on $M$, $u = 0$ on $\partial M$, and $\partial_\nu u = f$ on $\partial M$.
\end{definition}
For the existence of such $u$ see for instance  \thref{theo:thm5.4} below.

We now recall the BSD problem on a compact Riemannian manifold for the scalar case.
\begin{thm}\cite[Paragraph 2]{FerreroGazzolaWeth}\label{theo:bsdfct}
    Let $(M^n,g)$ be a compact Riemannian manifold of dimension $n\geq 2$ with smooth boundary. The operator $BSD^{func}$ is elliptic positive and self adjoint. It has a discrete spectrum consisting of an unbounded monotonously non-decreasing sequence of positive real eigenvalues of finite multiplicities $({q_k})_{k \geq 1}$. In other words, for every $k$ there exists a finite dimensional space of smooth solutions $u$ to the following boundary problem:  
\begin{equation}\label{eq:BSDfct}
\begin{cases}
    \Delta^2 u = 0 & \text{on}\ M \\
    u = 0 & \text{on } \partial M \\
    \Delta u - q_k \partial_\nu u = 0 & \text{on } \partial M.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

\end{thm}


\begin{definition}\label{def:DeltaBSNfct}
    Similarly we define the BSN operator denoted by $\Delta_{BSN}$, as follows: 
    \begin{align*}
BSN^{func}:L^2(\partial M) &\longrightarrow L^2(\partial M)\\
f&\longmapsto -\partial_\nu \Delta u
\end{align*}such that $u$ is related to $f$ by: $\Delta^2 u = 0$ on $M$, $\partial_\nu u = 0$ on $\partial M$, and $u = f$ on $\partial M$.
\end{definition}
For the existence of such $u$ see for instance  \thref{theo:thm5.4} below.
We now recall the BSN problem on a compact Riemannian manifold for the scalar case.
\begin{thm}\cite[Theorem 4.8]{hassannezhadETsiffert} \label{theo:bsnfct}
    Let $(M^n,g)$ be a compact Riemannian manifold of dimension $n\geq 2$ with smooth boundary. The operator $BSN^{func}$ is elliptic non-negative and self adjoint. It has a discrete spectrum consisting of an unbounded monotonously non-decreasing sequence of non-negative real eigenvalues of finite multiplicities $({\ell_k})_{k \geq 0}$. In other words, for every $k$ there exists a finite dimensional space of smooth solutions $u$ to the following boundary problem:  
\begin{equation}\label{eq:BSNfonction}
\begin{cases}
     \Delta^2 u = 0 & \text{on  } M \\
    \partial_\nu u = 0 & \text{on  } \partial M \\
\partial_\nu\Delta u + \ell_k  u = 0 & \text{on } \partial M.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
Moreover, $\ell_0 = 0$ is the eigenvalue associated with the constant eigenfunction.

\end{thm}
The proof of  \thref{theo:bsnfct} was given briefly in \cite{hassannezhadETsiffert}. In fact, it follows from  \lemref{lem:p=n} that  \thref{theo:bsdfct} and \thref{theo:bsnfct} are particular cases of  \thref{thm:TheoremeGeneralBSNF1} which we prove later on in this paper.
\subsection{Kuttler-Sigillito inequalities}
After obtaining well posed BSD and BSN problems and finding their variational characterizations, inequalities connecting the eigenvalues of different problems were established. These are known under the name of Kuttler-Sigillito inequalities. These inequalities were introduced in \cite{Kuttler&Sigillito} on a subset of $\mathbb{R}^2$ and were partly generalized in \cite{hassannezhadETsiffert} to the manifold setting. We provide these inequalities from \cite{hassannezhadETsiffert, Kuttler&Sigillito}, that we extend for differential forms later on. We denote by $\lambda_k$, $\mu_k$, $\sigma_k$, $q_k$, and $\ell_k$ the $k^{th}$ eigenvalues of the following problems, respectively: Dirichlet, Neumann, Steklov, BSD, and BSN. 

\textbf{Note that we always index the eigenvalues of non-negative operators so that $k=1$ corresponds to the first positive eigenvalue.}

 \begin{thm}\cite[Theorem 1.1]{hassannezhadETsiffert}\label{thm:firstineq}
    Let $(M^n, g)$ be a compact Riemannian manifold of dimension $n \geq 2$ with a $C^2$ boundary. For all integer $k\geq 1$, we have:
    \begin{equation*}
        \mu_k \sigma_1 \leq \ell_k \quad \text{and} \quad \mu_1 \sigma_k \leq \ell_k.
    \end{equation*}
\end{thm}
\begin{thm}\cite[Table 1]{Kuttler&Sigillito}\label{thm:ineqKuttlerSigillito}
  On a bounded domain $M$ of the plane with piecewise $C^1$ boundary $\partial M$, the following inequalities hold:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
    \item $q_1 \sigma_1^2 \leq \ell_1$,
    \item $\mu_1^{-1} \leq \lambda_1^{-1} + (q_1 \ell_1)^{-\frac{1}{2}}$,
    \item $\mu_1^{-1} \leq \lambda_1^{-1} + (q_1 \sigma_1)^{-1}$.
\end{enumerate}

\end{thm}




\subsection{BSD on differential forms}
We have seen above the BSD problem, defined for functions on a domain of $\mathbb{R}^2$ and on a compact Riemannian manifold with smooth boundary. This problem has been generalized for differential forms of arbitrary degree by F. El Chami, N. Ginoux, G. Habib, and O. Makhoul in \cite{BSFidaGeorgeOlaNicolas}. Motivated by the extension of Serrin problem \eqref{eq:IntroSerrin} to differential forms, they defined the boundary problem \eqref{eq:BSDF1}. They showed the existence of a discrete spectrum and provided classical and alternative characterizations for the first eigenvalue of this problem. We cite some of their results. 

 Let $(M^n,g)$ be a compact Riemannian manifold of dimension $n\geq 2$ with smooth boundary $\partial M$. 
\begin{notation}
  We denote by $\nu$ the inward unit normal along the boundary $\partial M$, by $d$ the exterior differential operator, by $\delta$ the codifferential operator, by $\iota: \partial M \to M$ the inclusion map, and by $\lrcorner$ the interior product. We write $\Delta=d\delta+\delta d$ the Hodge-de-Rham Laplacian on differential forms (see  \secref{sec:rappelGeometrie}). 

\end{notation} 
By \cite[Theorem 2.2]{BSFidaGeorgeOlaNicolas} the boundary problem \begin{equation}\label{eq:BSDF1}
(BSD)\begin{cases}
    \Delta^2 \w = 0 & \text{on }\ M \\
     \w = 0 &  \text{on  } \partial M\\
    \nu \lrcorner \Delta \w + q \iota^*  \delta\w = 0 & \text{on  } \partial M \\
     \iota^*  \Delta \w- q\nu \lrcorner d \w   = 0 & \text{on  } \partial M,
\end{cases}
\end{equation} on $p$-forms, has a discrete spectrum consisting of an unbounded monotonously non-decreasing sequence of positive eigenvalues of finite multiplicities $(q_{j,p})_{j\geq1}.$


\begin{thm}\cite[Theorem 2.6]{BSFidaGeorgeOlaNicolas}
    The first eigenvalue $q_{1,p}$ of BSD \eqref{eq:BSDF1} can be characterized as follows:
\begin{eqnarray}
\label{eq:q_1Standard}q_{1,p}&=&\inf\left\{\frac{\|\Delta\w\|_{L^2(M)}^2}{
\|\nu\lrcorner d\w\|_{L^2(\partial
M)}^2+\|\iota^*\delta\w\|_{L^2(\partial
M)}^2}\,|\,\w\in\Omega^p(M),\,\w_{|_{\partial
M}}=0\ \text{and}\ \nabla_\nu\w\neq 0\right\}\\
\label{eq:q_1Alternative}&=&\inf\left\{\frac{\|\w\|_{L^2(\partial M)}^2}{
\|\w\|_{L^2(
M)}^2}\,|\,\w\in\Omega^p(M)\setminus\{0\},\,\;\Delta\w=0\textrm{ on
}M\right\}.
\end{eqnarray}
\end{thm} Both infima are indeed minima, where the infimum in \eqref{eq:q_1Standard} is attained by a $q_{1,p}$-eigenform for BSD1 \eqref{eq:BSDF1} and \eqref{eq:q_1Alternative}
is attained by $\Delta\w$, where $\w$ is a $q_{1,p}$-eigenform for BSD1 \eqref{eq:BSDF1}. 

\subsection{BSN on differential forms}
To establish inequalities between the eigenvalues for differential forms, as was done in the scalar case, we need to find a suitable BSN problem for differential forms, inspired by the work done in \cite{BSFidaGeorgeOlaNicolas}. This first following problem is the natural analogue of the BSD problem presented in \cite{BSFidaGeorgeOlaNicolas}, adapted to Neumann boundary conditions.\\
Let us first define the space that will be the kernel of many boundary problems we are interested in.
\begin{definition}
    The absolute de Rham cohomology of order $p$ is the space \begin{equation}\label{eq:Hap}
        H_A^p(M) = \left\{ \w \in \Omega^p(M) \mid d\w = 0 \text{ on } M, \ \delta\w = 0 \text{ on } M, \ \nu \lrcorner \w = 0 \text{ on } \partial M \right\}.
 \end{equation} It has finite dimension, see \cite[Section 2]{RaulotSavo}.
\end{definition}
\begin{thm}\label{thm:TheoremeGeneralBSNF1}
 The following boundary problem
\begin{equation}\label{eq:BSNF1}
(BSN1)\begin{cases}
    \Delta^2 \w = 0 & \text{on } M \\
    \nu \lrcorner \w = 0 & \text{on } \partial M \\
    \nu \lrcorner d\w = 0 & \text{on } \partial M \\
    \nu \lrcorner \Delta \w + \ell \iota^* \delta \w = 0 & \text{on } \partial M\\
 \nu \lrcorner \Delta d\w + \ell \iota^* \w = 0 & \text{on } \partial M,
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
for differential p-forms, has a discrete spectrum consisting of an unbounded non-decreasing sequence of positive real eigenvalues with finite multiplicities $(\ell_i)_{i \geq 1}$ and possibly $\ell_0=0$.
Moreover, its kernel is $H_A^p(M)$.

\end{thm}

It happens that we could not use this version of the BSN, which we will refer to as BSN1, to establish Kuttler-Sigillito inequalities due to the non-suitable boundary conditions. For that reason, we have first introduced another version of the BSN, called BSN2, which can be used to establish the equivalent of  \thref{thm:firstineq}.

\begin{thm}\label{thm:TheoremeGeneralBSNF2}
 The following boundary problem
\begin{equation}\label{eq:BSNF2}
(BSN2)\begin{cases}
\Delta^2 \w = 0 & \text{on } M \\
\nu \lrcorner \w = 0 & \text{on } \partial M \\
\nu \lrcorner d\w = 0 & \text{on } \partial M \\
\iota^* \delta \w = 0 & \text{on } \partial M \\
\nu \lrcorner  \Delta d \w + \mathbf{l} \iota^* \w = 0 & \text{on } \partial M,
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
for differential p-forms, has a discrete spectrum consisting of an unbounded non-decreasing sequence of positive real eigenvalues with finite multiplicities $(\mathbf{l}_i)_{i \geq 1}$ and possibly $\mathbf{l}_0=0$. Moreover, its kernel is $H_A^p(M)$.
\end{thm}
The BSN2 version allows us to establish the analogous of  \thref{thm:firstineq}, but not the analogous of all the inequalities from  \thref{thm:ineqKuttlerSigillito} for differential forms. That is why we need to introduce a third BSN, called BSN3, that will improve the inequality of  \thref{thm:firstineq} for BSN2 and will allow us to prove the rest of the inequalities of  \thref{thm:ineqKuttlerSigillito}.

\begin{thm}\label{thm:TheoremeGeneralBSNF3} The following boundary problem
\begin{equation}\label{eq:BSNF3}
(BSN3)\begin{cases}
\Delta^2 \w = 0 & \text{on } M \\
\nu \lrcorner \w = 0 & \text{on } \partial M \\
\nu \lrcorner d \w = 0 & \text{on } \partial M \\
\nu \lrcorner \Delta \w = 0 & \text{on } \partial M \\
\nu \lrcorner \Delta d\w +  \textit{l} \iota^* \w = 0 & \text{on } \partial M,
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
for differential p-forms, has a discrete spectrum consisting of an unbounded non-decreasing sequence of positive real eigenvalues with finite multiplicities $( \textit{l}_i)_{i \geq 1}$ and possibly $\textit{l}_0=0$. Moreover, its kernel is $H_A^p(M).$

\end{thm}
The restriction to dimension $n\geq 2$ is needed, as shown by the following lemma.
\begin{lemma}
  The spectrum of BSN3 on functions $f:[0,1]\longrightarrow \mathbb{R}$ on the interval $M=[0,1]$ is reduced to $\{0,24\}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
We get the spectrum by simply solving the system \begin{equation*}
\begin{cases}
f^{(4)} = 0 & \text{on } M \\
f'(0) = 0 & \text{on } \partial M \\
f'(1)= 0 & \text{on } \partial M \\
-f^{(3)}(0)+\textit{l} f(0)= 0 & \text{on } \partial M \\
f^{(3)}(1)+\textit{l} f(1) = 0 & \text{on } \partial M.
\end{cases}
\end{equation*}
\end{proof}
The spectrum of BSD on function \eqref{eq:BSDfct} on an interval $[-1,1]$ is also reduced to $\{1,3\}$, see \cite[Remark 1.4]{FerreroGazzolaWeth}.


The following Lemma will be shown in \lemref{lem:l<l<l} later on.
\begin{lemma}
Using the notations defined previously, for all $p\in \{0,\cdots,n\}$ and all $k\geq 0$, we have the following inequalities:
 \[
    \ell_{k,p} \leq \textit{l}_{k,p} \leq \mathbf{l}_{k,p}.
    \]
\end{lemma}

Throughout this paper, we fix the degree $p\in \{0,\cdots,n\}$. To simplify the notation, for all $k\geq 1$ we will write $\ell_k, \mathbf{l}_k$ and $\textit{l}_k$ or simply $\ell,\mathbf{l}$ and $\textit{l}$ instead of $\ell_{k,p}, \mathbf{l}_{k,p}$ and $\textit{l}_{k,p}$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:p=n}
    For $p=0$, the three problems BSN1, BSN2 and BSN3 reduce to the classical BSN problem for scalar functions \eqref{eq:BSNfonction}.\\
    For $p=n$, the BSN1 reduce to the scalar BSD \eqref{eq:BSDfct}, the BSN2 and BSN3 are trivial. 
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
It is immediate that the three BSN problems for $p=0$ reduce to the BSN problem for functions. 
\\ For $p=n$ we have $\w=f \vol g$ with $f$ a real function. Then in the BSN1 , BSN2 and BSN3 the second and last boundary conditions are trivial. The remaining conditions in the BSN1 give us the scalar BSD conditions. In BSN2 and BSN3, we have $\Delta f=0$ on $M$ and $f_{|\partial M}=0$, using an integration by parts, we get that $f=0$ and so $\w=0$. Hence these problems are trivial. 
\end{proof}
That is why for all the results related to BSN2 and BSN3 we consider $p\in\{0,\cdots,n-1\}.$ 

\subsection{Kuttler-Sigillito inequalities for differential forms}\label{sec:IntroKuttlerSigillito}
Now that we have the BSN problems, we can establish the Kuttler-Sigillito inequalities. We will cite them here and we will prove them later on. Let us write respectively $\lambda_k,\ \mu_k$ and $\sigma_k$ the $k^{th}$ positive eigenvalue of the Dirichlet, Neumann and Steklov problems on differential forms (see \secref{sec:DNSformes}).  For BSN2 and BSN3, we have the following results on differential forms:

\begin{thm}\label{thm:3èmePBkuttlersigilitto1}
For $k\geq 1$, we have the following inequalities between the eigenvalues of the Steklov, Dirichlet, Neumann, BSD, BSN2 and BSN3 problems:
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:3èmePBmuksigma1<lk}
    \mu_k \sigma_1 \leq \textit{l}_k\leq \mathbf{l}_k,
    \end{equation}
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:3èmePBmu1sigmak<lk}
    \mu_1 \sigma_k \leq \textit{l}_k\leq \mathbf{l}_k.
    \end{equation}
    The inequality is strict for $k=1,$ i.e., $ \mu_1 \sigma_1 < \textit{l}_1.$
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:q1sigma1<l1}
    q_1 \sigma_1^2 <\textit{l}_1\leq \mathbf{l}_1.
    \end{equation}
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:mu1<lamba1+(q1l1)}
    \mu_1^{-1} < \lambda_1^{-1} + (q_1 \textit{l}_1)^{-\frac{1}{2}}.
    \end{equation} 
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:mu1<lamba1+(q1sigma1)}
    \mu_1^{-1} < \lambda_1^{-1} + (q_1 \sigma_1)^{-1}.
    \end{equation}
\end{thm}

\begin{remark}
    Inequalities \eqref{eq:q1sigma1<l1}, \eqref{eq:mu1<lamba1+(q1l1)} and \eqref{eq:mu1<lamba1+(q1sigma1)} are new on manifolds, even for $p=0,$ i.e., for scalar BSD and BSN problems.
\end{remark}
\begin{remark}
As mentioned earlier, the inequalities of  \thref{thm:3èmePBkuttlersigilitto1} are a priori not valid for BSN1. Moreover, inequalities \eqref{eq:mu1<lamba1+(q1l1)} and \eqref{eq:mu1<lamba1+(q1sigma1)} may not hold either for BSN2. Later, we will provide some insights by presenting parts of the proof to illustrate why they may not hold.

\end{remark}
\begin{remark}
    Kuttler and Sigillito \cite{Kuttler&Sigillito} established other spectral inequalities that involve geometric controls of the boundary. These were partially extended to scalar functions on manifolds in \cite{hassannezhadETsiffert}. We will study such inequalities in a forthcoming paper.
\end{remark}

\textbf{Outline of the Article:}
In Section 2 of this article, we provide a review of concepts related to differential forms, elliptic operators, and general theorem for the existence of solutions of boundary problems. We also recall spectral problems involving differential forms, particularly the Dirichlet, Neumann, Steklov, and biharmonic Steklov problems, while presenting their variational characterizations of eigenvalues.
In Sections 3, 4, and 5, we respectively study the spectra BSN1, BSN2 and BSN3 for differential forms. Finally, in Section 6, we establish generalizations of the Kuttler–Sigillito inequalities.


